-- 3. INSERT THE 242 NEW / UPGRADE-REPLACEMENT ROWS
-- ============================================================
-- Deterministic ids (UUID v5), ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING for retry-safety.
insert into public.dinner_verses
  (id, verse_ref, category, verse_text, context_text,
   question_level_1, question_level_2, question_level_3,
   prayer_level_1, active)
values
  ('8086a0b1-ff48-50fd-9da2-98bd38f50bff', 'Ecclesiastes 4:9-10', 'Community', 'Two are better than one, because they have a good reward for their labor. For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow; but woe to him who is alone when he falls, and doesn''t have another to lift him up.', 'This is part of a longer reflection on what actually makes life worth living. Right in the middle of it, the writer stops to make a simple, practical point: doing life with someone is better than doing it alone. It isn''t a poem about romance — it''s about anyone who actually has your back.',
   'Who''s the one person who''d genuinely show up if you called them at 2am?', 'Is there someone in your life right now who''s trying to do something hard alone — who you know needs to be pulled in — and you haven''t done anything about it yet?', 'The passage doesn''t say two are better than one because life is dangerous — it says so because "if they fall." Falling is treated as a given, not a maybe. What does it change to plan for the fall in advance, instead of assuming you''ll be the exception who doesn''t need anyone?',
   'God, thank you for the people who''ve lifted us up when we''ve fallen — we don''t say that out loud enough. Show us tonight who around us is trying to stand alone, and give us the nerve to reach out first. Amen.', true),
  ('d58cf53e-89a3-5c5f-90ca-252700f85c87', 'Mark 9:21-24', 'Faith', 'He asked his father, "How long has it been since this has come to him?" He said, "From childhood. Often it has cast him both into the fire and into the water, to destroy him. But if you can do anything, have compassion on us, and help us." Jesus said to him, "If you can believe, all things are possible to him who believes." Immediately the father of the child cried out with tears, "I believe. Help my unbelief!"', 'This father has watched his son suffer through violent seizures his entire childhood. He''s exhausted, and by the time he reaches Jesus, so is his faith. What he says next is one of the most honest sentences in the Gospels.',
   'Have you ever wanted to believe something was going to work out, but part of you just didn''t? What did that feel like?', 'Where in your life right now are you saying "I believe" out loud while a quieter voice inside you is saying "help me actually believe it"?', 'Watch where the word "if" moves in this exchange — the father starts with "if you can do anything," and by his own last sentence it''s become "help my unbelief," as if the uncertainty has shifted from Jesus''s ability to his own. Whether or not that''s exactly what''s happening in the father''s mind, Jesus heals the boy anyway, doubt and all — what does that tell you about what Jesus actually requires before he acts?',
   'Jesus, we believe — and we need help with the part of us that doesn''t. We''re not going to pretend to be more certain than we are tonight. Meet us exactly where our faith actually is, not where we wish it was. Amen.', true),
  ('5b88ff62-52d4-5af6-965f-c280b1723b67', 'Psalm 34:17-18', 'Hope', 'The righteous cry, and Yahweh hears, And delivers them out of all their troubles. Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, And saves those who have a crushed spirit.', 'David wrote this while running for his life, having just faked insanity to escape a king who wanted him dead. This isn''t written from safety looking back — it''s written from inside the wreckage.',
   'When you''re going through something hard, do you tend to pull people in close, or push everyone away?', 'When someone you love is hurting, do you tend to move toward them, try to fix them, or become uncomfortable and pull away? Why?', 'This verse doesn''t say God is near to the strong or the composed — it says he''s near specifically to the broken. What does it reveal about who God actually is, that brokenness draws him close instead of pushing him away?',
   'God, you say you''re near to the brokenhearted, so let''s not perform having it together tonight if we don''t. Whatever''s crushed in this room right now, come close to it, exactly like you promised. Amen.', true),
  ('3949ef33-ab88-56b1-b57f-266ada386c5e', 'Luke 15:17-24', 'Grace', 'But when he came to himself he said, ''How many hired servants of my father''s have bread enough to spare, and I''m dying with hunger! I will get up and go to my father, and will tell him, "Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in your sight. I am no more worthy to be called your son. Make me as one of your hired servants."'' "He arose, and came to his father. But while he was still far off, his father saw him, and was moved with compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him. The son said to him, ''Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in your sight. I am no longer worthy to be called your son.'' "But the father said to his servants, ''Bring out the best robe, and put it on him. Put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet. Bring the fattened calf, kill it, and let us eat, and celebrate; for this, my son, was dead, and is alive again. He was lost, and is found.'' They began to celebrate.', 'The son has already rehearsed his apology — he''s planning to ask to be a servant, not a son. He never gets to finish the speech. His father is already running toward him before he can offer to demote himself.',
   'Have you ever had to go back and admit you were wrong — to a parent, a friend, anyone? What made that hard?', 'Is there a version of "coming home" you''ve been putting off — nothing has to be dramatic, just something you know you need to make right?', 'The father sees him "while he was still far off" — before the son has said a word, before the apology is even finished. He''s already running. If God doesn''t wait for us to clean ourselves up before he moves toward us, what does that change about how you''d approach him the next time you''ve genuinely messed up?',
   'Father, thank you that you don''t wait for us to earn our way back before you run toward us. If there''s something tonight we''ve been rehearsing an apology for, help us actually go — and help us believe you''ll meet us halfway. Amen.', true),
  ('ab806580-5d0f-5637-86bd-88d7a393022d', 'Matthew 6:31-33', 'Purpose', '"Therefore don''t be anxious, saying, ''What will we eat?'', ''What will we drink?'' or, ''With what will we be clothed?'' For the Gentiles seek after all these things, for your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things. But seek first God''s Kingdom, and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well.', 'Jesus says this to people worried about real, physical survival — food and clothing, not abstract anxiety. He isn''t dismissing the worry. He''s redirecting where their energy goes first.',
   'What''s the one thing on your mind most days that you find yourself worrying about before almost anything else?', 'If you''re honest, what do you actually chase first on a normal day, before you''ve even thought about God at all?', 'Jesus doesn''t say the things we worry about don''t matter — food, clothing, and provision are all named as real needs God already knows about. What''s the actual promise here, and what would change about your daily decisions if you believed God would genuinely handle what''s "added" once you got the order right?',
   'God, you already know everything we''re carrying tonight — what we need, what we''re worried about, all of it. We invite you into first place, not because we''ve earned the order right, but because you asked us to, and you promised the rest would follow. Amen.', true),
  ('ef142b57-653a-5628-98a2-f3b498533a4a', 'Luke 12:13-15', 'Money', 'One of the multitude said to him, "Teacher, tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me." But he said to him, "Man, who made me a judge or an arbitrator over you?" He said to them, "Beware! Keep yourselves from covetousness, for a man''s life doesn''t consist of the abundance of the things which he possesses."', 'Jesus refuses to take a side in this inheritance dispute, and instead names the deeper issue behind the request itself — not who''s legally right, but what''s actually driving the demand.',
   'Have you ever been in a disagreement about something material where the real issue was actually something else entirely?', 'Is there a possession, or a claim to one, that you''ve held onto more tightly than it probably deserves?', 'Jesus doesn''t settle the argument over who deserves what — he questions the assumption underneath it, that having more would actually fix anything. What''s a fair claim you might be right to make, that still isn''t worth fighting for?',
   'Jesus, help us notice when we''re fighting for something material because of a need it can''t actually meet. Amen.', true),
  ('3e2ea5dc-8e68-5996-9102-ba702b60a2d1', '2 Corinthians 5:1, 6-8', 'Faith', 'For we know that if the earthly house of our tent is dissolved, we have a building from God, a house not made with hands, eternal, in the heavens. Being therefore always of good courage, and knowing that, while we are at home in the body, we are absent from the Lord; for we walk by faith, not by sight. We are of good courage, I say, and are willing rather to be absent from the body, and to be at home with the Lord.', 'Paul uses a specific image — a temporary tent versus a permanent building — to talk about the body and what comes after it, concrete language for a subject families often don''t have vocabulary for.',
   'What''s a temporary place you''ve lived — a dorm, a rental, a tent on a trip — versus somewhere that actually felt permanent?', 'Does thinking about your body as a "tent," something temporary, change how you think about the life you''re living in it right now?', 'Paul says he''s "of good courage" about this, not afraid of it. What would it take for that same courage to feel true for you, not just theologically correct?',
   'Father, give us real courage about what''s temporary and what''s eternal, not just an answer we''ve memorized. Amen.', true),
  ('17dd50fe-a47b-5708-9a72-3d01e3683031', 'Matthew 5:43-45', 'Love', '"You have heard that it was said, ''You shall love your neighbor, and hate your enemy.'' But I tell you, love your enemies, bless those who curse you, do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who mistreat you and persecute you, that you may be children of your Father who is in heaven. For he makes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.', 'Jesus is correcting a common assumption of his day — that loving your neighbor also gave you permission to hate your enemy. He doesn''t just forbid the hate. He commands the exact opposite of it.',
   'Who''s someone — doesn''t have to be dramatic — that''s genuinely hard for you to be kind to right now?', 'What''s actually stopping you from praying for that specific person, by name, sometime this week?', 'Jesus grounds this command in something surprising — not "because it''s the right thing to do," but because it''s literally how God acts, sending sun and rain on everyone regardless of what they deserve. If loving your enemy is what makes you look like your Father, what does it say about a faith that only extends love to people who''ve earned it?',
   'God, you send sun and rain on people who never say thank you, and you''re asking us to love like that too. We name the person we''re struggling with tonight, even just in our own heads, and ask you to help us want good for them, not just tolerate them. Amen.', true),
  ('fef73774-acd7-5100-80c5-390c8a62ff13', 'James 1:19-20', 'Anger & Conflict', 'So, then, my beloved brothers, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger; for the anger of man doesn''t produce the righteousness of God.', 'James is writing to a scattered, struggling church, and before he tackles almost anything else, he starts here — with how they actually talk to each other.',
   'Are you usually the person in an argument who''s already forming your response while the other person is still talking?', 'Think of the last time you got genuinely angry — did it get you closer to what you actually wanted, or further away?', 'James puts these in a specific order — quick to listen, slow to speak, slow to anger — not the order most arguments actually run in. What do you think changes in a conversation the moment your goal shifts from winning it to actually understanding the other person?',
   'God, we''re quicker to speak than to listen more often than we''d like to admit. Slow us down tonight, in this conversation and in the ones we''re avoiding, and show us where our anger''s been doing more talking than you ever asked it to. Amen.', true),
  ('8f23ae40-50b6-5020-88ce-2ad3c0730f6a', 'Matthew 6:19-21', 'Money', '"Don''t lay up treasures for yourselves on the earth, where moth and rust consume, and where thieves break through and steal; but lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume, and where thieves don''t break through and steal; for where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.', 'Jesus isn''t saying money itself is evil. He''s making an observation about direction — whatever you actually invest in ends up owning your attention, whether you meant it to or not.',
   'If someone looked at your bank statement and your calendar from the last month, what would they guess you care about most?', 'Is there something you''re pouring real time or money into that, if you''re honest, isn''t going to matter in twenty years?', 'Jesus says the heart follows the treasure — not the other way around. Most of us assume we should feel more spiritual first, and our spending would follow. What would it look like to actually reverse that — to move the treasure first and trust your heart to catch up?',
   'God, show us tonight where our treasure''s actually been going, even the parts we haven''t wanted to look at closely. We want our hearts to follow you — help us move what we treasure first, and trust you with the rest. Amen.', true),
  ('797db84f-cc89-5627-9a70-8960ba3abcb1', 'John 13:12-17', 'Leadership & Service', 'So when he had washed their feet, put his outer garment back on, and sat down again, he said to them, "Do you know what I have done to you? You call me, ''Teacher'' and ''Lord.'' You say so correctly, for so I am. If I then, the Lord and the Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another''s feet. For I have given you an example, that you also should do as I have done to you. Most assuredly I tell you, a servant is not greater than his lord, neither one who is sent greater than he who sent him. If you know these things, blessed are you if you do them."', 'Washing feet was considered a job for the lowest household servant, in a world of dusty roads and open sandals. Jesus, at his own last meal with his closest friends, ties on a towel and does it himself.',
   'What''s a job at home or at work that everyone quietly avoids because it feels beneath them?', 'Is there someone in your life you''d struggle to serve in a small, unglamorous way — because part of you thinks you''re above it?', 'Jesus doesn''t say "blessed are you for knowing this." He says blessed are you if you actually do it. Why do you think he draws such a hard line between understanding this and actually living it out?',
   'Jesus, you knelt down and did the lowest job in the room without being asked. Show us this week what that looks like for us — not admiring it from a distance, but actually picking up a towel for someone. Amen.', true),
  ('2d26aeda-e1c5-529d-9758-0c99620e1a61', 'Philippians 2:3-5', 'Ambition & Humility', 'doing nothing through rivalry or through conceit, but in humility, each counting others better than himself; each of you not just looking to his own things, but each of you also to the things of others. Have this in your mind, which was also in Christ Jesus,', 'Paul writes this to a church he genuinely loves, and instead of opening with a theological argument, he opens with a plea about how they actually treat each other — looking to each other''s interests, not just their own.',
   'Think of someone you saw today — what''s one thing they might have needed that you were too focused on yourself to really notice?', 'When you walk into a room, are you usually thinking about what you need from it, or what someone else in it might need?', 'Paul says to count others "better than yourself" — not equal, better. That''s not how most of us are trained to think about our own worth. What''s the difference between healthy self-respect and the kind of humility Paul''s actually describing?',
   'God, help us actually look around the room tonight instead of just through it — to notice what other people need, not just what we''re carrying. Grow in us the same mind that was in Jesus, who had every reason to think of himself first and never did. Amen.', true),
  ('69d16a23-5d30-54f8-8adf-7d9361c8de9e', 'John 8:3-11', 'Love', 'The scribes and the Pharisees brought a woman taken in adultery. Having set her in the midst, they told him, "Teacher, we found this woman in adultery, in the very act. Now in our law, Moses commanded us to stone such. What then do you say about her?" They said this testing him, that they might have something to accuse him of. But Jesus stooped down, and wrote on the ground with his finger. But when they continued asking him, he looked up and said to them, "He who is without sin among you, let him throw the first stone at her." Again he stooped down, and with his finger wrote on the ground. They, when they heard it, being convicted by their conscience, went out one by one, beginning from the oldest, even to the last. Jesus was left alone with the woman where she was, in the middle. Jesus, standing up, saw her and said, "Woman, where are your accusers? Did no one condemn you?" She said, "No one, Lord." Jesus said, "Neither do I condemn you. Go your way. From now on, sin no more."', 'This woman is a trap — a setup to catch Jesus, not an act of real justice. She''s been dragged into public with genuine guilt, and real people are standing there ready to legally kill her, right now.',
   'Has someone ever caught you at your absolute worst moment? What did they do with that?', 'Is there someone you''ve quietly written off because of something they did, and you''ve never really reconsidered it since?', 'Jesus never says what she did was okay — he still tells her to "sin no more." But he also refuses to pick up a stone. What''s the difference between the truth Jesus tells her and the judgment the crowd was ready to deliver — and which one actually changes a person?',
   'Jesus, thank you that you tell us the truth without ever picking up a stone. We''re carrying rocks tonight for people we''ve already decided aren''t worth a second look — help us put them down, and give us the same kind of honesty that leads somewhere good instead of just condemning. Amen.', true),
  ('51a60909-c953-58d8-8ad0-d421a59489bc', 'Genesis 2:18-24', 'Marriage', 'Yahweh God said, "It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him a helper suitable for him." Out of the ground Yahweh God formed every animal of the field, and every bird of the sky, and brought them to the man to see what he would call them. Whatever the man called every living creature, that was its name. The man gave names to all cattle, and to the birds of the sky, and to every animal of the field; but for man there was not found a helper suitable for him. Yahweh God caused a deep sleep to fall on the man, and he slept; and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh in its place. He made the rib, which Yahweh God had taken from the man, into a woman, and brought her to the man. The man said, "This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh. She will be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man." Therefore a man will leave his father and his mother, and will join with his wife, and they will be one flesh.', 'This happens before anything has gone wrong in the world — before sin, before shame, before any of it. In an otherwise "very good" creation, God himself is the one who says something isn''t good yet: the man is alone. The woman is made as a partner who corresponds to him, and the passage ends with the two joined as one.',
   'What''s something small your spouse — or, if you''re not married, someone in a marriage you admire — does that makes you feel truly known?', 'Where in your closest relationship have you been more focused on being understood than on actually understanding the other person?', 'Before God makes the woman, he has Adam name every animal first — and none of them turn out to be "suitable for him." Why do you think God let Adam feel that lack before giving him the answer, instead of just skipping ahead to it?',
   'God, being fully known by another person instead of left alone is a real gift, and we don''t want to take it for granted. Where our closest relationships have grown distant or distracted, draw us back toward "bone of my bones" — toward actually being one, not just living side by side. Amen.', true),
  ('08cc0c12-6fc5-59b0-8c82-d8cffdb880cd', 'Psalm 127:3-5', 'Parenting', 'Behold, children are a heritage of Yahweh. The fruit of the womb is his reward. As arrows in the hand of a mighty man, So are the children of youth. Happy is the man who has his quiver full of them. They won''t be disappointed when they speak with their enemies in the gate.', 'This psalm is about building — houses, cities, families — and right in the middle of it, the psalm calls children not a cost but a reward, arrows a parent aims deliberately toward the future.',
   'What''s something about being part of this family — as a kid, or as a parent — that you''re actually grateful for, even on the hard days?', 'Where has exhaustion or stress made it hard to see your kids — or yourself as a kid — the way this psalm describes: as a gift, not a weight?', 'An arrow only goes where it was aimed before it left the hand — once it''s flying, the direction is already decided. What''s the difference between raising kids by default, whatever happens happens, and actually aiming them somewhere on purpose while they''re still close?',
   'God, thank you for the children at this table — the little ones, and the ones who used to be. On the days it feels like more weight than gift, remind us what you actually called this. Help us aim well, not just survive the week. Amen.', true),
  ('dd3db9a7-2856-508b-b945-d87153be6589', 'John 8:34-36', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Jesus answered them, "Most assuredly I tell you, everyone who commits sin is the bondservant of sin. A bondservant doesn''t live in the house forever. A son remains forever. If therefore the Son makes you free, you will be free indeed.', 'Jesus is talking to people proud of their freedom — they insist they''ve never technically been slaves to anyone. He isn''t talking about that kind of slavery. He''s naming something everyone in the room, and everyone at every table since, actually recognizes.',
   'What''s something — it doesn''t have to be dramatic — that you know isn''t good for you, but you keep going back to anyway?', 'Is there a pattern in your life right now — big or small, no need to name it out loud — that you''ve tried to muscle through alone instead of actually letting anyone help you with?', 'Jesus draws a sharp line — a slave doesn''t stay in the family forever, but a son does, permanently. Being set free doesn''t mean the pull disappears overnight, and people walking in real freedom often still do hard, ongoing work to stay there — support, honesty, accountability. What''s the difference between white-knuckling a habit alone and being free in a way that''s still allowed to include a real fight?',
   'Jesus, we bring you what has a grip on us that shouldn''t — even the parts we''re not ready to say out loud tonight. You call that freedom even while the fight for it is still real. Thank you that we don''t have to win it alone first before it counts as grace. Amen.', true),
  ('888e3065-2f3c-5827-bfc9-5890b3bd4992', 'Luke 12:16-21', 'Money', 'He spoke a parable to them, saying, "The ground of a certain rich man brought forth abundantly. He reasoned within himself, saying, ''What will I do, because I don''t have room to store my crops?'' He said, ''This is what I will do. I will pull down my barns, and build bigger ones, and there I will store all my grain and my goods. I will tell my soul, "Soul, you have many goods laid up for many years. Take your ease, eat, drink, be merry."'' "But God said to him, ''You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you. The things which you have prepared--whose will they be?'' So is he who lays up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God."', 'Jesus tells this story right after someone in the crowd asks him to settle a family inheritance dispute. He doesn''t take a side on the money — he goes straight for the deeper question underneath it.',
   'If you had one more truly great financial year, what''s the first thing you''d actually do with it?', 'Be honest — how much of your planning for the future is really about security for your family, and how much is just about "more"?', 'The man in the story never does anything illegal, or even unkind — he''s just successful and makes reasonable plans. His fault, in God''s own words, is being rich toward himself instead of rich toward God. What''s the actual difference between those two, and which one describes your own plans right now?',
   'God, we don''t want to build bigger barns and call it wisdom. Show us tonight what it actually looks like to be rich toward you, not just toward ourselves, and give us the courage to hold what we have a little more loosely. Amen.', true),
  ('9e6edabd-55de-559e-9f8b-85436cbc0808', '1 Corinthians 15:3-7, 20-22', 'Hope', 'For I delivered to you first of all that which I also received: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve. Then he appeared to over five hundred brothers at once, most of whom remain until now, but some have also fallen asleep. Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles... But now Christ has been raised from the dead. He became the first fruits of those who are asleep. For since death came by man, the resurrection of the dead also came by man. For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all will be made alive.', 'Paul lays this out plainly, almost like a summary he wants remembered exactly: Christ died, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared to specific people afterward. It''s the center of what he says the whole Christian message actually is.',
   'When you think about someone you''ve lost, what''s the memory of them that comes to mind first?', 'Does the idea of your own death feel more like something to fear, or something you haven''t really let yourself think about at all?', 'Paul calls Jesus "the firstfruits" — the first of a full harvest still coming, not a one-time miracle. What does it change about how you think of death if Jesus''s resurrection is a preview of something, not just an isolated exception?',
   'God, death is real and it hurts, and we''re not pretending otherwise tonight. But we hold onto this — that Jesus was raised first, not last, and that changes what we''re actually waiting for. Be near to whoever at this table is grieving someone right now. Amen.', true),
  ('a7c50290-db9f-50b0-be35-d848f5022b38', 'Matthew 7:1-5', 'Wisdom', '"Don''t judge, so that you won''t be judged. For with whatever judgment you judge, you will be judged; and with whatever measure you measure, it will be measured to you. Why do you see the speck that is in your brother''s eye, but don''t consider the beam that is in your own eye? Or how will you tell your brother, ''Let me remove the speck from your eye;'' and behold, the beam is in your own eye? You hypocrite! First remove the beam out of your own eye, and then you can see clearly to remove the speck out of your brother''s eye.', 'Jesus uses a deliberately ridiculous image — an entire plank of wood stuck in someone''s eye — to make a point that would''ve gotten a laugh from his original audience before it convicted them.',
   'What''s something small that really annoys you about someone else''s habits — and does anyone at this table have that same habit, just in a different form?', 'Who''s someone you''ve judged pretty quickly lately, and have you actually considered what''s going on in your own life that you''d rather not have examined that closely?', 'Jesus doesn''t say never point out what''s wrong in someone else — he says get the beam out of your own eye first, so you can see clearly, which implies you''ll still help with the speck after. Once you''ve actually dealt with your own beam, does the same correction feel different to give — and different for the other person to receive?',
   'God, it''s so much easier to see everyone else''s speck than our own beam. There''s something specific we''ve been avoiding looking at in ourselves — bring it to mind right now, and give us the humility to deal with it before we say another word about anyone else. Amen.', true),
  ('e0c02b4d-4c5c-5d52-a187-3030de6f097f', 'Proverbs 15:1', 'Anger & Conflict', 'A gentle answer turns away wrath, But a harsh word stirs up anger.', 'This is one of the shortest, most quotable proverbs in the whole book — a single, sharp observation about how conversations actually go, tested a thousand times in every kitchen and living room.',
   'Think of the last small argument you had — did it start because of what was said, or because of how it was said?', 'Is there someone in your life you tend to respond to sharply, almost automatically, before you even think about it?', 'The proverb says a harsh word "stirs up" anger — like it''s feeding something that was already there. What is it about a gentle answer that seems to take the fuel away instead, and why is that usually the harder thing to choose in the moment you''re actually angry?',
   'God, help us notice, tonight and this week, the half-second before we respond sharply to someone, and give us a gentle answer instead, especially with the people we love most and forgive least easily. Amen.', true),
  ('0e87526b-80fd-5945-ace3-dcf6ab1a12ec', 'Mark 10:13-16', 'Parenting', 'They were bringing to him little children, that he should touch them, but the disciples rebuked those who were bringing them. But when Jesus saw it, he was moved with indignation, and said to them, "Allow the little children to come to me! Don''t forbid them, for the Kingdom of God belongs to such as these. Most assuredly I tell you, whoever will not receive the Kingdom of God like a little child, he will in no way enter into it." He took them in his arms, and blessed them, laying his hands on them.', 'The disciples think they''re protecting Jesus''s time by turning families away. Jesus becomes genuinely indignant about it, and does the exact opposite of what his own followers wanted.',
   'What''s something a child has said or done recently — yours, a niece or nephew, anyone — that stuck with you?', 'When was the last time you approached God the way a little kid approaches someone they trust completely, without pretending, without an agenda?', 'Jesus gets genuinely indignant that his own disciples are the ones keeping children away from him. What does it say about how easily even well-meaning faith can get in the way of the exact people Jesus wants closest to him?',
   'Jesus, you got angry on behalf of the children the world tried to push aside, and you still do. Help this family raise kids who feel like the Kingdom actually belongs to them, and help the adults at this table remember what it felt like to trust you that simply. Amen.', true),
  ('e9592983-bb32-5e09-b78d-993aff643f72', 'Luke 5:27-32', 'Love', 'After these things he went out, and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the tax office, and said to him, "Follow me!" He left everything, and rose up and followed him. Levi made a great feast for him in his house. There was a great crowd of tax collectors and others who were reclining with them. Their scribes and the Pharisees murmured against his disciples, saying, "Why do you eat and drink with the tax collectors and sinners?" Jesus answered them, "Those who are healthy have no need for a physician, but those who are sick do. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance."', 'Tax collectors in Jesus''s day were considered traitors — Jewish men collecting money for Rome, often skimming extra for themselves. Levi is exactly the kind of person religious people avoided, and Jesus doesn''t just tolerate him — he goes to his house and eats at his table.',
   'Is there a type of person — not one specific individual, just a "type" — you''d be a little uncomfortable seeing show up at this table?', 'Have you ever been the outsider in a room, the one everyone assumed had it together less than they did? What was that like?', 'The religious leaders'' complaint isn''t really about Levi — it''s about who Jesus is willing to be seen with. If the company Jesus kept tells you something about his actual character, what does it say, and does the company you keep say the same thing about yours?',
   'Jesus, you went looking for the person everyone else had already written off, and you sat down at his table instead of waiting for him to clean up first. There''s someone like that in our own lives this week — bring their face to mind, and give us the nerve to actually pull up a chair. Amen.', true),
  ('a3aee404-4005-5821-ab9b-2a5043c2711a', 'Luke 19:1-10', 'Grace', 'He entered and was passing through Jericho. There was a man named Zacchaeus. He was a chief tax collector, and he was rich. He was trying to see who Jesus was, and couldn''t because of the crowd, because he was short. He ran on ahead, and climbed up into a sycamore tree to see him, for he was to pass that way. When Jesus came to the place, he looked up and saw him, and said to him, "Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for today I must stay at your house." He hurried, came down, and received him joyfully. When they saw it, they all murmured, saying, "He has gone in to lodge with a man who is a sinner." Zacchaeus stood and said to the Lord, "Behold, Lord, half of my goods I give to the poor. If I have wrongfully exacted anything of anyone, I restore four times as much." Jesus said to him, "Today, salvation has come to this house, because he also is a son of Abraham. For the Son of Man came to seek and to save that which was lost."', 'Zacchaeus wasn''t just wealthy — as a chief tax collector, he''d built that wealth working for Rome and taking more than he was owed from his own people. Nobody in that crowd wanted him closer. Jesus invites himself to dinner at his house anyway.',
   'Have you ever really wanted to see or meet someone, and had to do something a little embarrassing to make it happen?', 'Is there someone you''ve assumed wouldn''t really want to change, so you stopped expecting anything different from them?', 'Jesus invites himself over before Zacchaeus has said a single word of apology or promised anything. The generosity comes after, not before. What does the order of events here tell you about what actually causes a person to change?',
   'Jesus, you went looking for exactly the person the crowd had already given up on. Thank you for not waiting for us to prove ourselves first. Give us the same eyes for someone the people around us have already written off. Amen.', true),
  ('2c440792-8c2d-529c-acdc-14775bb31063', 'Mark 1:40-45', 'Faith', 'There came to him a leper, begging him, kneeling down to him, and saying to him, "If you want to, you can make me clean." Being moved with compassion, he stretched out his hand, and touched him, and said to him, "I want to. Be made clean." When he had said this, immediately the leprosy departed from him, and he was made clean. He strictly warned him, and immediately sent him out, and said to him, "See you say nothing to anybody, but go show yourself to the priest, and offer for your cleansing the things which Moses commanded, for a testimony to them." But he went out, and began to proclaim it much, and to spread about the matter, so that Jesus could no more openly enter into a city, but was outside in desert places: and they came to him from everywhere.', 'Leprosy in that time meant more than sickness — it meant isolation from family, worship, and everyday life. This man isn''t asking Jesus to heal him. He already believes Jesus can. He''s asking if Jesus actually wants to.',
   'When you need help with something, what usually worries you more — whether someone''s able to help, or whether they''d actually want to?', 'When it comes to something you need help with, are you more likely to doubt whether God CAN help, or whether he WANTS to?', 'Jesus doesn''t have to touch him — elsewhere he heals people with just a word, from a distance. He touches him anyway, before the leprosy is gone, while the man is still considered unclean by everyone around him. Notice the order: he touches him, *then* speaks the words that heal him — contact comes first. Why do you think that detail matters, when the touch itself couldn''t have been what actually cured the disease?',
   'Jesus, you reached out and touched what everyone else stayed away from. If there''s a part of us tonight that feels unclean or unwanted, we bring it to you — not asking if you''re able, but trusting that you''re willing. Amen.', true),
  ('ae747444-6c66-50f6-b71a-1473beec9ad9', 'Mark 10:17-22', 'Money', 'As he was going out into the way, one ran to him, knelt before him, and asked him, "Good Teacher, what shall I do that I may inherit eternal life?" Jesus said to him, "Why do you call me good? No one is good except one-- God. You know the commandments: ''Do not murder,'' ''Do not commit adultery,'' ''Do not steal,'' ''Do not give false testimony,'' ''Do not defraud,'' ''Honor your father and mother.''" He said to him, "Teacher, I have observed all these things from my youth." Jesus looking at him loved him, and said to him, "One thing you lack. Go, sell whatever you have, and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me, taking up the cross." But his face fell at that saying, and he went away sorrowful, for he was one who had great possessions.', 'This man ran to Jesus, knelt down, and by every measure of his culture was doing everything right. He genuinely wanted an answer. What Jesus tells him isn''t a trick — it''s the one thing standing between this man and actually following him.',
   'Have you ever really wanted advice about something, and then not liked the answer you got?', 'If Jesus looked at your life the way he looked at this man''s — with real love, not judgment — what do you think he''d name as the "one thing" for you?', 'Mark tells us Jesus loved him before he says the hard thing, not after — this isn''t Jesus rejecting a man who failed a test, it''s Jesus, out of love, naming the actual obstacle. Why do you think this story doesn''t end with the man changing his mind, and what does it say about Jesus that he lets him walk away instead of chasing him down with an easier offer?',
   'God, we don''t want to walk away sad the way he did. Show us, gently, what our own "one thing" might be — and give us the courage to hear it as love, even when it costs us something. Amen.', true),
  ('85a4ee38-ccaa-5f6c-9f7f-eaaf8fdaa3e6', 'Luke 7:36-50', 'Forgiveness', 'One of the Pharisees invited him to eat with him. He entered into the Pharisee''s house, and sat at the table. Behold, a woman in the city who was a sinner, when she knew that he was reclining in the Pharisee''s house, she brought an alabaster jar of ointment. Standing behind at his feet weeping, she began to wet his feet with her tears, and she wiped them with the hair of her head, kissed his feet, and anointed them with the ointment. Now when the Pharisee who had invited him saw it, he said to himself, "This man, if he were a prophet, would have perceived who and what kind of woman this is who touches him, that she is a sinner." Jesus answered him, "Simon, I have something to tell you." He said, "Teacher, say on." "A certain lender had two debtors. The one owed five hundred denarii, and the other fifty. When they couldn''t pay, he forgave them both. Which of them therefore will love him most?" Simon answered, "He, I suppose, to whom he forgave the most." He said to him, "You have judged correctly." Turning to the woman, he said to Simon, "Do you see this woman? I entered into your house, and you gave me no water for my feet, but she has wet my feet with her tears, and wiped them with the hair of her head. You gave me no kiss, but she, since the time I came in, has not ceased to kiss my feet. You didn''t anoint my head with oil, but she has anointed my feet with ointment. Therefore I tell you, her sins, which are many, are forgiven, for she loved much. But to whom little is forgiven, the same loves little." He said to her, "Your sins are forgiven." Those who sat at the table with him began to say to themselves, "Who is this who even forgives sins?" He said to the woman, "Your faith has saved you. Go in peace."', 'Simon invited Jesus to dinner but skipped the basic hospitality a guest was normally given — water for his feet, a greeting kiss, oil for his head. A woman known throughout the city for her sin gives Jesus everything Simon didn''t.',
   'When''s the last time someone else''s generosity made you a little uncomfortable, maybe because you hadn''t done the same?', 'Simon judges the woman by her reputation before she does anything in the story. Is there someone you''ve already decided you know the whole story on?', 'Jesus tells a story where forgiveness produces great love, then looks at this woman and says, "her sins... are forgiven, for she loved much." What do you think Jesus wants Simon to understand about the relationship between being forgiven and loving deeply? Why might the person who thinks they need very little forgiveness have the hardest time understanding her?',
   'Jesus, Simon offered you a seat at the table and nothing else. She offered everything she had. Show us where we''ve been more like Simon — polite, but withholding — and help us love the way someone loves who knows exactly how much they''ve been forgiven. Amen.', true),
  ('7b6d5c6c-f3e3-52af-97cb-7141701ab480', 'Matthew 23:23-28', 'Integrity', '"Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe mint, dill, and cumin, and have left undone the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faith. But you ought to have done these, and not to have left the other undone. You blind guides, who strain out a gnat, and swallow a camel! "Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you clean the outside of the cup and of the platter, but within they are full of extortion and unrighteousness. You blind Pharisee, first clean the inside of the cup and of the platter, that the outside of it may become clean also. "Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitened tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but inwardly are full of dead men''s bones, and of all uncleanness. Even so you also outwardly appear righteous to men, but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and iniquity.', 'The scribes and Pharisees were scrupulous about tithing even their kitchen spices, down to the exact measurement. Jesus isn''t against that precision. He''s naming what they skipped while they were being so precise: justice, mercy, and faith.',
   'What''s something people can look really put-together about on the outside, while something totally different is going on underneath?', 'Where in your own life are you more careful about how something looks than about whether it''s actually right?', 'Jesus doesn''t tell them to stop tithing the mint and dill — he says "you ought to have done these, and not left the other undone." He''s not against small faithfulness; he''s against small faithfulness substituting for the weightier things. What''s the difference between being precise about small things and using small things to avoid the big ones?',
   'God, it''s so much easier to get the visible things right than the invisible ones. Show us tonight where we''ve been polishing the outside of the cup. We don''t want to just look clean to other people — we want to actually be clean where only you can see. Amen.', true),
  ('3b1403f5-6243-5910-b4d6-2da88d9167a1', 'John 20:24-29', 'Faith', 'But Thomas, one of the twelve, called Didymus, wasn''t with them when Jesus came. The other disciples therefore said to him, "We have seen the Lord!" But he said to them, "Unless I see in his hands the print of the nails, and put my hand into his side, I will not believe." After eight days again his disciples were inside, and Thomas was with them. Jesus came, the doors being locked, and stood in the midst, and said, "Peace be to you." Then he said to Thomas, "Reach here your finger, and see my hands. Reach here your hand, and put it into my side. Don''t be unbelieving, but believing." Thomas answered him, "My Lord and my God!" Jesus said to him, "Because you have seen me, you have believed. Blessed are those who have not seen, and have believed."', 'Thomas missed the first time Jesus appeared to the other disciples, and refused to just take their word for it. A full eight days pass — plenty of time for Thomas to sit with his doubt — before Jesus shows up again.',
   'What''s something you refused to believe was true until you saw it with your own eyes?', 'What''s something about faith you''ve mostly taken on other people''s word for, rather than experienced yourself?', 'Jesus doesn''t scold Thomas for doubting, or make him wait longer to prove a point. He shows up offering exactly what Thomas said he needed. What does it say about Jesus that he meets a doubter''s exact conditions instead of just demanding more faith?',
   'Jesus, thank you that you didn''t walk away from Thomas''s doubt — you walked toward it. If there''s someone at this table who needs to see something before they can believe it, meet them there, the way you met him. Amen.', true),
  ('d724e1a8-d83c-5809-8a54-8f67d730026b', 'Matthew 18:1-5', 'Parenting', 'In that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Who then is greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven?" Jesus called a little child to himself, and set him in the midst of them, and said, "Most assuredly I tell you, unless you turn, and become as little children, you will in no way enter into the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever therefore humbles himself as this little child, the same is the greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever receives one such little child in my name receives me,', 'The disciples ask a status question — who''s the greatest. Jesus doesn''t answer with a person''s name. He picks up a child, who in that culture had no status or authority at all, and says: that.',
   'What''s something a little kid does naturally that adults usually have to work hard to relearn?', 'Where in your life have you been quietly competing for status or importance instead of just showing up honestly?', 'The disciples wanted to know who''s greatest. Jesus answers a completely different question — who''s willing to be small. What''s the difference between humility that''s really just low self-esteem, and the kind of childlike humility Jesus is actually pointing at here?',
   'God, we spend a lot of energy trying to matter. Tonight, help us actually become like this child — not less than we are, just less concerned with being seen as more. Amen.', true),
  ('458f8bca-7641-5182-a527-3f7436c6a3c4', 'Luke 7:11-15', 'Grief & Loss', 'It happened soon afterwards, that he went to a city called Nain. Many of his disciples, along with a great multitude, went with him. Now when he drew near to the gate of the city, behold, one who was dead was carried out, the only son of his mother, and she was a widow. Many people of the city were with her. When the Lord saw her, he had compassion on her, and said to her, "Don''t cry." He came near and touched the coffin, and the bearers stood still. He said, "Young man, I tell you, arise!" He who was dead sat up, and began to speak. And he gave him to his mother.', 'This woman had already lost her husband. Now she''s lost her only son — in that culture, her only remaining source of provision and protection. Nobody sends for Jesus here. He simply sees her.',
   'Has someone ever shown up for you at exactly the right moment, without you even asking them to?', 'Is there someone in your life carrying a loss right now who needs you to simply notice them, not fix anything?', 'Jesus speaks to her before he does anything about her son — "don''t cry" comes before the miracle, not after. Then Luke tells us he gave the son back to his mother, not just to the crowd. Why do you think Luke includes that last detail — that Jesus''s compassion here reaches past the miracle itself and back to the person left standing?',
   'Lord, grief doesn''t always ask for help before it needs it. If it''s sitting at this table tonight, however big or small, meet it the way you met her — before we even asked. Amen.', true),
  ('3b0b0e4d-82a9-5997-86c3-cc56c3a53c28', 'Mark 1:35-38', 'Prayer', 'Early in the night, he rose up and went out, and departed into a deserted place, and prayed there. Simon and those who were with him followed after him; and they found him, and told him, "Everyone is looking for you." He said to them, "Let''s go elsewhere into the next towns, that I may preach there also, because for this reason I came forth."', 'The night before, Jesus had healed a large crowd of sick and demon-possessed people in Capernaum — he could have stayed and kept meeting the obvious, urgent need right in front of him. Instead, before daylight, he leaves to be alone and pray.',
   'When do you actually get quiet — no phone, no noise — even for a few minutes?', 'What''s a good, even urgent, demand on your time right now that''s genuinely hard to say no to?', 'Simon''s message basically says "you''re needed here," and it''s true — people really were looking for him. Jesus doesn''t stay to meet the obvious demand; whatever he got from being alone with God is what shaped what he did next instead. If being wanted and needed isn''t, by itself, a good enough reason to stay somewhere, what do you think actually is?',
   'God, we let being needed decide our schedule more than we let you. Teach us what it looks like to get quiet with you before the day gets loud, the way Jesus did — not to escape what''s asked of us, but so we actually know what we''re supposed to be doing. Amen.', true),
  ('004bb6ec-ef54-5bb5-bdd0-4a725de509b6', 'John 3:1-8', 'Faith', 'Now there was a man of the Pharisees named Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews. The same came to him by night, and said to him, "Rabbi, we know that you are a teacher come from God, for no one can do these signs that you do, unless God is with him." Jesus answered him, "Most assuredly, I tell you, unless one is born anew, he can''t see the Kingdom of God." Nicodemus said to him, "How can a man be born when he is old? Can he enter a second time into his mother''s womb, and be born?" Jesus answered, "Most assuredly I tell you, unless one is born of water and spirit, he can''t enter into the Kingdom of God! That which is born of the flesh is flesh. That which is born of the Spirit is spirit. Don''t marvel that I said to you, ''You must be born anew.'' The wind blows where it wants to, and you hear its sound, but don''t know where it comes from and where it is going. So is everyone who is born of the Spirit."', 'Nicodemus was a respected religious leader with a reputation to protect, and he comes to Jesus at night, likely so he wouldn''t be seen. He clearly already believes something real about Jesus. Jesus doesn''t ease him in gently.',
   'Have you ever asked a question you were a little embarrassed to ask out loud?', 'Is there a part of your faith that still genuinely confuses you, the way "born again" confused Nicodemus?', 'Nicodemus comes with a compliment and a careful, respectful question. Jesus responds with something that sounds almost like a riddle — birth, wind, spirit — that never fully resolves by the end of the conversation. Why do you think Jesus doesn''t just give this well-educated man a simple, satisfying answer?',
   'God, we don''t always understand what you''re asking of us, and that''s okay to admit. Like Nicodemus, we come with our questions tonight, even the ones we''d rather ask quietly. Meet us in the not-fully-understanding, the way you met him. Amen.', true),
  ('276a5ddb-77bd-509c-b5d6-0f575244dd11', 'Mark 3:1-5', 'Anger & Conflict', 'He entered again into the synagogue, and there was a man there who had his hand withered. They watched him, whether he would heal him on the Sabbath day, that they might accuse him. He said to the man who had his hand withered, "Stand up." He said to them, "Is it lawful on the Sabbath day to do good, or to do harm? To save a life, or to kill?" But they were silent. When he had looked around at them with anger, being grieved at the hardening of their hearts, he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as healthy as the other.', 'The religious leaders in the room aren''t watching to see if this man gets healed — they''re watching to see if Jesus will break a Sabbath rule so they can accuse him. Jesus asks them a direct question first, and they refuse to answer.',
   'Think of a time someone seemed more upset about a rule being broken than about a person actually being helped — what did that look like?', 'What''s a "rule" — written or unwritten — you''ve sometimes valued more than the person it was supposed to protect?', 'This is one of the places the Gospels directly describe Jesus feeling anger — and it''s paired with grief, not just irritation. He''s angry and heartbroken at the same time, over the same thing: hearts more concerned with being right than with a man''s withered hand. What does it tell you about what actually makes Jesus angry, versus what we usually assume would?',
   'Jesus, show us the places where we''ve cared more about being technically correct than about the person standing right in front of us. We don''t want to grieve your heart the way this silence did. Amen.', true),
  ('1e476f2c-40cf-5b6f-96d5-5e40d42c058e', 'John 21:15-19', 'Discipleship', 'So when they had eaten their breakfast, Jesus said to Simon Peter, "Simon, son of Jonah, do you love me more than these?" He said to him, "Yes, Lord; you know that I have affection for you." He said to him, "Feed my lambs." He said to him again a second time, "Simon, son of Jonah, do you love me?" He said to him, "Yes, Lord; you know that I have affection for you." He said to him, "Tend my sheep." He said to him the third time, "Simon, son of Jonah, do you have affection for me?" Peter was grieved because he asked him the third time, "Do you have affection for me?" He said to him, "Lord, you know everything. You know that I have affection for you." Jesus said to him, "Feed my sheep. Most assuredly I tell you, when you were young, you dressed yourself, and walked where you wanted to. But when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will dress you, and carry you where you don''t want to go." Now he said this, signifying by what kind of death he would glorify God. When he had said this, he said to him, "Follow me."', 'Not long before this, Peter denied even knowing Jesus three times, the same night Jesus was arrested. Now, over breakfast by the lake, Jesus asks him the same question three times — once for every denial.',
   'Has anyone ever given you a genuine second chance after you really let them down?', 'Is there a specific failure you still carry around, even though the person involved has already moved past it?', 'Jesus already knows how Peter feels about him — he doesn''t need to ask three times to find out, and Peter even gets visibly hurt by the third question. What do you think Jesus is actually doing by asking the same question three times, if it isn''t to get new information?',
   'Jesus, you didn''t need Peter to explain himself — you needed him to hear himself say it, three times, until the old failure stopped having the last word. If we''re still carrying something we think disqualifies us, ask us again tonight, as many times as it takes. Amen.', true),
  ('22ced265-eb57-5748-a90d-4c21ab5d2fb6', 'John 4:7-26', 'Grace', 'A woman of Samaria came to draw water. Jesus said to her, "Give me a drink." For his disciples had gone away into the city to buy food. The Samaritan woman therefore said to him, "How is it that you, being a Jew, ask for a drink from me, a Samaritan woman?" (For Jews have no dealings with Samaritans.) Jesus answered her, "If you knew the gift of God, and who it is who says to you, ''Give me a drink,'' you would have asked him, and he would have given you living water." The woman said to him, "Sir, you have nothing to draw with, and the well is deep. From where then have you that living water? Are you greater than our father, Jacob, who gave us the well, and drank of it himself, as did his children, and his cattle?" Jesus answered her, "Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again, but whoever drinks of the water that I will give him will never thirst again; but the water that I will give him will become in him a well of water springing up to eternal life." The woman said to him, "Sir, give me this water, so that I don''t get thirsty, neither come all the way here to draw." Jesus said to her, "Go, call your husband, and come here." The woman answered, "I have no husband." Jesus said to her, "You said well, ''I have no husband,'' for you have had five husbands; and he whom you now have is not your husband. This you have said truly." The woman said to him, "Sir, I perceive that you are a prophet. Our fathers worshiped in this mountain, and you Jews say that in Jerusalem is the place where people ought to worship." Jesus said to her, "Woman, believe me, the hour comes, when neither in this mountain, nor in Jerusalem, will you worship the Father. You worship that which you don''t know. We worship that which we know; for salvation is from the Jews. But the hour comes, and now is, when the true worshippers will worship the Father in spirit and truth, for the Father seeks such to be his worshippers. God is spirit, and those who worship him must worship in spirit and truth." The woman said to him, "I know that Messiah comes," (he who is called Christ). "When he has come, he will declare to us all things." Jesus said to her, "I am he, the one who speaks to you."', 'Jews and Samaritans avoided each other by long custom, and a Jewish teacher speaking to any woman alone in public, let alone a Samaritan one with her particular history, broke more than one social rule at once. Jesus starts the conversation anyway, with a simple, ordinary request.',
   'Have you ever had a real, unexpected conversation with someone you assumed you''d have nothing in common with?', 'What kind of person do you tend to avoid starting a real conversation with?', 'Jesus lays out this woman''s entire complicated history — five husbands, and the man she''s with now isn''t one of them — without a single word of shame or condemnation attached. He simply states it as true, and keeps talking to her. Most of us either avoid an uncomfortable truth about someone or use it against them once we know it. Jesus does neither — have you ever experienced being known that completely by someone who stayed kind about it?',
   'Being fully known is a little frightening, Jesus — and it''s exactly what you''re offering. Search us as completely as you searched her, tell us what''s true without making us feel small for it, and stay at the table with us anyway. Amen.', true),
  ('042d23c7-641e-565d-8967-2fa12b9dfecf', 'John 21:1-14', 'Discipleship', 'After these things, Jesus revealed himself again to the disciples at the sea of Tiberias. He revealed himself this way. Simon Peter, Thomas called Didymus, Nathanael of Cana in Galilee, and the sons of Zebedee, and two others of his disciples were together. Simon Peter said to them, "I''m going fishing." They told him, "We are also coming with you." They immediately went out, and entered into the boat. That night, they caught nothing. But when day had already come, Jesus stood on the beach, yet the disciples didn''t know that it was Jesus. Jesus therefore said to them, "Children, have you anything to eat?" They answered him, "No." He said to them, "Cast the net on the right side of the boat, and you will find some." They cast it therefore, and now they weren''t able to draw it in for the multitude of fish. That disciple therefore whom Jesus loved said to Peter, "It''s the Lord!" So when Simon Peter heard that it was the Lord, he wrapped his coat around him (for he was naked), and threw himself into the sea. But the other disciples came in the little boat (for they were not far from the land, but about two hundred cubits away), dragging the net full of fish. So when they got out on the land, they saw a fire of coals there, and fish laid on it, and bread. Jesus said to them, "Bring some of the fish which you have just caught." Simon Peter went up, and drew the net to land, full of great fish, one hundred fifty-three; and even though there were so many, the net wasn''t torn. Jesus said to them, "Come and eat breakfast." None of the disciples dared inquire of him, "Who are you?" knowing that it was the Lord. Then Jesus came and took the bread, gave it to them, and the fish likewise. This is now the third time that Jesus was revealed to his disciples, after he had risen from the dead.', 'The disciples go back to what they know — fishing — and come up empty all night. When the risen Jesus shows up on the beach at dawn, they don''t recognize him until the miracle repeats itself: a boat that couldn''t catch anything suddenly can''t hold what it caught.',
   'What''s your go-to comfort activity when you don''t know what else to do?', 'Is there an area of your life right now that feels like "fishing all night and catching nothing" — a lot of effort, not much to show for it?', 'The resurrected Lord of the universe cooks breakfast on a beach for a group of exhausted fishermen. He could have announced himself with something more dramatic. Why do you think this is how he chose to show up — with an ordinary meal, not a spectacle?',
   'Jesus, thank you for meeting us in ordinary moments, not just dramatic ones. If we''ve been fishing all night with nothing to show for it, we welcome you onto the shore of it — cook breakfast, and let us recognize you there. Amen.', true),
  ('b158bcac-21da-5a99-9fa7-4f897daa5e6a', 'Luke 9:57-62', 'Discipleship', 'As they went on the way, a certain man said to him, "I want to follow you wherever you go, Lord." Jesus said to him, "The foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head." He said to another, "Follow me!" But he said, "Lord, allow me first to go and bury my father." But Jesus said to him, "Leave the dead to bury their own dead, but you go and announce the Kingdom of God." Another also said, "I want to follow you, Lord, but first allow me to bid farewell to those who are at my house." But Jesus said to him, "No one, having put his hand to the plow, and looking back, is fit for the Kingdom of God."', 'Three different people offer to follow Jesus, each with a reasonable-sounding condition attached. Jesus doesn''t soften his answer to any of them.',
   'What''s something you''ve said "yes, but first..." to, when you probably should have just done it right away?', 'What''s a "but first" you keep attaching to something you know God''s actually asking of you?', 'These aren''t unreasonable requests — burying a father, saying goodbye to family are normal, decent things to want. Jesus''s answers sound almost harsh next to how reasonable the requests are. What does that gap tell you about what following Jesus actually costs, versus what we tend to assume it costs?',
   'Jesus, we bring our "buts" tonight — the reasonable-sounding reasons we''ve delayed following you all the way. We''re not pretending they''re small. We''re asking for the courage to put the plow down and not look back. Amen.', true),
  ('0978aa03-5052-52d8-a1c0-519f13123abf', 'Matthew 26:36-39', 'Surrender', 'Then Jesus came with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to his disciples, "Sit here, while I go there and pray." He took with him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, and began to be sorrowful and severely troubled. Then he said to them, "My soul is exceedingly sorrowful, even to death. Stay here, and watch with me." He went forward a little, fell on his face, and prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass away from me; nevertheless, not what I desire, but what you desire."', 'This is the night before the crucifixion. Jesus brings his three closest friends with him and tells them plainly how he feels — sorrowful, to the point of death — before he goes further to pray alone.',
   'When you''re really struggling with something, do you tend to tell people how bad it actually is, or downplay it?', 'What''s something right now you''d want God to take away from you, the way Jesus asked for the cup to pass?', 'Jesus knows what''s coming and asks his Father, honestly, if there''s another way — he doesn''t pretend to already be at peace with it before he actually gets there. What does it mean for your own prayers that even Jesus asked God to change his circumstances before he surrendered to them?',
   'Father, like Jesus, we''re allowed to ask you to take the hard thing away — we do that honestly tonight. And also like him, we want to end up able to say: not what we want, but what you want. Get us there. Amen.', true),
  ('d695c7b7-b240-5811-96d2-2965476448bf', 'Luke 23:33-34', 'Forgiveness', 'When they came to the place that is called The Skull, they crucified him there with the criminals, one on the right and the other on the left. Jesus said, "Father, forgive them, for they don''t know what they are doing." Dividing his garments among them, they cast lots.', 'This is said while Jesus is actually being crucified — not afterward, once the pain had passed. The people he''s asking God to forgive are, in that exact moment, the ones doing it.',
   'Is it harder to forgive someone while they''re still in the middle of hurting you, or after it''s over?', 'Is there someone you''re waiting for an apology from before you''ll consider forgiving them?', 'Jesus doesn''t say they''re forgiven — he asks the Father to forgive them, and gives a reason: they don''t know what they''re doing. That''s not an excuse that erases what they did. What''s the difference between forgiveness that requires someone to understand their wrong first, and the kind Jesus offers here, in the middle of the wrong, before anyone''s understood anything?',
   'Jesus, you prayed for the people nailing you to a cross while they were still doing it. We don''t have anything close to that to forgive tonight, and it still feels hard sometimes. Give us whatever you had in that moment, for whoever we''re struggling to forgive right now. Amen.', true),
  ('50720a72-c5be-5473-a643-402884fccff3', 'Luke 24:13-32', 'Hope', 'Behold, two of them were going that very day to a village named Emmaus, which was sixty stadia from Jerusalem. They talked with each other about all of these things which had happened. It happened, while they talked and questioned together, that Jesus himself came near, and went with them. But their eyes were kept from recognizing him. He said to them, "What are you talking about as you walk, and are sad?" One of them, named Cleopas, answered him, "Are you the only stranger in Jerusalem who doesn''t know the things which have happened there in these days?" He said to them, "What things?" They said to him, "The things concerning Jesus, the Nazarene, who was a prophet mighty in deed and word before God and all the people; and how the chief priests and our rulers delivered him up to be condemned to death, and crucified him. But we were hoping that it was he who would redeem Israel. Yes, and besides all this, it is now the third day since these things happened. Also, certain women of our company amazed us, having arrived early at the tomb; and when they didn''t find his body, they came saying that they had also seen a vision of angels, who said that he was alive. Some of us went to the tomb, and found it just like the women had said, but they didn''t see him." He said to them, "Foolish men, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken! Didn''t the Christ have to suffer these things and to enter into his glory?" Beginning from Moses and from all the prophets, he explained to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself. They drew near to the village, where they were going, and he acted like he would go further. They urged him, saying, "Stay with us, for it is almost evening, and the day is almost over." He went in to stay with them. It happened, that when he had sat down at the table with them, he took the bread and gave thanks. Breaking it, he gave to them. Their eyes were opened, and they recognized him, and he vanished out of their sight. They said one to another, "Weren''t our hearts burning within us, while he spoke to us along the way, and while he opened the Scriptures to us?"', 'These two disciples are walking away from Jerusalem, grieving, having just watched everything they hoped for collapse. Jesus himself joins them on the road, and they don''t recognize him — not until much later, at a table, over bread.',
   'Has there ever been a moment you only fully understood after it was already over — where you looked back and realized something significant was happening while you were living it?', 'What''s a season of your life that felt distant from God at the time, but looks different to you looking back?', 'Jesus walks with them the whole way, explaining the Scriptures, and they still don''t recognize him — until he breaks the bread. He''d been walking beside them, explaining the very Scriptures that pointed to himself, this entire time. Why do you think recognition comes at the ordinary table moment, and not during the theology lesson that came right before it?',
   'Jesus, we don''t always recognize you while you''re walking with us either. Open our eyes the way you opened theirs — maybe not today, maybe not until we look back — and set our hearts burning the way theirs did. Amen.', true),
  ('ca407f9a-3715-5d1f-851d-d0d587ed4c81', 'John 20:19-23', 'Peace', 'When therefore it was evening, on that day, the first day of the week, and when the doors were locked where the disciples were assembled, for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood in the midst, and said to them, "Peace be to you." When he had said this, he showed them his hands and his side. The disciples therefore were glad when they saw the Lord. Jesus therefore said to them again, "Peace be to you. As the Father has sent me, even so I send you." When he had said this, he breathed on them, and said to them, "Receive the Holy Spirit! Whoever''s sins you forgive, they are forgiven them. Whoever''s sins you retain, they have been retained."', 'The disciples are locked in a room out of real fear — the same authorities who killed Jesus are still out there. The first thing the risen Jesus does when he appears to them isn''t a rebuke for abandoning him at the crucifixion. It''s peace.',
   'When you walk into a room where someone''s afraid, what''s usually the first thing you say or do?', 'What''s a fear you''re currently locked behind, the way the disciples were locked in that room?', 'These are the same men who scattered when Jesus was arrested — one of them denied even knowing him. Jesus''s first words to them after rising from the dead are "peace be with you," said twice, before he ever sends them anywhere. Could they have received a commission from him before they''d received the peace, or does the order actually matter?',
   'Peace before commission — that''s not how we usually operate, Jesus. We receive it in that order tonight anyway: your peace first, before we try to figure out what we''re supposed to do with it. Send us out the way you sent them. Amen.', true),
  ('6952319a-6d46-529e-964e-d595f9a64036', 'Mark 12:41-44', 'Money', 'Jesus sat down opposite the treasury, and saw how the multitude cast money into the treasury. Many who were rich cast in much. A poor widow came, and she cast in two small brass coins, which equal a quadrans coin. He called his disciples to himself, and said to them, "Most assuredly I tell you, this poor widow gave more than all those who are giving into the treasury, for they all gave out of their abundance, but she, out of her poverty, gave all that she had to live on."', 'Jesus sits down and watches people give — not to judge them, but simply to see. Wealthy people put in large, visible sums. Then a widow puts in two coins worth almost nothing, and Jesus is the only one who seems to notice what that actually cost her.',
   'Has someone ever done something small for you that meant more than a much bigger gesture would have?', 'What''s something small you could give — of your time, attention, or resources — that would actually cost you something, rather than something you wouldn''t miss?', 'Nobody else in the story seems to notice the widow at all — she''s easy to miss next to the loud, visible giving happening around her. Jesus sees her anyway and calls his disciples over specifically to point her out. If Jesus is measuring by what something costs the giver instead of the amount given, what would your own giving look like re-measured on that scale instead of the one you actually use?',
   'Nobody else in that story noticed her, Jesus. Help us see the small, costly things happening around us that we usually miss — and let our own giving cost us something real, not just what''s left over. Amen.', true),
  ('ecc8ee57-0f8a-5672-8759-e01c3d5d1693', 'Proverbs 18:22', 'Marriage', 'Whoever finds a wife finds a good thing, And obtains favor of Yahweh.', 'This is a proverb — a short observation about life, not a guarantee. Solomon frames marriage here as something to be grateful for, not just something achieved or earned.',
   'If you''re married, what''s one small thing about your spouse you''re grateful for today? If you''re not, what''s a marriage you''ve watched up close that you admire?', 'Where has gratitude for the people closest to you quietly turned into just taking them for granted?', 'The proverb calls marriage a "good thing" connected to God''s favor — not to how hard someone worked to earn it. What does it look like to actually treat a close relationship as a gift you were given, rather than something you''re owed?',
   'God, thank you for the people you''ve placed closest to us. Where we''ve slipped into taking them for granted, wake us back up to noticing them as the gift they are. Amen.', true),
  ('2b660dbf-277a-5dc1-8ba1-63ce82219561', 'Song of Solomon 8:6-7', 'Marriage', 'Set me as a seal on your heart, As a seal on your arm; For love is strong as death. Jealousy is as cruel as Sheol; Its flashes are flashes of fire, A very flame of Yahweh. Many waters can''t quench love, Neither can floods drown it. If a man would give all the wealth of his house for love, He would be utterly scorned.', 'This is love poetry, spoken between two people who delight in each other — Scripture''s own permission to talk about marriage as joyful and passionate, not only dutiful.',
   'What''s a love story — real, or from a movie or book — that genuinely moves you?', 'Has the passion in a close relationship of yours faded into routine? What would it take to actually notice each other again?', 'The poem insists love can''t be bought — "if a man gave all the wealth of his house for love, he would be utterly scorned." What''s the difference between love that''s purchased or performed and the kind this poem describes, and which one actually lasts?',
   'Lord, thank you for making love this strong and this stubborn — unable to be bought or drowned out. Rekindle whatever''s gone quiet in our closest relationships, and remind us what we first saw in each other. Amen.', true),
  ('9d249e4b-a602-5f0f-9f1f-14e417e9ec1e', 'Proverbs 31:10-12', 'Marriage', 'Who can find a worthy woman? For her price is far above rubies. The heart of her husband trusts in her. He shall have no lack of gain. She does him good, and not harm, All the days of her life.', 'This is the opening of a longer poem honoring a capable, trustworthy wife — not a checklist to measure up to, but a picture of trust and mutual good sustained over a whole life, "all the days."',
   'Who''s someone whose word you trust completely — where you never wonder if they''re telling you the truth?', 'Is there someone close to you whose good qualities you''ve stopped saying out loud, even though you still notice them?', 'The poem measures worth by trust built over "all the days," not one impressive moment. What''s the difference between admiring someone for something they did once and actually trusting them because of years of small, consistent good?',
   'Father, thank you for the people who do us good, day after day, without anyone applauding. Help us actually say the words of appreciation out loud tonight, not just think them. Amen.', true),
  ('4088dbfa-4c1b-5cf1-8d87-3ffa53b35501', '1 Peter 3:7', 'Marriage', 'You husbands, in like manner, live with your wives according to knowledge, giving honor to the woman, as to the weaker vessel, as being also joint heirs of the grace of life; that your prayers may not be hindered.', 'Peter is writing to husbands, in a culture where women had few legal protections. "Weaker vessel" isn''t Peter ranking a wife''s worth — his very next words call her a "joint heir," a full and equal co-owner of everything that matters most. The instruction is to honor and understand her precisely because she was more vulnerable in that world, not because she counted for less.',
   'What does it mean to really try to understand someone, instead of just reacting to them?', 'Is there someone in your life you''ve stopped trying to understand, and started just responding to instead?', 'Peter ties a husband''s prayers being "hindered" directly to how he treats his wife, as if the two are connected in a way most people wouldn''t expect. What does it say about God''s priorities that how you treat the person closest to you affects your relationship with him?',
   'Lord, teach us what it means to actually live with someone "according to knowledge" — paying real attention, not assuming we already understand them. Remove whatever''s been standing between our closest relationships and our prayers. Amen.', true),
  ('e60f5f16-c001-5a03-ac23-05bfd161ca6a', 'Ephesians 5:25-33', 'Marriage', 'Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the assembly, and gave himself up for it; that he might sanctify it, having cleansed it by the washing of water with the word, that he might present the assembly to himself gloriously, not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing; but that it should be holy and without blemish. Even so ought husbands also to love their own wives as their own bodies. He who loves his own wife loves himself. For no man ever hated his own flesh; but nourishes and cherishes it, even as the Lord also does the assembly; because we are members of his body, of his flesh and bones. "For this cause a man will leave his father and mother, and will be joined to his wife. The two will become one flesh." This mystery is great, but I speak concerning Christ and of the assembly. Nevertheless each of you must also love his own wife even as himself; and let the wife see that she respects her husband.', 'Paul is speaking to husbands here, and the standard he sets is Christ''s own sacrificial love — giving himself up entirely, not asking to be served. This is a passage about what love is supposed to cost the one giving it, not about who''s in charge.',
   'What''s something someone sacrificed for you that you didn''t fully appreciate until much later?', 'In your closest relationship, are you more focused on what you''re owed, or on what you''re willing to give?', 'Paul says a husband who loves his wife "loves himself" — that her good and his good aren''t actually separate accounts. What would it look like to stop keeping score of whose needs come first, because you''d stopped seeing them as two competing totals?',
   'Jesus, you loved your church enough to give yourself up completely, before we''d earned it. Teach us that kind of love in our closest relationships — not scorekeeping, not self-protecting, just actually giving ourselves the way you gave yourself. Amen.', true),
  ('a2024749-e594-5618-8566-b4415bb9a698', 'Ephesians 6:1-4', 'Parenting', 'Children, obey your parents in the Lord, for this is right. "Honor your father and mother," which is the first commandment with a promise: "that it may be well with you, and you may live long on the earth." You fathers, don''t provoke your children to wrath, but nurture them in the discipline and instruction of the Lord.', 'Paul addresses two different people in the same short passage — children, then fathers — and gives each of them something specific to actually do, not just one side of the family a set of rules to follow.',
   'What''s a family rule that actually made sense to you once you understood why it existed?', 'Kids — has a parent''s correction ever actually felt like it came from love, not just anger? Parents — has your own frustration ever driven a reaction more than what your kid actually needed?', 'Paul doesn''t just tell children to obey — in the very next breath he tells fathers not to provoke their children to anger. The instruction cuts both directions at once. What changes in a household when both the obedience and the gentleness are treated as equally serious, not just one side''s job?',
   'God, in this family we''re each being asked for something — some of us honor, some of us patience. Show each of us our own actual part in that tonight, not just the part that''s someone else''s job. Amen.', true),
  ('2396ba8e-26f5-5b97-a6bf-28650266f4ce', '1 Thessalonians 2:11-12', 'Parenting', 'As you know how we exhorted, comforted, and implored every one of you, as a father does his own children, to the end that you should walk worthily of God, who calls you into his own Kingdom and glory.', 'Paul isn''t a biological father to the believers in Thessalonica — he''s describing how he cared for them by comparing it to a good father''s care: not just correction, but exhortation, comfort, and pleading, all together.',
   'Who''s someone who''s encouraged you in a way you still remember?', 'Do you tend to correct people more than you comfort them, or the other way around?', 'Paul names three different things he did — exhorted (pushed forward), comforted (held close), and implored (pleaded). Most people naturally lean on one of the three. Which one do you rely on most, and what might you be missing by not using the other two?',
   'Lord, thank you for people who''ve pushed us forward, comforted us, and pleaded with us — sometimes all in one conversation. Help us learn to do all three for the people in our own home, not just the one that comes easiest. Amen.', true),
  ('fcdd4231-7213-5762-93d6-81f83a5ad6e4', '1 Samuel 1:27-28', 'Parenting', 'For this child I prayed; and Yahweh has given me my petition which I asked of him: therefore also I have granted him to Yahweh; as long as he lives he is granted to Yahweh. He worshiped Yahweh there.', 'Hannah had wept and prayed for years for a child, promising that if God gave her a son, she''d give him back to serve God''s purposes. This is her keeping that promise — bringing her long-awaited son to the temple to be raised there, not in her own home.',
   'Has anyone ever given you something they clearly really wanted to keep for themselves?', 'Is there something — or someone — in your life you''re holding onto more tightly than you probably should, out of fear of losing it?', 'Hannah prayed for years for this exact child, and the moment she has him, she gives him away to serve a purpose bigger than her own household. What does it suggest about the nature of real love, that it can hold something precious loosely instead of gripping it tightly?',
   'Father, we don''t find it easy to hold the people and things we love loosely. Teach us what Hannah knew — that giving someone back to you isn''t losing them, it''s trusting you with them. Amen.', true),
  ('fb5e7538-d736-5e7f-ad37-7c040dc613b7', 'Hebrews 12:9-11', 'Parenting', 'Furthermore, we had the fathers of our flesh to chasten us, and we paid them respect. Shall we not much rather be in subjection to the Father of spirits, and live? For they indeed, for a few days, punished us as seemed good to them; but he for our profit, that we may be partakers of his holiness. All chastening seems for the present to be not joyous but grievous; yet afterward it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby.', 'The writer compares human fathers'' discipline — imperfect, limited, done "as seemed good to them" — to God''s discipline, which is always aimed at our actual good. The comparison is about purpose, not method.',
   'Was there ever a rule or consequence growing up that felt unfair at the time, but makes more sense to you now?', 'Is there a correction you''ve received — from a parent, a boss, anyone — that actually helped you, even though it didn''t feel good in the moment?', 'The passage admits plainly that discipline never feels good "for the present" — it doesn''t try to sell correction as pleasant. What''s the difference between discipline that''s actually aimed at someone''s good, even when it hurts, and discipline that''s really just the person giving it venting their own frustration?',
   'God, thank you for caring about us enough to correct us, even when it doesn''t feel good. Help us tell the difference, in our own home, between discipline that''s actually for someone''s good and moments we''re just reacting out of our own frustration. Amen.', true),
  ('69bc24f7-5516-5eed-a199-2047f5d768d2', '3 John 1:4', 'Parenting', 'I have no greater joy than this, to hear about my children walking in truth.', 'John is likely writing about people he''d spiritually mentored, not necessarily his own biological children — but he reaches for the warmest family language available to describe how it feels to watch someone he cares about live well.',
   'Who''s someone whose growth or success genuinely makes you happy, almost like it''s your own?', 'Is there someone you''ve stopped checking in on, who you used to care a lot about?', 'John says this — not his own accomplishments — is his greatest joy. What would it look like to actually measure whether your own life is going well by that standard, instead of the more common ones?',
   'Jesus, thank you for the specific people whose lives bring us real joy just to watch. Help us tell them so, out loud, and help us become people whose greatest joy is someone else''s good, not just our own. Amen.', true),
  ('4b2b6670-ee3b-544b-a35b-e4172a844fbd', 'Luke 2:41-52', 'Parenting', 'His parents went every year to Jerusalem at the feast of the Passover. When he was twelve years old, they went up to Jerusalem according to the custom of the feast, and when they had fulfilled the days, as they were returning, the boy Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem. Joseph and his mother didn''t know it, but supposing him to be in the company, they went a day''s journey, and they looked for him among their relatives and acquaintances. When they didn''t find him, they returned to Jerusalem, looking for him. It happened after three days they found him in the temple, sitting in the midst of the teachers, both listening to them, and asking them questions. All who heard him were amazed at his understanding and his answers. When they saw him, they were astonished, and his mother said to him, "Son, why have you treated us this way? Behold, your father and I were anxiously looking for you." He said to them, "Why were you looking for me? Didn''t you know that I must be in my Father''s house?" They didn''t understand the saying which he spoke to them. And he went down with them, and came to Nazareth. He was subject to them, and his mother kept all these sayings in her heart. And Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and men.', 'Jesus is twelve — old enough in that culture to be nearly an adult by some measures, still a child by others. His parents travel a full day before even noticing he''s missing, then spend three anxious days searching before finding him exactly where he''d been the whole time: in the temple.',
   'Has anyone ever completely lost track of you, or you of them, even briefly — what was it like when you found each other again?', 'Parents — where do you find it hardest to loosen your grip as your kids grow up? Kids and teens — where do you feel misunderstood when you''re doing something that genuinely matters to you?', 'Mary asks Jesus, in effect, why he did this to them — and his answer doesn''t really apologize, it just states what was obviously true to him, even though "they didn''t understand the saying." He still goes home and is obedient. What does it look like to hold both of those at once — being genuinely misunderstood, and still choosing to stay in loving relationship with the people who don''t fully get you?',
   'Father, growing up — and letting go — is hard on everyone in a family, not just one side of it. Give parents the trust to loosen their grip at the right moments, and give kids the patience to stay in relationship even when they feel misunderstood. Amen.', true),
  ('f876d4c4-268e-52d1-a790-491cf2b7d7dd', 'Genesis 37:3-4', 'Family', 'Now Israel loved Joseph more than all his children, because he was the son of his old age, and he made him a coat of many colors. His brothers saw that their father loved him more than all his brothers, and they hated him, and couldn''t speak peaceably to him.', 'This opens one of the most consequential family stories in Scripture — a father''s obvious favoritism toward one son curdling into real hatred among the rest. What starts here eventually leads to Joseph being sold into slavery by his own brothers.',
   'Growing up, did it ever feel like someone — a sibling, a classmate, a teammate — was clearly the favorite? What was that like?', 'If you''re responsible for more than one child, or more than one close relationship, is there a way you show favor without meaning to?', 'The text doesn''t say the brothers were wrong to notice the favoritism — it says they noticed it accurately, and it became hatred. What does this story suggest about how much damage a small, repeated pattern — a coat, an obvious preference — can eventually cause, long before anyone intended real harm?',
   'God, help us see the small ways we might be playing favorites in our own family — with our time, our praise, our patience — before it grows into something none of us wanted. Amen.', true),
  ('1428c7fe-b08e-5f5d-9b60-3d4f0ef77f88', 'Genesis 33:1-4', 'Family', 'Jacob lifted up his eyes, and looked, and, behold, Esau was coming, and with him four hundred men. He divided the children between Leah, Rachel, and to the two handmaids. He put the handmaids and their children in front, Leah and her children after, and Rachel and Joseph at the rear. He himself passed over in front of them, and bowed himself to the ground seven times, until he came near to his brother. Esau ran to meet him, embraced him, fell on his neck, kissed him, and they wept.', 'Jacob had deceived his brother Esau out of both his birthright and his father''s blessing, then fled for his life. Decades later, coming home, Jacob is still afraid Esau wants him dead, and has arranged his family defensively, expecting the worst.',
   'Have you ever been surprised by how someone reacted when you expected the worst from them?', 'Is there a relationship in your life still shaped by something that happened a long time ago, even though the other person might have moved past it?', 'Jacob arrives bracing for judgment, bowing seven times — and Esau just runs to him. Scripture never tells us what changed in Esau over those years, or whether it was easy. What do you think made this reunion possible, and is every relationship like this able to end the same way?',
   'Lord, we don''t always know what softens a heart toward us, or ours toward someone else — sometimes it just is what it is by the time paths cross again. Where reconciliation is actually possible in our own family, help us not brace for the worst the way Jacob did. Amen.', true),
  ('c496eb6c-4957-56a6-b009-e432c453aba0', 'Luke 15:25-32', 'Family', '"Now his elder son was in the field. As he came near to the house, he heard music and dancing. He called one of the servants to him, and asked what was going on. He said to him, ''Your brother has come, and your father has killed the fattened calf, because he has received him back safe and healthy.'' But he was angry, and would not go in. Therefore his father came out, and begged him. But he answered his father, ''Behold, these many years I have served you, and I never disobeyed a commandment of yours, but you never gave me a goat, that I might celebrate with my friends. But when this, your son, came, who has devoured your living with prostitutes, you killed the fattened calf for him.'' "He said to him, ''Son, you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours. But it was appropriate to celebrate and be glad, for this, your brother, was dead, and is alive again. He was lost, and is found.''"', 'While his younger brother was off wasting the family inheritance, this son stayed, worked, and did everything right. When the younger brother comes home to a celebration, nobody thinks to come get the older brother from the field.',
   'Have you ever done everything right and felt like it went unnoticed, especially next to someone else getting attention for doing something wrong?', 'Is there someone in your life whose struggles seem to get more attention than your own steady faithfulness?', 'The father doesn''t argue with the older son''s facts — he agrees, "you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours." He doesn''t correct the math. He just invites him to see it differently: that his brother being found is worth celebrating, regardless of what it costs in fairness. What''s the difference between what''s fair and what''s worth celebrating — and can you actually want both at the same time?',
   'Father, we don''t always love it when someone else gets celebrated for finally doing what we''ve been doing all along. Help us celebrate other people''s homecomings without needing our own faithfulness to be the story instead. Amen.', true),
  ('fdd84e3a-7ce1-5114-878e-489ff74439a2', 'Genesis 45:1-5', 'Family', 'Then Joseph couldn''t control himself before all those who stood before him, and he cried, "Cause every man to go out from me!" There stood no man with him, while Joseph made himself known to his brothers. He wept aloud. The Egyptians heard, and the house of Pharaoh heard. Joseph said to his brothers, "I am Joseph! Does my father still live?" His brothers couldn''t answer him; for they were terrified at his presence. Joseph said to his brothers, "Come near to me, please." They came near. "He said, I am Joseph, your brother, whom you sold into Egypt. Now don''t be grieved, nor angry with yourselves, that you sold me here, for God sent me before you to preserve life.', 'Years after his brothers sold him into slavery, Joseph is now powerful in Egypt, face to face with them again, and they have no idea who he is. He sends everyone else out of the room before he tells them.',
   'Have you ever needed to have a really important, private conversation with someone — one you didn''t want an audience for?', 'Is there someone you''re not fully honest with, out of fear of how they''ll react once they know the whole truth?', 'Joseph clears the room before he reveals himself — this reconciliation happens in private, not as a performance for anyone else. His brothers are terrified, and his first real words to them are about what God was doing, not about what they did to him. What do you notice about the order Joseph puts things in, and what does it suggest about how he''d spent those years thinking about what happened to him?',
   'Lord, some of the hardest, most honest conversations in a family happen away from everyone else''s eyes. Where we need to have one of those, give us Joseph''s courage to actually say the true thing, and whatever peace he''d already made before he said it. Amen.', true),
  ('a14d08c4-3988-5939-ae2c-35010ff30285', 'Ruth 1:16-17', 'Family', 'Ruth said, "Don''t entreat me to leave you, and to return from following after you, for where you go, I will go; and where you lodge, I will lodge; your people shall be my people, and your God my God; where you die, will I die, and there will I be buried: Yahweh do so to me, and more also, if anything but death part you and me."', 'Naomi has lost her husband and both her sons, and tells her daughters-in-law to go back to their own families and start over. Ruth — not blood family, an in-law with every reasonable excuse to leave — refuses.',
   'Is there someone in your life who''s family to you even though you''re not related by blood?', 'Naomi actually tries to talk Ruth out of staying. Is there a relationship in your life where you''ve had to choose to stay, even when leaving would''ve been easier and nobody would have blamed you?', 'Ruth doesn''t just promise loyalty — she says "your people shall be my people, and your God my God." She''s not just committing to a person; she''s committing to that person''s whole life and faith. What''s the difference between loyalty that sticks around for the good parts and the kind Ruth is describing here?',
   'Father, thank you for the people who''ve chosen to stay when they didn''t have to — the family we didn''t choose by blood, but who chose us anyway. Help us be that kind of family for someone who needs it. Amen.', true),
  ('5829a409-8339-572e-a6fd-95ca5772a241', '1 Timothy 5:4', 'Family', 'But if any widow has children or grandchildren, let them learn first to show piety towards their own family, and to repay their parents, for this is acceptable in the sight of God.', 'Paul is giving the early church practical instructions about caring for widows, and he starts with the most obvious place responsibility should land first: their own children and grandchildren, before anyone else steps in.',
   'What''s something a parent or grandparent did for you when you were young that you''d genuinely like to repay someday?', 'Is there an aging family member you''ve been meaning to check on, call, or actually go see?', 'Paul calls this "repaying" — not just being nice, but settling something that''s actually owed. What changes if you think of caring for aging parents as repaying a real debt, rather than an optional kindness you get to if you happen to have the time?',
   'God, we''ve been on the receiving end of years of care we didn''t ask for and can never fully repay. Show us one specific, real way to show that kind of care to our own family this week — not just think about it. Amen.', true),
  ('1436ef14-d3b8-5f31-891f-7f01cc2da859', 'Galatians 6:1-2', 'Community', 'Brothers, even if a man is caught in some fault, you who are spiritual must restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness; looking to yourself so that you also aren''t tempted. Bear one another''s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ.', 'Paul is describing how to treat someone in the community who''s messed up — not by looking down on them, but by gently helping carry what they''re carrying, while staying humble enough to know it could just as easily be you.',
   'What''s something someone helped you carry — literally or otherwise — that you couldn''t have carried alone?', 'Is there a burden someone in your own family is carrying right now that you could actually help with, in a specific, practical way?', 'Paul pairs two instructions in the same breath — restore someone gently, and watch out for your own temptation while you do it. Why do you think he ties helping someone else directly to staying humble about your own weakness, instead of treating them as two separate ideas?',
   'Lord, show us a burden in our own home tonight that we could actually help carry, not just notice from a distance. And keep us humble enough to remember we need the same kind of help ourselves. Amen.', true),
  ('63e6a5da-b5c4-5bf1-bfe3-f16b5534ac84', 'Ephesians 4:29', 'Integrity', 'Let no corrupt speech proceed out of your mouth, but such as is good for building up as the need may be, that it may give grace to those who hear.', 'Paul doesn''t just say "don''t say bad things" — he gives a specific, positive standard instead: does what you''re about to say actually build the other person up, and fit what they need right now?',
   'What''s something someone said to you once that you still remember, because it built you up?', 'Is there a way you tend to talk to the people in your own home that you''d never talk to anyone else — friends, coworkers, strangers?', 'The standard here isn''t just "true" or "not unkind" — it''s "good for building up as the need may be." That''s a higher, more specific bar than simply avoiding insults. What''s the difference between words that are technically fair and words that actually build someone up in the exact moment they need it?',
   'Jesus, we say things inside our own home we''d never say anywhere else, and we know it. Guard what comes out of our mouths tonight, especially with the people who hear us the most. Amen.', true),
  ('5f481326-7357-5b3f-a011-22344bed1a76', 'Joshua 4:1-7', 'Family', 'It happened, when all the nation were clean passed over the Jordan, that Yahweh spoke to Joshua, saying, Take twelve men out of the people, out of every tribe a man, and command you them, saying, Take hence out of the midst of the Jordan, out of the place where the priests'' feet stood firm, twelve stones, and carry them over with you, and lay them down in the lodging-place, where you shall lodge this night. Then Joshua called the twelve men, whom he had prepared of the children of Israel, out of every tribe a man: and Joshua said to them, Pass over before the ark of Yahweh your God into the midst of the Jordan, and take up every man of you a stone on his shoulder, according to the number of the tribes of the children of Israel; that this may be a sign among you, that when your children ask in time to come, saying, What do you mean by these stones? then you shall tell them, Because the waters of the Jordan were cut off before the ark of the covenant of Yahweh; when it passed over the Jordan, the waters of the Jordan were cut off: and these stones shall be for a memorial to the children of Israel forever.', 'After crossing the Jordan River on dry ground, God has Joshua set up a monument of stones — not really for that generation''s benefit, but specifically so that children, years later, would ask what the stones meant, giving their parents a reason to tell the story.',
   'Does your family have a tradition or story that gets retold every year? What is it?', 'Is there something God has done in your own life you''ve never actually told your kids, or the younger people around you, about?', 'God doesn''t just want this remembered — he builds in a way for the next generation to ask about it on their own, out of real curiosity, instead of being lectured. What''s the difference between a family tradition that invites questions and one that just delivers a message whether anyone''s curious or not?',
   'God, help us build the kind of family traditions that make our kids actually want to ask what they mean, instead of tuning us out. And give us the stories worth telling when they do ask. Amen.', true),
  ('808bbcf5-8625-555e-a0e8-471f1f67d671', 'John 11:28-37', 'Grief & Loss', 'When she had said this, she went away, and called Mary, her sister, secretly, saying, "The Teacher is here, and is calling you." When she heard this, she arose quickly, and went to him. Now Jesus had not yet come into the village, but was in the place where Martha met him. Then the Jews who were with her in the house, and were consoling her, when they saw Mary, that she rose up quickly and went out, followed her, saying, "She is going to the tomb to weep there." Therefore when Mary came to where Jesus was, and saw him, she fell down at his feet, saying to him, "Lord, if you would have been here, my brother wouldn''t have died." When Jesus therefore saw her weeping, and the Jews weeping who came with her, he groaned in the spirit, and was troubled, and said, "Where have you laid him?" They told him, "Lord, come and see." Jesus wept. The Jews therefore said, "See how much affection he had for him!" Some of them said, "Couldn''t this man, who opened the eyes of him who was blind, have also kept this man from dying?"', 'Jesus already knew he was about to raise Lazarus — he''d said as much to Martha just before this. Knowing that didn''t stop him from being deeply troubled, or from weeping, when he saw Mary''s grief and the grief of everyone around her.',
   'Has someone else''s grief ever moved you to tears, even over a loss that wasn''t yours?', 'Is there a loss in your life, big or small, that you''ve never really let yourself feel all the way?', 'Jesus knew Lazarus would be alive again within minutes, and he wept anyway. Scripture doesn''t explain why. What do you think it means that knowing the ending didn''t erase the weight of the moment, even for him?',
   'Jesus, you knew Lazarus would live, and you wept anyway. We don''t fully understand that, and we don''t need to. Be as near to our grief tonight as you were to theirs. Amen.', true),
  ('d94e779b-7198-5887-957b-47992564d10f', 'Psalm 88', 'Grief & Loss', 'Yahweh, the God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before you. Let my prayer enter into your presence. Turn your ear to my cry. For my soul is full of troubles. My life draws near to Sheol. I am counted among those who go down into the pit. I am like a man who has no help, set apart among the dead, like the slain who lie in the grave, whom you remember no more. They are cut off from your hand. You have laid me in the lowest pit, in the darkest depths. Your wrath lies heavily on me. You have afflicted me with all your waves. You have taken my friends from me. You have made me an abomination to them. I am confined, and I can''t escape. My eyes are dim from grief. I have called on you daily, Yahweh. I have spread out my hands to you. But to you, Yahweh, I have cried. In the morning, my prayer comes before you. Yahweh, why do you reject my soul? Why do you hide your face from me? I am afflicted and ready to die from my youth up. While I suffer your terrors, I am distracted. Your fierce wrath has gone over me. Your terrors have cut me off. They came around me like water all day long. They completely engulfed me. You have put lover and friend far from me, and my friends into darkness.', 'Of all the psalms, this is the one that ends without turning toward praise or resolution — it simply stops, still in the dark. It''s included in Scripture exactly as it is.',
   'Have you ever prayed about something and felt like the prayer just went nowhere?', 'Is there something you''ve stopped bringing to God because it never seems to change?', 'This psalm made it into God''s own Word without a happy ending stapled onto it. What do you think it means that Scripture makes room for prayers that never resolve, right alongside the ones that do?',
   'God, we don''t always get an answer, and this psalm proves we''re allowed to say so. Hear us anyway. Amen.', true),
  ('25638f6e-ea4d-5fd8-a016-0a917c4c4354', '2 Samuel 18:31-33', 'Grief & Loss', 'Behold, the Cushite came; and the Cushite said, News for my lord the king; for Yahweh has avenged you this day of all those who rose up against you. The king said to the Cushite, Is it well with the young man Absalom? The Cushite answered, The enemies of my lord the king, and all who rise up against you to do you hurt, be as that young man is. The king was much moved, and went up to the chamber over the gate, and wept: and as he went, thus he said, my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom! would I had died for you, Absalom, my son, my son!', 'Absalom had led a violent rebellion against his own father, David. None of that matters to David the moment he learns his son is dead — he doesn''t ask about the victory. He asks about his son.',
   'Has grief ever caught you off guard for someone you had a genuinely complicated relationship with?', 'Is there a relationship in your life so complicated you''re not sure how you''d feel if you lost it?', 'David''s men just won a war that saved his kingdom, and he can''t celebrate — he can only repeat his son''s name. Scripture never tells him to get over it or move on to the celebration. What does it say about grief that it doesn''t ask permission, even when the timing seems wrong to everyone around you?',
   'Lord, some grief doesn''t care what else is happening around it. If that''s true for someone at this table tonight, don''t rush them past it. Amen.', true),
  ('d828fbff-7c92-56a8-8a24-2a63f6b18f89', 'Ecclesiastes 3:1-4', 'Grief & Loss', 'For everything there is a season, and a time for every purpose under heaven: A time to be born, And a time to die; A time to plant, And a time to pluck up that which is planted; A time to kill, And a time to heal; A time to break down, And a time to build up; A time to weep, And a time to laugh; A time to mourn, And a time to dance;', 'The Teacher lists these opposite pairs not as things to feel guilty about, but as a plain description of how life actually moves — through both, in their own time.',
   'What season of life do you feel like you''re in right now — building something, or letting something go?', 'Is there a "time to weep" you''ve been rushing yourself out of, because it felt like you should be past it by now?', 'The passage never says which season is better, or how long each one should last — only that each one has its time. What would change if you stopped judging which season you''re supposed to already be in?',
   'God, thank you for making room in your Word for every season, not just the good ones. Give us permission to actually be in the one we''re in right now. Amen.', true),
  ('1e9c8ad8-0467-58f5-9fbe-4f7e5787552f', 'Matthew 5:4', 'Grief & Loss', 'Blessed are those who mourn, For they shall be comforted.', 'This sits inside the Beatitudes — a list of people Jesus calls blessed that would have genuinely surprised his original audience, since mourning wasn''t usually anyone''s idea of a blessed state.',
   'What''s a word you''d use instead of "blessed" to describe how mourning actually feels?', 'Does it feel true to you that God calls mourning blessed, or does that feel hard to actually believe right now?', 'Jesus doesn''t say blessed are those who mourn quickly, or mourn correctly, or mourn quietly — just those who mourn. What is he promising here, and what is he deliberately not promising?',
   'Jesus, you called mourning blessed instead of shameful. Help us actually believe that tonight, even if we can''t feel it yet. Amen.', true),
  ('c313af5c-76da-580c-aa3a-a175fdb24be5', 'Genesis 16:7-13', 'Grief & Loss', 'The angel of Yahweh found her by a fountain of water in the wilderness, by the fountain in the way to Shur. He said, "Hagar, Sarai''s handmaid, where did you come from? Where are you going?" She said, "I am fleeing from the face of my mistress Sarai." The angel of Yahweh said to her, "Return to your mistress, and submit yourself under her hands." The angel of Yahweh said to her, "I will greatly multiply your seed, that they will not be numbered for multitude." The angel of Yahweh said to her, "Behold, you are with child, and will bear a son. You shall call his name Ishmael, because Yahweh has heard your affliction. He will be like a wild donkey among men. His hand will be against every man, and every man''s hand against him. He will live opposite all of his brothers." She called the name of Yahweh who spoke to her, "You are a God who sees," for she said, "Have I even stayed alive after seeing him?"', 'Hagar is alone in the wilderness, pregnant, having fled a household where she was mistreated. Nobody sent anyone to look for her. God finds her anyway.',
   'Has there been a moment you felt like nobody really knew where you were or what you were going through?', 'Is there a part of your life right now that feels invisible to the people around you?', 'Hagar names God herself, based on this one encounter — "You are a God who sees." She''s a servant, a foreigner, someone with no social standing at all in that world. What does it say about God that this is exactly who he shows up for, and lets name him?',
   'God, you are the God who sees — Hagar said so, and we''re asking you to be that for us too. See what feels invisible tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('c3aa4b77-1ca7-522a-9ca7-cd81d9185533', '1 Kings 19:1-13', 'Grief & Loss', 'Ahab told Jezebel all that Elijah had done, and withal how he had slain all the prophets with the sword. Then Jezebel send a messenger to Elijah, saying, So let the gods do to me, and more also, if I don''t make your life as the life of one of them by tomorrow about this time. When he saw that, he arose, and went for his life, and came to Beersheba, which belongs to Judah, and left his servant there. But he himself went a day''s journey into the wilderness, and came and sat down under a juniper tree: and he requested for himself that he might die, and said, It is enough; now, O Yahweh, take away my life; for I am not better than my fathers. He lay down and slept under a juniper tree; and, behold, an angel touched him, and said to him, Arise and eat. He looked, and, behold, there was at his head a cake baked on the coals, and a jar of water. He ate and drink, and laid him down again. The angel of Yahweh came again the second time, and touched him, and said, Arise and eat, because the journey is too great for you. He arose, and ate and drink, and went in the strength of that food forty days and forty nights to Horeb the Mount of God. He came there to a cave, and lodged there; and, behold, the word of Yahweh came to him, and he said to him, What are you doing here, Elijah? He said, I have been very jealous for Yahweh, the God of hosts; for the children of Israel have forsaken your covenant, thrown down your altars, and slain your prophets with the sword: and I, even I only, am left; and they seek my life, to take it away. He said, Go forth, and stand on the mountain before Yahweh. Behold, Yahweh passed by, and a great and strong wind tore the mountains, and broke in pieces the rocks before Yahweh; but Yahweh was not in the wind: and after the wind an earthquake; but Yahweh was not in the earthquake: and after the earthquake a fire; but Yahweh was not in the fire: and after the fire a still small voice. It was so, when Elijah heard it, that he wrapped his face in his mantle, and went out, and stood in the entrance of the cave. Behold, there came a voice to him, and said, What are you doing here, Elijah?', 'Elijah had just won a massive spiritual victory, and now, threatened and utterly exhausted, he runs into the wilderness and asks God to let him die. God doesn''t argue with him or rush him. He sends food, lets him sleep, twice — and only after that does he actually speak, not in the dramatic wind or earthquake or fire, but in a quiet voice.',
   'What genuinely restores you when you''re running on empty — sleep, food, quiet, something else?', 'Have you ever hit a point of real exhaustion right after something that should have felt like a win?', 'God''s first response to Elijah''s despair isn''t a pep talk or a rebuke — it''s food and rest, twice, before a single word. What does that order suggest about how God cares for someone who''s completely run out?',
   'God, sometimes we need to be fed and rested before we''re ready to hear from you at all. Meet us in whatever order we actually need tonight, not the order we think we should be in. Amen.', true),
  ('683e2f6c-0caf-5ffe-ba01-9282b4eb8342', 'Psalm 25:16-18', 'Grief & Loss', 'Turn to me, and have mercy on me, For I am desolate and afflicted. The troubles of my heart are enlarged. Oh bring me out of my distresses. Consider my affliction and my travail. Forgive all my sins.', 'David doesn''t lead with a request to fix his circumstances — he asks first simply to be seen, for his loneliness and trouble to be considered.',
   'What''s a time you felt lonely even though you weren''t physically alone?', 'Is there a loneliness in your life right now that the people around you probably don''t even know about?', 'David asks God to "consider" his affliction before he asks for anything to change. What''s the difference between asking God to fix something and asking God to simply notice it first?',
   'God, before we ask you to fix anything tonight, we just want you to notice. See the loneliness in this room, named or not. Amen.', true),
  ('f61fbe37-2cec-5abb-a887-5b0901ddd490', 'Psalm 13', 'Grief & Loss', 'How long, Yahweh? Will you forget me forever? How long will you hide your face from me? How long shall I take counsel in my soul, Having sorrow in my heart every day? How long shall my enemy triumph over me? Behold, and answer me, Yahweh, my God. Give light to my eyes, lest I sleep in death; Lest my enemy say, "I have prevailed against him;" Lest my adversaries rejoice when I fall. But I trust in your loving kindness. My heart rejoices in your salvation. I will sing to Yahweh, Because he has been good to me.', 'This is a complete short prayer — it opens in raw complaint and, without any stated explanation, ends in trust. Scripture doesn''t tell us how much time passed between the two halves, or whether the circumstances actually changed.',
   'Have you ever asked "how long" about something — out loud or just in your own head?', 'Is there something right now you''d honestly ask God "how long" about, if you let yourself?', 'The psalm never explains what changed between the complaint and the trust — no resolved circumstance is mentioned in between. What do you think happened there, and does the trust in the last lines require the complaint in the first ones to be fully answered first?',
   'Yahweh, how long — we ask that too, sometimes. Help us trust you can hold both the honest complaint and the real trust, even in the same breath. Amen.', true),
  ('a1059dff-b8f4-5da2-adb4-0ea6bf35363a', 'Habakkuk 1:2-4', 'Suffering', 'Yahweh, how long will I cry, and you will not hear? I cry out to you "Violence!" and will you not save? Why do you show me iniquity, and look at perversity? For destruction and violence are before me. There is strife, and contention rises up. Therefore the law is paralyzed, and justice never goes forth; for the wicked surround the righteous; therefore justice goes forth perverted.', 'Habakkuk is a prophet watching real injustice happen all around him, and he doesn''t hold back — he directly accuses God of not listening and not acting.',
   'Is there something happening in the world, or in your own life, that just seems wrong and doesn''t seem to be getting fixed?', 'Have you ever been genuinely angry at God, not just sad or confused?', 'Habakkuk isn''t punished for this — his complaint became part of Scripture, God''s own Word. What does it say about God that he preserved a prophet''s direct accusation against him, not only the prayers that praised him?',
   'God, we don''t always understand why things stay broken as long as they do. Habakkuk asked you directly, and it''s in your Word — so we''re asking directly too. Amen.', true),
  ('08430829-fb63-54a5-83ec-68e027ff2152', 'Job 1:13-22', 'Suffering', 'It fell on a day when his sons and his daughters were eating and drinking wine in their eldest brother''s house, that there came a messenger to Job, and said, "The oxen were plowing, and the donkeys feeding beside them, and the Sabeans attacked, and took them away. Yes, they have killed the servants with the edge of the sword, and I alone have escaped to tell you." While he was still speaking, there also came another, and said, "The fire of God has fallen from the sky, and has burned up the sheep and the servants, and consumed them, and I alone have escaped to tell you." While he was still speaking, there came also another, and said, "The Chaldeans made three bands, and swept down on the camels, and have taken them away, yes, and killed the servants with the edge of the sword; and I alone have escaped to tell you." While he was still speaking, there came also another, and said, "Your sons and your daughters were eating and drinking wine in their eldest brother''s house, and, behold, there came a great wind from the wilderness, and struck the four corners of the house, and it fell on the young men, and they are dead. I alone have escaped to tell you." Then Job arose, and tore his robe, and shaved his head, and fell down on the ground, and worshiped. He said, "Naked I came out of my mother''s womb, and naked shall I return there. Yahweh gave, and Yahweh has taken away. Blessed be the name of Yahweh." In all this, Job did not sin, nor charge God with wrongdoing.', 'In a single day, Job loses his livestock, his servants, and all ten of his children, one devastating report after another, before he''s had a moment to process any of it.',
   'Has something you were counting on ever fallen apart faster than you could process it?', 'When something falls apart quickly, what''s your first instinct — to talk, to go quiet, to move, to pray, something else entirely?', 'Job says "Yahweh gave, and Yahweh has taken away, blessed be the name of Yahweh" before anyone has offered him a single explanation for why any of it happened — and this isn''t the end of his story; his questions get much harder later. What do you make of the fact that his first response is worship, not because he understands, but before he''s even had time to demand to?',
   'God, we don''t have Job''s words yet, and that''s okay. Whatever we''re carrying tonight that we can''t explain, hold it with us. Amen.', true),
  ('82d0cae3-0fb4-5fb5-9a0c-959a1abf6f93', 'Habakkuk 3:17-19', 'Suffering', 'For though the fig tree doesn''t flourish, Nor fruit be in the vines; The labor of the olive fails, The fields yield no food; The flocks are cut off from the fold, And there is no herd in the stalls: Yet I will rejoice in Yahweh. I will be joyful in the God of my salvation! Yahweh, the Lord, is my strength. He makes my feet like deer''s feet, And enables me to go in high places.', 'This comes after Habakkuk''s hard questions earlier in the book — his circumstances haven''t changed by this point, and he''s not pretending they have. He names every single thing that could go wrong, then chooses something anyway.',
   'What''s something you''d genuinely struggle to be okay with losing?', 'Can you think of a time you chose joy or trust even though your actual circumstances hadn''t improved at all?', 'Habakkuk doesn''t say "I will rejoice because it will get better" — he says it while everything is still failing, named specifically, one thing at a time. What''s the difference between rejoicing because things are fine and rejoicing while naming, out loud, that they''re not?',
   'God, we''re not going to pretend everything''s fine if it isn''t. But like Habakkuk, we choose you as our strength anyway, in the middle of it, not just after. Amen.', true),
  ('f72be009-ff58-5897-9050-9eac0bf48c21', 'Psalm 22:1-2', 'Grief & Loss', 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Why are you so far from helping me, and from the words of my groaning? My God, I cry in the daytime, but you don''t answer; In the night season, and am not silent.', 'These are the exact words Jesus cried out from the cross. David wrote them centuries earlier, and Jesus, in his own worst moment, prayed someone else''s ancient lament as his own.',
   'Have you ever borrowed someone else''s words — a song, a verse, a quote — because you couldn''t find your own in a hard moment?', 'Has there been a time you felt genuinely forsaken, even for a moment?', 'Jesus, who is God, prayed these exact words on the cross. If the Son of God cried out asking why he''d been forsaken, what does that mean about whether feeling forsaken is a failure of faith?',
   'God, Jesus prayed this exact prayer, so we know it''s allowed. If we feel far from you tonight, we''re not the first, and we''re not doing faith wrong by saying so. Amen.', true),
  ('67ba5bd6-12a1-5062-84c1-f8988915af30', 'Romans 8:26-27', 'Suffering', 'In the same way, the Spirit also helps our weaknesses, for we don''t know how to pray as we ought. But the Spirit himself makes intercession for us with groanings which can''t be uttered. He who searches the hearts knows what is on the Spirit''s mind, because he makes intercession for the saints according to God.', 'Paul is honest that even people who genuinely want to pray well don''t always know how — and instead of treating that as a problem, he says the Spirit fills exactly that gap.',
   'Has there been a time you wanted to pray about something but genuinely didn''t know what to say?', 'Is there something right now you don''t have words for, even to God?', 'Paul says the Spirit intercedes with "groanings which can''t be uttered" — not polished words, not even words at all. What does it mean for prayer that its most honest form might not be sentences?',
   'Spirit, take what we can''t put into words tonight, and carry it for us. Amen.', true),
  ('09c10a80-d3e4-5d65-bab6-121d01abdc1f', 'Job 2:11-13', 'Friendship', 'Now when Job''s three friends heard of all this evil that had come on him, they each came from his own place: Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite, and they made an appointment together to come to sympathize with him and to comfort him. When they lifted up their eyes from a distance, and didn''t recognize him, they raised their voices, and wept; and they each tore his robe, and sprinkled dust on their heads toward the sky. So they sat down with him on the ground seven days and seven nights, and none spoke a word to him, for they saw that his grief was very great.', 'When Job''s friends hear what''s happened, they travel to be with him and, for a full week, say nothing at all — they just sit with him in it. Worth knowing, without needing to read it here: a few chapters later, once they finally do start talking, a lot of what they say gets it wrong. This moment, before any of that, they got exactly right.',
   'Has someone ever helped you most just by showing up, without trying to fix anything or say the right thing?', 'Is there someone hurting in your life right now you''ve been avoiding, because you don''t know what to say to them?', 'These friends sit in silence for seven days — an enormous, costly commitment of time and presence, with zero words spoken. Why do you think this moment, not their later explanations, is the one Scripture holds up as them at their best?',
   'God, teach us to be the kind of presence Job''s friends were on their first day — willing to just sit, without needing to fix or explain anything. Amen.', true),
  ('26fe9919-5219-5793-a759-4b02eb09174e', '1 Thessalonians 5:14-15', 'Community', 'We exhort you, brothers, admonish the disorderly, encourage the fainthearted, support the weak, be patient toward all. See that no one returns evil for evil to anyone, but always follow after that which is good, for one another, and for all.', 'Paul gives a short, practical list for how a community should treat different people within it — not one-size-fits-all advice, but specific care matched to what each person actually needs.',
   'Which of these — admonishing, encouraging, supporting, being patient — comes most naturally to you?', 'Is there someone in your life who needs a different kind of care from you than the kind you''re naturally good at giving?', 'Paul doesn''t give one instruction for how to treat everyone — the disorderly need admonishment, the fainthearted need encouragement, the weak need support, and everyone needs patience. What does it cost you to figure out which one someone actually needs, instead of just giving everyone the kind of care that''s easiest for you to give?',
   'God, show us tonight what kind of care the people around us actually need, not just the kind we''re used to giving. Amen.', true),
  ('c5ffa479-ce53-555d-a605-5976b13dcfd3', '2 Corinthians 1:3-4', 'Suffering', 'Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort; who comforts us in all our affliction, that we may be able to comfort those who are in any affliction, through the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God.', 'Paul doesn''t describe comfort as something that just makes you feel better in the moment — he describes it as something with a destination, meant to be passed on to someone else who needs it next.',
   'Who''s comforted you in a way you still remember clearly?', 'Has pain you''ve already been through ever turned into something that helped you comfort someone else going through it now?', 'Paul says God comforts us specifically "that we may be able to comfort" others — the comfort has somewhere to go beyond just us. What changes if you think of your hardest seasons as something being prepared for someone else''s benefit, not only your own?',
   'God, thank you for every bit of comfort we''ve ever received. Show us who needs some of it passed along tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('542940be-4f2f-5764-8608-dfdd8f7f3060', 'John 11:20-27', 'Hope', 'Then when Martha heard that Jesus was coming, she went and met him, but Mary stayed in the house. Therefore Martha said to Jesus, "Lord, if you would have been here, my brother wouldn''t have died. Even now I know that, whatever you ask of God, God will give you." Jesus said to her, "Your brother will rise again." Martha said to him, "I know that he will rise again in the resurrection at the last day." Jesus said to her, "I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me, though he die, yet will he live. Whoever lives and believes in me will never die. Do you believe this?" She said to him, "Yes, Lord. I have come to believe that you are the Christ, God''s Son, he who comes into the world."', 'Martha meets Jesus on the road, before he''s even reached the house, still grieving — and he asks her directly whether she believes this claim about who he is, before anything has actually been fixed.',
   'What''s a promise you''ve held onto, even when you couldn''t fully picture how it would come true?', 'Is there a claim about God you say you believe, but haven''t really tested by actually leaning on it?', 'Jesus asks Martha "do you believe this?" while her brother is still dead in the tomb — her "yes" isn''t a response to a miracle she''s already seen. What does it mean to believe a claim about Jesus before the evidence, rather than after it?',
   'Jesus, you asked Martha to believe before she saw. We''re asking for that same kind of faith tonight — not after everything''s fixed, but now. Amen.', true),
  ('7aa1301c-aa63-5cec-819b-aa6d772bd09c', 'Revelation 21:1-4', 'Hope', 'I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away, and the sea is no more. I saw the holy city, New Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, made ready like a bride adorned for her husband. I heard a loud voice out of heaven saying, "Behold, God''s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God. He will wipe away from them every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away."', 'This is Scripture''s own final answer to grief — not offered as something to rush toward past every present pain, but as a real promise about where the whole story actually ends.',
   'If you could undo one kind of pain from the world entirely, what would it be?', 'Does "no more death, no more mourning, no more pain" feel real to you, or more like a nice idea you haven''t let yourself actually hope for?', 'John doesn''t say God removes us from a broken world — he says God''s own dwelling comes down to be with us, and from inside that closeness, personally wipes every tear. What''s the difference between hope that escapes pain and hope that God enters into it with us until it ends?',
   'God, we hold onto this tonight — that death, mourning, crying, and pain don''t get the last word. Come close now, the way you promise to fully then. Amen.', true),
  ('187b91e8-e53f-5439-85a2-f01b061a35b5', 'Philippians 1:20-24', 'Hope', 'according to my earnest expectation and hope, that I will in no way be disappointed, but with all boldness, as always, now also Christ will be magnified in my body, whether by life, or by death. For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain. But if I live on in the flesh, this will bring fruit from my work; yet I don''t make known what I will choose. But I am in a dilemma between the two, having the desire to depart and be with Christ, which is far better. Yet, to remain in the flesh is more needful for your sake.', 'Paul is writing from prison, genuinely unsure whether he''ll live or die, and he''s honest that part of him would actually prefer to die and be with Christ — but he chooses to hope for more life anyway, for the sake of the people who still need him.',
   'If you had a big, uncertain decision ahead of you, who would you actually want with you while you made it?', 'Is there a "dilemma between the two" in your own life right now — two things pulling at you, both with real weight?', 'Paul says dying would be "far better" for him personally, and still chooses to hope for more life, for other people''s sake. What does it look like to hold a genuine hope for what''s beyond this life and a real commitment to the people still here with you, at the same time?',
   'God, thank you that death isn''t something Paul — or we — have to be afraid of. And thank you for the people who still need us here. Help us hold both without rushing past either one. Amen.', true),
  ('af6ccc8b-9f32-5a87-9223-0f525af148f8', 'Genesis 4:6-7', 'Anger & Conflict', 'Yahweh said to Cain, "Why are you angry? Why has the expression of your face fallen? If you do well, will it not be lifted up? If you don''t do well, sin crouches at the door. Its desire is for you, but you are to rule over it."', 'Cain is angry because his offering wasn''t accepted the way his brother Abel''s was. Before anything else happens — and something worse does happen, just a few verses later — God meets Cain right at the anger itself, and asks him a real question instead of condemning him outright.',
   'What usually sets you off — is it a big thing, or is it small things stacked up?', 'When you feel anger rising, what does "ruling over it" actually look like for you in the moment, not just in theory?', 'God doesn''t tell Cain his anger is the problem — he tells him what he does next is the choice that''s still open. What''s the difference between anger itself and the door it can open?',
   'Lord, you met Cain right at his anger, before anything worse happened. Meet us there too, and help us choose well at the door. Amen.', true),
  ('7da1afdb-0a44-583e-b25c-e92b02f44361', 'Matthew 5:21-22', 'Anger & Conflict', '"You have heard that it was said to the ancient ones, ''You shall not murder;'' and ''Whoever shall murder shall be in danger of the judgment.'' But I tell you, that everyone who is angry with his brother without a cause shall be in danger of the judgment; and whoever shall say to his brother, ''Raca!'' shall be in danger of the council; and whoever shall say, ''You fool!'' shall be in danger of the fire of Gehenna.', 'Jesus isn''t adding a new law here — he''s showing that the command against murder was never only about the final act. He traces the same danger back to contempt: the anger and the insults that come long before anyone would call it violence. "Raca" was an insult in Jesus''s own language meaning something like "empty-headed" or "worthless." "Gehenna" was a real valley outside Jerusalem, used by Jesus elsewhere as an image of judgment — both words name the way contempt escalates toward writing someone off entirely.',
   'What''s an insult, even a small one, that''s stuck with you longer than you expected it to?', 'Is there a difference, in your own life, between how you talk about someone and how you''d talk to them directly?', 'Jesus puts unresolved contempt in the same sentence as murder — not the same act, but the same root. What does that suggest about why he cared so much about how we talk about each other, not just what we do to each other?',
   'Jesus, you saw what''s underneath the anger, not just the act itself. Search us there tonight, in the words we''d never call violence. Amen.', true),
  ('66b5e0fa-beb7-5626-8119-d61cb5e443fe', 'Ephesians 4:26-27', 'Anger & Conflict', '"Be angry, and don''t sin." Don''t let the sun go down on your wrath, neither give place to the devil.', 'Paul doesn''t tell believers not to be angry — he assumes they will be, sometimes rightly. What he warns against is what anger becomes when it''s left to sit past its own day.',
   'Have you ever gone to bed angry at someone and woken up feeling completely different about it — better or worse?', 'What usually happens when you sit on anger instead of dealing with it — does it fade, or does it grow?', 'Paul separates "be angry" from "don''t sin" — as if they''re not automatically the same thing. What''s the actual line between them, in your own experience?',
   'Father, we''re not asking to never feel this. Just help us not let it outlast its own day. Amen.', true),
  ('ff1ff682-39c7-56c1-940c-37eba1868de6', 'Proverbs 29:11', 'Anger & Conflict', 'A fool vents all of his anger, But a wise man brings himself under control.', 'This is a short, plain proverb — not a claim that wisdom means never feeling anger, but that wisdom shows in what you do with all of it in the moment.',
   'Do you tend to say everything you''re feeling in the moment, or hold most of it back?', 'Has "venting all of it" ever cost you something afterward that you wish you''d kept back?', 'The proverb doesn''t say a wise person has no anger — it says a wise person doesn''t let all of it out at once. What''s worth saying in the moment, and what''s worth waiting on?',
   'God, give us the kind of self-control this proverb is talking about — not silence, but wisdom about what needs to be said now and what can wait. Amen.', true),
  ('d13cd35c-34f9-5273-b00d-e708c352e9cb', 'Proverbs 18:13', 'Anger & Conflict', 'He who gives answer before he hears, That is folly and shame to him.', 'A one-line proverb about a habit almost everyone recognizes — jumping to a response before actually taking in what the other person said.',
   'Are you someone who waits to hear the whole thing, or someone who''s already forming your answer halfway through?', 'Think of a recent disagreement — how much of it did you actually hear, and how much were you just waiting to respond to?', 'This proverb calls answering too early "folly and shame" — pretty strong language for something that just feels like normal conversation. Why might genuinely hearing someone out be harder, and more important, than it sounds?',
   'Lord, slow us down enough to actually hear each other tonight and every night, before we''re already forming our answer. Amen.', true),
  ('44cb359c-307c-5769-89e8-83cb0c646bdb', 'James 3:5-10', 'Anger & Conflict', 'So the tongue is also a little member, and boasts great things. See how a small fire can spread to a large forest! And the tongue is a fire. The world of iniquity among our members is the tongue, which defiles the whole body, and sets on fire the course of nature. For every kind of animal, bird, creeping thing, and thing in the sea, is tamed, and has been tamed by mankind. But nobody can tame the tongue. It is a restless evil, full of deadly poison. With it we bless our God and Father, and with it we curse men, who are made in the image of God. Out of the same mouth comes forth blessing and cursing. My brothers, these things ought not to be so.', 'James isn''t talking about strangers here — the tension he names is that the very same mouth that prays and worships can turn around and tear someone down, sometimes in the same day, sometimes at the same table.',
   'What''s a tone of voice — sarcastic, sharp, dismissive — that you know gets under someone else''s skin?', 'Is there a way you talk to the people closest to you that you''d never talk to anyone else?', 'James says taming animals is easier than taming the tongue. What makes the words we say to the people we love the hardest ones to actually control?',
   'God, we don''t want the same mouth that praises you to be the one that wounds the people we love. Tame our tongues tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('f469e2e6-67b1-50fc-83e2-722dd6e67531', 'Proverbs 17:14', 'Anger & Conflict', 'The beginning of strife is like breaching a dam, Therefore stop contention before quarreling breaks out.', 'The image is simple and physical — once a dam breaks, the water doesn''t stop where you want it to. The proverb''s advice is about timing: deal with it before the break, not after.',
   'Can you think of a small disagreement that turned into something much bigger than it needed to be?', 'Is there a moment early in an argument where you can usually feel it about to "breach the dam" — and what do you normally do right then?', 'This proverb doesn''t say avoid all conflict — it says catch it early. What would it look like, in a real disagreement, to stop it "before quarreling breaks out" instead of after?',
   'Father, help us notice the moment before things break, not just clean up after they do. Amen.', true),
  ('f0d58053-a98d-5442-b404-a5d9179f8323', 'Matthew 18:15-17', 'Anger & Conflict', '"If your brother sins against you, go, show him his fault between you and him alone. If he listens to you, you have gained back your brother. But if he doesn''t listen, take one or two more with you, that at the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established. If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the assembly. If he refuses to hear the assembly also, let him be to you as a Gentile or a tax collector.', 'Jesus gives a specific order here — go to the person first, privately, before involving anyone else. Each further step only happens if the one before it didn''t work.',
   'When you''re upset with someone, is your first instinct to talk to them directly, or to talk to someone else about it first?', 'What makes going straight to the person harder than talking about them to someone else instead?', 'Jesus starts with "between you and him alone" — not a group text, not mentioning it to someone else first. What does that first step cost that the easier version doesn''t?',
   'Jesus, going directly to someone is harder than talking around them. Give us the courage to go first to the person, not last. Amen.', true),
  ('cfb1930d-1799-5b12-a298-4b38f2a14ace', 'Philippians 4:2-3', 'Anger & Conflict', 'I exhort Euodia, and I exhort Syntyche, to think the same way in the Lord. Yes, I beg you also, true partner, help these women, for they labored with me in the Gospel, with Clement also, and the rest of my fellow workers, whose names are in the book of life.', 'Euodia and Syntyche were real leaders in the Philippian church, and whatever their disagreement was, Paul cared enough about it to name them both, directly, in a letter meant to be read aloud to the whole church.',
   'Has a disagreement between two people you respect ever surprised you?', 'Is there a conflict you know about, between other people, that you''ve stayed out of but wondered whether you should help with?', 'Paul doesn''t take a side between Euodia and Syntyche — he asks someone else to help them, and asks both of them to agree "in the Lord." What does it look like to care about a conflict''s resolution without needing to decide who was right?',
   'Lord, even people who genuinely love you can still disagree. Help us be the kind of help Paul was asking for, not one more side. Amen.', true),
  ('322bbad9-c6a9-55fb-99e2-96836a6ac5af', 'Acts 15:36-39', 'Anger & Conflict', 'After some days Paul said to Barnabas, "Let''s return now and visit our brothers in every city in which we proclaimed the word of the Lord, to see how they are doing." Barnabas planned to take John, who was called Mark, with them also. But Paul didn''t think that it was a good idea to take with them someone who had withdrawn from them in Pamphylia, and didn''t go with them to do the work. Then the contention grew so sharp that they separated from each other. Barnabas took Mark with him, and sailed away to Cyprus,', 'Paul and Barnabas were close partners in ministry, and this disagreement was sharp enough that Scripture uses the word "contention" and tells us plainly that they parted ways because of it. The text doesn''t say which one of them was right.',
   'Have you ever disagreed with someone you genuinely respect, and neither of you backed down?', 'Is there a relationship in your life that changed shape after a disagreement, even if it didn''t end?', 'Scripture records this falling-out without telling us who was right, and without erasing either man''s importance to the story that follows. What does it mean that two good people, both trying to do the right thing, can still end up on opposite sides?',
   'Father, not every disagreement gets resolved the way we''d want. Help us trust you with the ones that don''t. Amen.', true),
  ('c2cff955-e6de-5ccd-b955-9b609215d570', 'Romans 12:18', 'Anger & Conflict', 'If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.', 'Paul''s wording is careful — "if it is possible," "as much as it is up to you." He''s not commanding a guaranteed outcome, only the part that''s actually yours to give.',
   'Is there a relationship where you''ve done what you could, but peace still hasn''t come?', 'What''s the difference between giving up on peace and accepting that it isn''t only up to you?', 'Paul doesn''t promise every relationship will end at peace — he only asks for your part of it. What does that free you from, and what does it still ask of you?',
   'God, we can''t make peace happen alone. Help us do our part honestly, and trust you with the rest. Amen.', true),
  ('3f323581-ef8a-5783-838f-62afc9c7a4ff', 'Romans 12:19-21', 'Anger & Conflict', 'Don''t seek revenge yourselves, beloved, but give place to God''s wrath. For it is written, "Vengeance belongs to me; I will repay, says the Lord." Therefore "If your enemy is hungry, feed him. If he is thirsty, give him a drink. For in doing so, you will heap coals of fire on his head." Don''t be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.', 'Paul isn''t telling his readers that what was done to them didn''t matter — he''s telling them who gets to settle it, and it isn''t them.',
   'Have you ever wanted to get back at someone, even just a little, for something they did to you?', 'What''s a small, practical way you could respond to someone difficult with actual good instead of matching them?', 'Paul says vengeance belongs to God, not to us — not because the wrong doesn''t matter, but because settling it isn''t our job. What does it cost to actually let that be true?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to be overcome by whatever''s been done to us. Help us answer it with good, and leave the settling to you. Amen.', true),
  ('5232ae34-62d3-57f5-9ce8-e5509f4f8a23', 'Leviticus 19:17-18', 'Anger & Conflict', '"You shall not hate your brother in your heart. You shall surely rebuke your neighbor, and not bear sin because of him. You shall not take vengeance, nor bear any grudge against the children of your people; but you shall love your neighbor as yourself. I am Yahweh.', 'This command pairs two things people usually treat as opposites: don''t hate silently, and don''t take revenge — instead, say the hard thing directly. Holding it in and lashing out are treated here as two versions of the same failure.',
   'Do you tend to say what''s bothering you, or hold it in until it comes out some other way?', 'Is there a grudge you''ve been carrying quietly instead of actually addressing?', 'This command treats silent hatred and open vengeance as connected, not opposite problems — and puts "rebuke your neighbor" in between them as the alternative to both. Why might honest, direct confrontation be the harder, healthier option compared to either one?',
   'Father, we don''t want to carry grudges quietly or get even loudly. Teach us the harder, honest way in between. Amen.', true),
  ('ab67ce45-6606-5ae8-9b79-3fd4dbd82477', 'Psalm 32:3-5', 'Forgiveness', 'When I kept silence, my bones wasted away through my groaning all day long. For day and night your hand was heavy on me. My strength was sapped in the heat of summer. I acknowledged my sin to you. I didn''t hide my iniquity. I said, I will confess my transgressions to Yahweh, And you forgave the iniquity of my sin.', 'David describes what it physically felt like to carry something unconfessed, and then what changed the moment he stopped hiding it and just said it plainly.',
   'Have you ever noticed that not admitting something actually takes more energy than just saying it?', 'Is there something you''ve been carrying quietly that''s wearing on you more than you''ve let on?', 'David describes real physical weight from staying silent, and real relief the moment he confessed. What is it about naming something honestly, instead of managing it privately, that actually changes something?',
   'God, we carry things quietly that we don''t need to carry alone. Give us the courage David found to just say it. Amen.', true),
  ('bf146183-4229-5b45-91ea-d5b96cac9b02', '2 Samuel 12:1-9, 13', 'Forgiveness', 'Yahweh sent Nathan to David. He came to him, and said to him, "There were two men in one city; the one rich, and the other poor. The rich man had very many flocks and herds, but the poor man had nothing, except one little ewe lamb, which he had bought and raised. It grew up together with him, and with his children. It ate of his own food, drank of his own cup, and lay in his bosom, and was to him like a daughter. A traveler came to the rich man, and he spared to take of his own flock and of his own herd, to dress for the wayfaring man who had come to him, but took the poor man''s lamb, and dressed it for the man who had come to him." David''s anger was greatly kindled against the man, and he said to Nathan, "As Yahweh lives, the man who has done this is worthy to die! He shall restore the lamb fourfold, because he did this thing, and because he had no pity!" Nathan said to David, "You are the man. This is what Yahweh, the God of Israel, says: ''I anointed you king over Israel, and I delivered you out of the hand of Saul. I gave you your master''s house, and your master''s wives into your bosom, and gave you the house of Israel and of Judah; and if that would have been too little, I would have added to you many more such things. Why have you despised the word of Yahweh, to do that which is evil in his sight? You have struck Uriah the Hittite with the sword, and have taken his wife to be your wife, and have slain him with the sword of the children of Ammon." David said to Nathan, "I have sinned against Yahweh." Nathan said to David, "Yahweh also has put away your sin. You will not die.', 'Nathan doesn''t accuse David directly at first — he tells a story that lets David''s own sense of justice condemn the wrongdoer, before revealing that the story was about David himself. David''s response is immediate and unguarded: no excuse, no defense, just "I have sinned."',
   'Has anyone ever confronted you in a way that actually helped, rather than just making you defensive?', 'When you''ve done something wrong, is your first instinct to explain it, or to just own it the way David does here?', 'David gets angrier at the story than he ever gets at himself — until Nathan says four words. What does it take to see clearly in ourselves what we can spot instantly in someone else?',
   'God, give us Nathan''s courage to speak the truth, and David''s honesty to actually hear it. Amen.', true),
  ('9f18f969-64f8-5ac1-a169-80094e9a1761', 'Matthew 5:23-24', 'Forgiveness', '"If therefore you are offering your gift at the altar, and there remember that your brother has anything against you, leave your gift there before the altar, and go your way. First be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift.', 'Jesus is describing someone in the middle of worship — already at the altar — who remembers a relationship that needs attention, and tells them to stop and go deal with it first.',
   'Who might be owed an apology from you, rather than the other way around?', 'What usually stops you from apologizing first, even when you know you should?', 'Jesus doesn''t say wait until after you''ve worshiped to go make it right — he says stop what you''re doing and go now. What does that order say about how seriously God takes what''s broken between us and other people?',
   'God, show us who we owe an apology to, and give us the courage to go make it right before we bring you anything else. Amen.', true),
  ('e2c02f5f-e2e6-5778-9c34-deef15e4e12f', 'Matthew 18:21-35', 'Forgiveness', 'Then Peter came and said to him, "Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? Until seven times?" Jesus said to him, "I don''t tell you until seven times, but, until seventy times seven. Therefore the Kingdom of Heaven is like a certain king, who wanted to reconcile accounts with his servants. When he had begun to reconcile, one was brought to him who owed him ten thousand talents. But because he couldn''t pay, his lord commanded him to be sold, with his wife, his children, and all that he had, and payment to be made. The servant therefore fell down and kneeled before him, saying, ''Lord, have patience with me, and I will repay you all!'' The lord of that servant, being moved with compassion, released him, and forgave him the debt. "But that servant went out, and found one of his fellow servants, who owed him one hundred denarii, and he grabbed him, and took him by the throat, saying, ''Pay me what you owe!'' "So his fellow servant fell down at his feet and begged him, saying, ''Have patience with me, and I will repay you!'' He would not, but went and cast him into prison, until he should pay back that which was due. So when his fellow servants saw what was done, they were exceedingly sorry, and came and told to their lord all that was done. Then his lord called him in, and said to him, ''You wicked servant! I forgave you all that debt, because you begged me. Shouldn''t you also have had mercy on your fellow servant, even as I had mercy on you?'' His lord was angry, and delivered him to the tormentors, until he should pay all that was due to him. So my heavenly Father will also do to you, if you don''t each forgive your brother from your hearts for his misdeeds."', 'Ten thousand talents was a debt no servant could ever realistically repay — the amount is deliberately, almost comically enormous, set right next to the second servant''s very ordinary, repayable debt. Peter''s question about counting to seven led Jesus somewhere Peter probably wasn''t expecting.',
   'Have you ever been forgiven for something and then turned around and been unusually hard on someone else for something small?', 'Is there someone you''re keeping a tally on — counting how many times they''ve let you down?', 'The first debt is impossible to repay; the second is small enough to actually pay back. Why do you think Jesus made the gap between the two debts so extreme?',
   'Jesus, we''ve been forgiven more than we can repay. Help that shape how we treat the much smaller debts owed to us. Amen.', true),
  ('5687a34f-91b7-5205-a113-73f60d798be7', '2 Corinthians 5:18-19', 'Forgiveness', 'But all things are of God, who reconciled us to himself through Jesus Christ, and gave to us the ministry of reconciliation; namely, that God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself, not reckoning to them their trespasses, and having committed to us the word of reconciliation.', 'Paul is describing something bigger than any single relationship here — God reconciling an entire world to himself through Christ, and then handing that same work to ordinary people to carry forward.',
   'Where have you seen someone actually help two other people find their way back to each other?', 'Is reconciliation something you think of as your job, or as something that just happens or doesn''t?', 'Paul calls this "the ministry of reconciliation" — an actual assignment, not just a nice outcome when it happens. What changes if you start treating reconciliation as your job, not just something you hope works out on its own?',
   'God, you reconciled us to yourself first. Give us a part in that same work between the people around us. Amen.', true),
  ('8f8fc5d6-ce08-555f-9f88-4d69f6b8b6e4', 'Philemon 1:15-18', 'Forgiveness', 'For perhaps he was therefore separated from you for a while, that you would have him forever, no longer as a slave, but more than a slave, a beloved brother, especially to me, but how much rather to you, both in the flesh and in the Lord. If then you count me a partner, receive him as you would receive me. But if he has wronged you at all, or owes you anything, put that to my account.', 'Paul is writing to Philemon on behalf of Onesimus, a man who wronged him, asking Philemon to receive him back — not as an order Paul forces, but as a personal appeal, offering to personally cover whatever is owed.',
   'Has anyone ever vouched for you or stepped in to help repair a relationship you''d damaged?', 'Is there a broken relationship in your life where you''d genuinely welcome someone else''s help repairing it?', 'Paul asks, he doesn''t command — and he offers to personally absorb the cost of what''s owed rather than requiring Philemon to just let it go. What''s the difference between asking someone to forgive a debt and offering to pay it yourself?',
   'Jesus, some of us have needed someone to step in and help repair what we couldn''t fix alone. Make us that kind of help for someone else. Amen.', true),
  ('9d03a6a7-8c10-5fa3-a984-d8871c28ba12', '2 Timothy 4:14-16', 'Forgiveness', 'Alexander, the coppersmith, did much evil to me. The Lord will repay him according to his works, of whom you also must beware; for he greatly opposed our words. At my first defense, no one came to help me, but all left me. May it not be held against them.', 'Paul names a specific person who genuinely wronged him, and doesn''t pretend that relationship is fine — he warns Timothy to be careful of the same man. In the very next breath, he also releases the friends who abandoned him at his lowest moment, without requiring anything from them first.',
   'Is there a relationship in your life you''ve had to accept won''t be repaired, at least not right now?', 'What''s the difference, for you, between forgiving someone and deciding you still need to be careful around them?', 'Paul does two different things in the same breath here — he warns Timothy about someone who genuinely harmed him, and he releases people who let him down, without either one requiring the relationship to go back to how it was. Why might warning someone and releasing someone both be honest responses, instead of contradictory ones?',
   'Lord, some relationships aren''t repaired, not yet and maybe not ever. Help us release what we''re carrying without pretending it''s settled when it isn''t. Amen.', true),
  ('ad7aeec2-a64e-5107-b89f-3db9fe863ac9', 'Matthew 4:1-4', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Then Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. When he had fasted forty days and forty nights, he was hungry afterward. The tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread." But he answered, "It is written, ''Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds out of the mouth of God.''"', 'Jesus is at his most physically vulnerable here — forty days without food — when the temptation comes. Scripture doesn''t treat being tempted as something shameful for him. It treats how he responds as what matters.',
   'What''s a moment you were tempted by something small — a snack you said you wouldn''t eat, a show you said you''d stop watching — and what actually happened?', 'Does temptation usually hit you hardest when you''re tired, hungry, stressed, or alone — or does it not follow a pattern for you?', 'Jesus was genuinely tempted, and Scripture doesn''t treat that as a flaw in him. What changes if you stop treating the temptation itself as the problem, and start paying attention only to what you do next?',
   'God, thank you that Jesus knows what it''s like to be tempted. Help us tell the truth about where we feel it most, without any shame in admitting it. Amen.', true),
  ('a89df63b-41e3-5937-9827-b9b234c40af8', 'Ephesians 5:15-16', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Therefore watch carefully how you walk, not as unwise, but as wise; redeeming the time, because the days are evil.', 'Paul''s instruction here is about ordinary daily attention — being intentional about time — not a single dramatic decision.',
   'What''s the first thing you reach for in the morning, and the last thing you check at night?', 'Where does a chunk of your day usually disappear to, if you''re honest about tracking it?', 'Paul calls this "redeeming" the time, like it''s something that can be lost or bought back. What''s one thing in your day that quietly costs you more time than you''d ever plan to give it?',
   'Lord, help us notice where our time actually goes, and use it wisely instead of just letting it slip by. Amen.', true),
  ('150e4e3b-84b2-5023-bb04-427f79ce4fc8', 'James 1:13-15', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Let no man say when he is tempted, "I am tempted by God," for God can''t be tempted by evil, and he himself tempts no one. But each one is tempted, when he is drawn away by his own lust, and enticed. Then the lust, when it has conceived, bears sin; and the sin, when it is full grown, brings forth death.', 'James is careful to say temptation doesn''t come from God — and then traces a specific process: being drawn away, then desire taking hold, then it growing into something else entirely.',
   'Can you think of a time something small you wanted turned into a much bigger decision than you meant to make?', 'Where in that process — being drawn, wanting it, acting on it — do you usually still have the most power to stop?', 'James describes temptation growing in stages, not happening all at once. At which point in that process do you think a person most often still believes they could stop, right before they don''t?',
   'Father, help us notice the small pull before it grows into something we can''t easily undo. Amen.', true),
  ('fbaf4b85-0daf-5646-8c5e-a88458f7b260', 'Proverbs 25:28', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Like a city that is broken down and without walls Is a man whose spirit is without restraint.', 'In the ancient world, a city''s walls were its protection — without them, anything could get in. The proverb uses that image for a person with no self-restraint.',
   'What''s an area of your life where you feel like you have strong "walls," and one where you feel like you don''t have any?', 'What boundary have you been putting off, even though you already know you need it?', 'This proverb doesn''t describe self-control as something that restricts you — it describes the lack of it as something that leaves you exposed. What''s the difference between a wall that protects you and a wall that just controls you?',
   'Father, build up whatever walls are broken down in us, not to trap us, but to protect what matters. Amen.', true),
  ('6fc20d5c-caf2-5a28-b92e-a3110dd82bca', 'Philippians 2:14', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Do all things without murmurings and disputes,', 'A short, plain instruction, part of a longer list of how Paul wanted the Philippians to live with each other day to day.',
   'What''s something small you find yourself complaining about almost automatically?', 'Does complaining about something ever become a habit you keep going even after the actual problem is gone?', 'Paul doesn''t just say "don''t complain about big things" — he says "all things." What would change in a normal day if grumbling wasn''t your default response to annoyance?',
   'God, catch us in the small complaints before they become a habit we don''t even notice anymore. Amen.', true),
  ('997cb780-b3ad-507a-8a23-d4421a5e12fe', 'Genesis 39:9-12', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'He isn''t greater in this house than I, neither has he kept back anything from me but you, because you are his wife. How then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?" It happened that as she spoke to Joseph day by day, that he didn''t listen to her, to lie by her, or to be with her. It happened about this time, that he went into the house to do his work, and there were none of the men of the house inside. She caught him by his garment, saying, "Lie with me!" He left his garment in her hand, and ran outside.', 'Joseph doesn''t stay to reason with the situation once it becomes physical — he leaves his garment behind and runs, even though it costs him something (the coat becomes evidence later used against him).',
   'When is leaving immediately a better plan than trying to think your way through something in the moment?', 'What''s something you know you should walk away from faster than you actually do?', 'Joseph doesn''t win an argument here — he loses his coat and runs. Why might running, and accepting whatever it costs you, sometimes be the stronger response than staying to prove you can resist?',
   'Jesus, give us Joseph''s readiness to run, even when staying feels like it would prove something. Amen.', true),
  ('9199625f-f5bd-53fb-9f10-90a653264dba', 'Hebrews 4:15', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'For we don''t have a high priest who can''t be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but one who has been in all points tempted like we are, yet without sin.', 'This verse is describing Jesus in his role interceding for us — someone who actually understands temptation from the inside, not someone evaluating it from a safe distance.',
   'Is it easier to be honest with someone who''s been through something similar to what you''re facing, or someone who hasn''t?', 'If Jesus genuinely understands what you''re tempted by, does that change how honest you''d be with him about it?', 'This verse holds two things together — Jesus was tempted "in all points," and he was "without sin." What does it mean that understanding your struggle and never having given in to it aren''t opposites in him?',
   'Jesus, you understand this better than anyone else could. Help us bring it to you honestly instead of hiding it from you. Amen.', true),
  ('c90bf92b-c0d1-5f7b-8fc1-c4809b7f0b72', 'Romans 7:15, 18-19, 24-25', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'For I don''t know what I am doing. For I don''t practice what I desire to do; but what I hate, that I do. For I know that in me, that is, in my flesh, dwells no good thing. For desire is present with me, but I don''t find it doing that which is good. For the good which I desire, I don''t do; but the evil which I don''t desire, that I practice. What a wretched man I am! Who will deliver me out of the body of this death? I thank God through Jesus Christ, our Lord!', 'This is Paul, the same person who wrote much of the New Testament, describing the exact experience of doing the thing he doesn''t want to do, over and over. He doesn''t end there — but he doesn''t rush past the honesty either.',
   'Have you ever done something you genuinely didn''t want to do, right after telling yourself you wouldn''t?', 'Does naming that pattern out loud, the way Paul does here, feel like it would help or feel too exposing?', 'Paul spends several verses on the wretchedness of the pattern before he ever gets to "I thank God." What does it cost to actually sit in the honest part before jumping to the hopeful part?',
   'Lord, we know this pattern Paul is describing. Thank you that the answer isn''t pretending it away, but you. Amen.', true),
  ('a0018873-35cf-5dff-ae12-5670c285efea', '1 Corinthians 6:12', 'Recovery & Freedom', '"All things are lawful for me," but not all things are expedient. "All things are lawful for me," but I will not be brought under the power of anything.', 'Paul is quoting a phrase the Corinthians used to justify their choices — "all things are lawful" — and adding a second test that matters more than whether something is technically permitted.',
   'What''s something that''s completely fine in general, but not fine for you specifically, because of how it affects you?', 'Think of something you''d defend as "not against the rules" — does it, if you''re honest, have more control over you than you''d like?', 'Paul doesn''t ask whether something is allowed — he asks whether it has power over him. Of those two questions, which one do you actually reach for first in real life?',
   'Lord, show us what has more control over us than we''ve been willing to admit, even things that aren''t wrong in themselves. Amen.', true),
  ('edd55185-0d24-5d5c-8a7a-a2d20c47a2ef', 'Galatians 5:1', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Stand firm therefore in the liberty by which Christ has made us free, and don''t be entangled again with a yoke of bondage.', 'Paul is writing to people who had already experienced freedom and were being pulled back toward the old way of living — the warning only makes sense because going back is a real possibility, not a hypothetical one.',
   'Have you ever gone back to an old habit you thought you''d already left behind?', 'What usually pulls you back toward something you know isn''t good for you — stress, boredom, a specific person or place, something else?', 'Paul assumes the readers could genuinely go back to bondage after real freedom — he doesn''t treat that as impossible or as proof the freedom wasn''t real. If going back doesn''t erase the freedom that came before it, what does that change about how you''d respond to your own relapse?',
   'Father, if we''ve gone back to something we thought we''d left, don''t let shame keep us there. Help us stand firm again, today. Amen.', true),
  ('db2d53e3-786b-59a2-9603-fa071f8ba159', '1 Corinthians 9:24-27', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Don''t you know that those who run in a race all run, but one receives the prize? Run like that, that you may win. Every man who strives in the games exercises self-control in all things. Now they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible. I therefore run like that, as not uncertainly. I fight like that, as not beating the air, but I beat my body and bring it into submission, lest by any means, after I have preached to others, I myself should be rejected.', 'Paul uses the image of an athlete in training — someone who practices self-control constantly, as a discipline, not as a single act of willpower in a crisis moment.',
   'What''s something you''ve actually gotten better at through practice and repetition, not just deciding to be good at it?', 'Which area of your life are you treating as a one-time decision that might actually need ongoing training instead?', 'Paul compares this to an athlete training constantly, not just showing up on race day and trying hard. What would it look like to treat self-control as a practice you build, rather than a test you pass or fail once?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to just white-knuckle our way through hard moments. Teach us to train, the way an athlete trains, for the long run. Amen.', true),
  ('86e74d0f-9ad7-5400-b488-0c82a45453fa', 'Philippians 4:8', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Finally, brothers, whatever things are true, whatever things are honorable, whatever things are just, whatever things are pure, whatever things are lovely, whatever things are of good report; if there is any virtue, and if there is any praise, think about these things.', 'Paul isn''t only telling his readers what to avoid — he''s telling them what to deliberately fill their minds with instead.',
   'What''s something good you could think about or do that you almost never make time for?', 'When you''re trying to stop doing something, do you usually have something better ready to replace it with, or just an empty space?', 'Paul gives a whole list of things to think about on purpose — not just a warning about what to avoid. In your own experience, what usually happens when you only focus on stopping something without having anything ready to replace it?',
   'Father, don''t just take things away from us — show us what to fill the space with instead. Amen.', true),
  ('ffd3c52d-f97e-5fb7-9066-88d3712fb417', 'Galatians 6:7-8', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Don''t be deceived. God is not mocked, for whatever a man sows, that will he also reap. For he who sows to his own flesh will from the flesh reap corruption. But he who sows to the Spirit will from the Spirit reap eternal life.', 'Paul states this plainly, as a warning against self-deception — the idea that repeated choices don''t actually lead anywhere in particular.',
   'What''s a small habit, good or bad, that you can already see is heading somewhere?', 'What are you already sowing, right now, that you haven''t wanted to admit out loud?', 'Paul isn''t describing God stepping in to punish here — he''s describing how sowing and reaping simply work. Does thinking of it that way make the warning easier to shrug off, or harder to actually ignore?',
   'Lord, help us be honest about what we''re sowing, and give us the courage to plant something different starting today. Amen.', true),
  ('cf37ba49-942c-5905-9306-f34bac1f3b7d', 'Luke 15:11-16', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'He said, "A certain man had two sons. The younger of them said to his father, ''Father, give me my share of your property.'' He divided his livelihood between them. Not many days after, the younger son gathered all of this together and traveled into a far country. There he wasted his property with riotous living. When he had spent all of it, there arose a severe famine in that country, and he began to be in need. He went and joined himself to one of the citizens of that country, and he sent him into his fields to feed pigs. He wanted to fill his belly with the husks that the pigs ate, but no one gave him any.', 'This is only the first half of a story most people know the ending to. Read on its own, without jumping ahead to the return home, it''s an honest picture of how a good start can slide into real need.',
   'Has a choice that felt exciting at first ever turned out to cost a lot more than you expected?', 'Where''s your own "far country" — a place you went looking for something good and ended up further from it instead?', 'The story doesn''t rush from the request for money to the pigsty — it shows the actual progression. Why might it matter to see the slide happening in stages, instead of jumping straight to the lesson at the end?',
   'God, if we''re partway down a road we didn''t mean to travel this far on, help us see it clearly tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('118b9c51-36ac-5ef6-8338-297ab4a20ec7', 'Psalm 40:1-3', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'I waited patiently for Yahweh. He turned to me, and heard my cry. He brought me up also out of a horrible pit, Out of the miry clay. He set my feet on a rock, And gave me a firm place to stand. He has put a new song in my mouth, even praise to our God. Many shall see it, and fear, and shall trust in Yahweh.', 'The psalmist doesn''t describe rescue as instant — he starts with "I waited patiently," and only then describes being lifted out.',
   'Has there been a time you had to wait a long time for something to actually get better?', 'What "pit" are you still waiting to be lifted out of?', 'The psalm doesn''t skip the waiting to get to the rescue — both are named. What do you make of the fact that this song of praise begins with patience, not with the rescue itself?',
   'Lord, we''re still waiting on some things. Give us patience while we wait, and hold our feet steady wherever we''re standing right now. Amen.', true),
  ('050d73c9-8e02-5ae7-99aa-0d215bef8605', 'Isaiah 61:1', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'The Spirit of the Lord Yahweh is on me; because Yahweh has anointed me to preach good news to the humble; he has sent me to bind up the broken-hearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to those who are bound;', 'Centuries after Isaiah wrote this, Jesus stood up in a synagogue, read this exact passage, and said it was being fulfilled in himself that day. It''s a description of what he came to do, not just a general promise of relief.',
   'What''s a word you''d use for what real freedom would feel like in an area where you feel "bound" right now?', 'Do you actually believe this liberty is available to you specifically, or does it feel like it''s meant for other people''s struggles, not yours?', 'Jesus applied this passage directly to himself and his own mission. What does it mean that setting captives free isn''t just something God does, but something Jesus specifically said he came to do?',
   'Jesus, you said this was your mission. We''re asking for that liberty tonight, wherever we feel bound. Amen.', true),
  ('8d9fde7f-b171-5da8-bf69-15f476a2d9b5', 'Micah 7:8-9', 'Redemption', 'Don''t rejoice against me, my enemy. When I fall, I will arise. When I sit in darkness, Yahweh will be a light to me. I will bear the indignation of Yahweh, Because I have sinned against him, Until he pleads my case, and executes judgment for me. He will bring me forth to the light. I will see his righteousness.', 'The speaker doesn''t minimize what''s happened — he names his own sin plainly — but he also states, just as plainly, that falling isn''t the end of the story.',
   'When you fail at something you were trying to do better, what''s your instinct right after — hide it, explain it, or just get back up?', 'When has a fall in your own story felt more final to you than Scripture treats falling here?', 'The speaker says "when I fall, I will arise" — not "if." Does planning for the real possibility of falling again make you more likely to fall, or more ready to get back up quickly when you do?',
   'Jesus, when we fall — and we will — help us get back up like this, honest about what happened and confident it isn''t the end. Amen.', true),
  ('d6fbc134-2022-5bee-b16d-dda72ec7e316', 'Micah 7:18-19', 'Redemption', 'Who is a God like you, who pardons iniquity, And passes over the disobedience of the remnant of his heritage? He doesn''t retain his anger forever, Because he delights in loving kindness. He will again have compassion on us. He will tread our iniquities under foot; And you will cast all their sins into the depths of the sea.', 'This comes right after the same prophet''s picture of falling and rising again — the same book turns from "when I fall" to a description of God actually delighting in showing mercy, not just tolerating the need for it.',
   'When has someone let something go with you completely, instead of just not bringing it up anymore?', 'What are you still carrying that you believe, in your head, has already been forgiven?', 'The image here isn''t just forgiveness — it''s sin being thrown into the sea, gone. What''s the difference between believing you''re forgiven and actually believing it''s gone?',
   'God, you don''t just tolerate forgiving us — you delight in it. Help that be more real to us than the shame we''re used to carrying. Amen.', true),
  ('7be65ad1-0457-594c-b654-5f84db878dec', 'Matthew 5:29-30', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'If your right eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away from you. For it is more profitable for you that one of your members should perish, than for your whole body to be cast into Gehenna. If your right hand causes you to stumble, cut it off, and throw it away from you: for it is profitable for you that one of your members should perish, and not your whole body be thrown into Gehenna.', 'Jesus is using deliberate, extreme exaggeration here — no one in his audience would have taken "pluck it out" as a literal instruction, and neither should we. The point is the seriousness of removing whatever specifically causes you to stumble, not a command to injure yourself.',
   'Name something small and specific — an app, a place, a time of day — that reliably leads you somewhere you don''t want to go.', 'What would it actually look like to remove that specific access point from your life, rather than just resolving to resist it next time?', 'Jesus doesn''t say "try harder next time you see it" — he says get rid of it, as drastically as it takes. Why might removing access to a temptation be more realistic than counting on willpower alone in the moment?',
   'Father, show us what specifically needs to go, and give us the courage to actually remove it instead of just resolving to resist it. Amen.', true),
  ('7986d43c-ff65-5db6-a2d4-3f335315a246', 'James 5:16', 'Community', 'Confess your offenses to one another, and pray one for another, that you may be healed. The effective, earnest prayer of a righteous man is powerfully effective.', 'James doesn''t say confess only to God privately — he specifically says confess "to one another," among people, as part of what leads to healing.',
   'Is it easier for you to keep a struggle private, or to tell someone else about it?', 'Who in your life would you trust enough to actually be honest with about something you''re working through?', 'James connects confession to other people directly with healing — not just confession to God alone. What does it cost to say something out loud to another person that you''ve only ever said to God, and what might it be worth?',
   'God, thank you for the people you''ve put around us. Give us the courage to actually use that support instead of carrying things alone. Amen.', true),
  ('171f6230-da49-521a-83fd-24a5f0c64c9b', 'Romans 6:12-14', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Therefore don''t let sin reign in your mortal body, that you should obey it in its lusts. Neither present your members to sin as instruments of unrighteousness, but present yourselves to God, as alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness to God. For sin will not have dominion over you. For you are not under law, but under grace.', 'Paul writes this to people who are already free in Christ — the instruction isn''t about becoming free, it''s about what to do with freedom that''s already true.',
   'What''s something you do almost automatically, without really deciding to, when you''re stressed or bored?', 'Paul talks about what you "present" yourself to, like handing something over. What do you tend to hand yourself over to without thinking about it?', 'Paul says sin won''t have dominion over us — not that we''ll never be tempted, but that it doesn''t get to rule. What''s the difference between a struggle you''re still in and a master that''s still ruling you?',
   'God, show us what we''ve been handing ourselves over to without noticing. We want to belong to you instead. Amen.', true),
  ('4a7682ea-14ba-5cd8-b9b8-c1a8aab609dc', 'Matthew 6:24', 'Money', '"No one can serve two masters, for either he will hate the one and love the other; or else he will be devoted to one and despise the other. You can''t serve both God and Mammon.', '"Mammon" was a word for money or possessions, personified almost like a rival god. Jesus isn''t saying money is evil — he''s saying it''s powerful enough to actually compete for the position only God should hold.',
   'If someone looked only at how you spend your time and money, what would they guess actually matters most to you?', 'Is there a decision you''ve made recently where money quietly had more say than you''d like to admit?', 'Jesus doesn''t say serving money is hard — he says it''s impossible to do at the same time as serving God. Why might money specifically, more than most things, demand that kind of total loyalty?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to serve two masters. Show us where money has more of a vote than you do. Amen.', true),
  ('83a19d8d-bccd-5535-96a1-cdecafac09d0', 'Genesis 2:15', 'Work', 'Yahweh God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it.', 'This happens before anything goes wrong in the story — work isn''t a punishment invented later, it''s part of the very first, very good world God made.',
   'What kind of work — a job, a chore, a project — actually feels satisfying to you when it''s done?', 'Do you usually think of work as something that gets in the way of your real life, or as part of it?', 'God gives Adam work before sin ever enters the story. What changes if work is something God built into a perfect world, not just something we have to endure until we get to rest?',
   'Father, thank you for making work good before it ever got hard. Help us find that goodness in our own work today. Amen.', true),
  ('5c44f798-d6cc-5344-b9bd-d26d8a7f69ca', 'Luke 22:24-27', 'Ambition & Humility', 'There arose also a contention among them, which of them was considered to be greatest. He said to them, "The kings of the Gentiles lord it over them, and those who have authority over them are called ''benefactors.'' But not so with you. But one who is the greater among you, let him become as the younger, and one who is governing, as one who serves. For who is greater, one who sits at the table, or one who serves? Isn''t it he who sits at the table? But I am in the midst of you as one who serves.', 'This argument breaks out among the disciples during the Last Supper — one of the most serious moments in the entire Gospels — which makes the timing almost uncomfortable to read.',
   'Have you ever caught yourself comparing your own accomplishments to someone else''s, right in the middle of something that should''ve mattered more?', 'What does "greatness" actually look like among the people you spend the most time with — who gets treated like they matter most?', 'Jesus doesn''t scold the disciples for wanting to matter — he redefines what mattering actually looks like, using himself as the example. What would change in your own life if you measured greatness by who serves, not who''s served?',
   'Jesus, you served even in the middle of an argument about who was greatest. Teach us that kind of greatness. Amen.', true),
  ('a050f0d6-1b71-5003-9487-a2eb4b90934b', '2 Corinthians 9:6-7', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'Remember this: he who sows sparingly will also reap sparingly. He who sows bountifully will also reap bountifully. Let each man give according as he has determined in his heart; not grudgingly, or under compulsion; for God loves a cheerful giver.', 'Paul is writing about a specific financial collection for a struggling church, but his focus isn''t on the total amount raised — it''s on the spirit behind each person''s decision.',
   'What''s something you''ve given away — money, time, an object — that felt genuinely good to give, not like an obligation?', 'When you last gave something away, were you giving because you were supposed to, or because you actually wanted to?', 'Paul says God loves a cheerful giver — not just a giver, and not just a generous amount. What does it mean that the attitude behind a gift matters to God as much as the gift itself?',
   'God, make us cheerful givers, not grudging ones. Show us something to give freely today. Amen.', true),
  ('acea37f5-3c58-591d-8102-99faff668479', 'Titus 2:11-12', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men, instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present world;', 'Paul doesn''t present self-control as a rule imposed from outside — he calls it something grace itself teaches, as a natural outcome of it, not a requirement added on top of it.',
   'Has anyone ever taught you something valuable just by how they lived, without ever lecturing you about it?', 'Do you experience self-control more as pressure from outside yourself, or as something growing from the inside?', 'Paul says grace itself teaches self-control — not law, not willpower alone. Which one, in your own experience, actually lasts: forcing yourself, or being taught to want something?',
   'Father, teach us self-control the way grace is supposed to — not as pressure, but as something we actually want because of what you''ve done for us. Amen.', true),
  ('004d3658-b6c8-5003-a3e3-27e61111f5d9', 'Genesis 3:17-19', 'Work', 'To Adam he said, "Because you have listened to your wife''s voice, and have eaten of the tree, of which I commanded you, saying, ''You shall not eat of it,'' cursed is the ground for your sake. In toil you will eat of it all the days of your life. Thorns also and thistles will it bring forth to you; and you will eat the herb of the field. By the sweat of your face will you eat bread until you return to the ground, for out of it you were taken. For you are dust, and to dust you shall return."', 'This comes right after Genesis 2:15, where work was simply good. Here, after sin enters the story, that same work becomes genuinely hard — thorns, sweat, toil — without ever being erased as something God gave in the first place.',
   'What''s the most physically or mentally exhausting kind of work you''ve ever done?', 'Do you ever feel like work is fighting against you, not just something you do?', 'This curse doesn''t take work away — it makes the same good thing from Genesis 2 genuinely harder. What does it mean that work can be both a real gift and a real struggle at the same time, not one or the other?',
   'God, some of our work is genuinely hard. Meet us in the sweat of it, not just in the moments it feels good. Amen.', true),
  ('b8368f75-f0c5-5fe4-86db-6f463c28d444', '1 Timothy 6:6-10', 'Money', 'But godliness with contentment is great gain. For we brought nothing into the world, and we certainly can''t carry anything out. But having food and clothing, we will be content with that. But those who are determined to be rich fall into a temptation and a snare and many foolish and harmful lusts, such as drown men in ruin and destruction. For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Some have been led astray from the faith in their greed, and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows.', 'This verse is often misquoted as "money is the root of all evil" — it actually says "the love of money," and specifically warns about the drive to become rich, not about having money itself.',
   'Where''s the line, for you, between wanting enough and wanting to be rich?', 'Has chasing more of something — money or anything else — ever cost you more than it gave you?', 'Paul says being "determined to be rich" is the trap, not wealth itself. What''s the difference between having money and being ruled by the pursuit of it?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to be pierced by chasing more. Teach us contentment as the real gain it actually is. Amen.', true),
  ('b13851cd-638b-5b48-9d3b-609270505c71', 'Proverbs 30:8-9', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'Remove far from me falsehood and lies. Give me neither poverty nor riches. Feed me with the food that is needful for me; Lest I be full, deny you, and say, ''Who is Yahweh?'' Or lest I be poor, and steal, And so dishonor the name of my God.', 'This is an unusual prayer — most people pray for more, not for "just enough." The writer names a real danger on both ends: too much can breed self-sufficiency, too little can breed desperation.',
   'What would "just enough" actually look like for you, specifically?', 'Have you noticed a connection, in your own life, between how much you have and how much you actually think about God?', 'The writer doesn''t ask for wealth as a blessing or poverty as humility — he asks for a specific middle amount, because he thinks either extreme could pull him away from God. Do you think he''s right that both directions carry real spiritual risk?',
   'Father, we don''t often pray for "just enough." Teach us to actually want it, instead of always wanting more. Amen.', true),
  ('9ec0f95c-5da5-5654-8f8f-c67c781d9a5e', 'Philippians 3:4-8', 'Ambition & Humility', 'though I myself might have confidence even in the flesh. If any other man thinks that he has confidence in the flesh, I yet more: circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of Hebrews; concerning the law, a Pharisee; concerning zeal, persecuting the assembly; concerning the righteousness which is in the law, found blameless. However, what things were gain to me, these have I counted loss for Christ. Yes most assuredly, and I count all things to be loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus, my Lord, for whom I suffered the loss of all things, and count them nothing but refuse, that I may gain Christ', 'Paul is listing his own résumé here — genuinely impressive credentials by every standard of his world — before deliberately setting the whole list aside as worthless compared to what he actually values now.',
   'What''s an accomplishment you were once really proud of that matters a lot less to you now?', 'What''s on your own version of this list — the credentials or achievements you''d be tempted to lead with if you were trying to impress someone?', 'Paul doesn''t say his old accomplishments were worthless in themselves — he says he counts them as loss "for Christ," by comparison. What would it take for you to genuinely hold your own achievements that loosely?',
   'Jesus, help us hold what we''ve achieved loosely enough to actually see what matters more. Amen.', true),
  ('81fd3414-b823-52c4-aad3-d82c100116ce', '2 Peter 2:19', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'promising them liberty, while they themselves are bondservants of corruption; for by whom a man is overcome, by the same is he also brought into bondage.', 'Peter is warning about false teachers who promised freedom while actually being enslaved themselves — the verse''s larger point stands on its own: whatever overcomes you becomes your master, no matter what it promised on the way in.',
   'Has something that promised to make your life easier ever ended up controlling more of it than you expected?', 'What''s something in your life that started as a choice and now feels more like a habit that chooses for you?', 'Peter says being overcome by something is the same as being enslaved to it — not a separate, lesser category. Why might it matter to call it slavery honestly, instead of a softer word like "habit" or "preference"?',
   'Lord, help us call things what they actually are, instead of softening the truth about what has a hold on us. Amen.', true),
  ('00d4c4f3-cf2e-51d1-8052-e9ebef9c384c', 'Exodus 20:8-11', 'Work', '"Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy. You shall labor six days, and do all your work, but the seventh day is a Sabbath to Yahweh your God. You shall not do any work in it, you, nor your son, nor your daughter, your man-servant, nor your maid-servant, nor your cattle, nor your stranger who is within your gates; for in six days Yahweh made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that is in them, and rested the seventh day; therefore Yahweh blessed the Sabbath day, and made it holy.', 'This command covers everyone in the household, including servants and animals — rest isn''t treated as a reward for finishing enough work, it''s built in as something owed to everyone regardless of how much is left undone.',
   'What does an actual day of rest look like for you, if you''ve ever really had one?', 'What usually keeps you from resting, even when you technically have the time?', 'This command roots rest in God''s own example — he rested, not because he was tired, but because rest was part of the pattern from the beginning. What does it mean that rest is commanded, not just permitted?',
   'God, you built rest into the very rhythm of the world. Help us actually take it instead of just believing in it. Amen.', true),
  ('39888ec2-9d77-5c6d-8239-0be36e41111e', 'Luke 14:7-11', 'Ambition & Humility', 'He spoke a parable to those who were invited, when he noticed how they chose the best seats, and said to them, "When you are invited by anyone to a marriage feast, don''t sit in the best seat, since perhaps someone more honorable than you might be invited by him, and he who invited both of you would come and tell you, ''Make room for this person.'' Then you would begin, with shame, to take the lowest place. But when you are invited, go and sit in the lowest place, so that when he who invited you comes, he may tell you, ''Friend, move up higher.'' Then you will be honored in the presence of all who sit at the table with you. For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted."', 'Jesus tells this after literally watching people jockey for the best seats at a dinner — the story is about an actual social habit he noticed happening in front of him.',
   'Have you ever watched people compete for the "best seat," literally or otherwise, at something you were at?', 'Where in your own life are you angling for recognition instead of just letting your actual contribution speak?', 'Jesus isn''t just giving etiquette advice — he ends with a much bigger claim about exaltation and humility. Taking the lowest place strategically, hoping to be noticed, and actually not needing the recognition at all can look identical from the outside — how would you tell them apart in yourself?',
   'Jesus, we don''t want to perform humility just to be noticed for it. Teach us the real thing. Amen.', true),
  ('8f6a05e9-897f-5b62-b32e-d4eac0420700', 'Ecclesiastes 5:10', 'Money', 'He who loves silver shall not be satisfied with silver; nor he who loves abundance, with increase: this also is vanity.', 'The Teacher states this as a simple, observed fact about how desire for money actually works — not a moral rule so much as a description of what he''s watched happen, over and over.',
   'Has getting more of something you wanted ever actually satisfied the wanting, or just moved it somewhere else?', 'What''s something you thought would finally be "enough," and wasn''t?', 'The Teacher calls this pattern "vanity" — not evil, exactly, but empty, going nowhere. Why might loving money specifically create an appetite that more money can never actually fill?',
   'God, we''ve chased "enough" in the wrong places before. Teach us what will actually satisfy. Amen.', true),
  ('a484cee9-4729-5ef8-ba72-d53c4486225b', 'Acts 20:35', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'In all things I gave you an example, that so laboring you ought to help the weak, and to remember the words of the Lord Jesus, that he himself said, ''It is more blessed to give than to receive.''"', 'This is one of the only sayings of Jesus recorded outside the four Gospels — Paul quotes it from memory, as something already well known among believers by the time he''s writing.',
   'What''s something you gave away recently that felt better to give than anything you''ve received lately?', 'Do you actually believe giving is more blessed than receiving, or does that feel like something you''re supposed to say?', 'Jesus doesn''t say giving is a duty or a sacrifice — he says it''s more blessed, genuinely better, than receiving. What would change about how you give if you actually believed that was true?',
   'Jesus, we want to believe this, not just repeat it. Show us the blessing in giving, not just the cost. Amen.', true),
  ('9a108254-5598-5ef4-9bfc-91963222243e', '2 Peter 1:5-6', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Yes, and for this very cause adding on your part all diligence, in your faith supply moral excellence; and in moral excellence, knowledge; and in knowledge, self-control; and in self-control patience; and in patience godliness;', 'Peter lists these qualities as a chain, each one building on the last — self-control isn''t presented alone, it''s one link connecting knowledge to patience.',
   'What''s a skill or quality you''ve actually built over time, one step at a time, rather than getting all at once?', 'Where in this chain — faith, excellence, knowledge, self-control, patience — do you feel like you''re currently working?', 'Peter doesn''t ask for self-control by itself, disconnected from everything else — it comes from knowledge and leads toward patience. What might change if you stopped trying to will self-control into existence and instead worked on what comes right before it?',
   'God, build this chain in us, one link at a time. We don''t need to master everything today. Amen.', true),
  ('7ea070aa-e556-5059-9d09-7266c3e4bc5f', 'Proverbs 5:22', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'The evil deeds of the wicked ensnare him. The cords of his sin hold him firmly.', 'The image here is of something that starts as a single action and ends up wound tight enough to actually hold a person in place, like rope.',
   'Has a small, repeated choice ever turned into something harder to get out of than you expected?', 'What''s a "cord" in your own life right now — something that started small and has quietly gotten stronger with repetition?', 'The proverb doesn''t describe one dramatic trap — it describes cords, plural, each one probably thin enough to ignore on its own. Why might the danger of a pattern be easier to miss than the danger of a single bad decision?',
   'Lord, show us the cords we''ve stopped noticing because we got used to them. Help us cut what needs to be cut. Amen.', true),
  ('922eef17-61bf-5bfc-a40e-84917559a0fd', 'Genesis 2:2-3', 'Work', 'On the seventh day God finished his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made. God blessed the seventh day, and made it holy, because he rested in it from all his work which he had created and made.', 'God doesn''t rest because he''s tired — he rests as part of the pattern of creation itself, setting an example long before any command to rest is given to anyone else.',
   'What''s the difference between resting because you have to and resting because it''s actually good?', 'Do you tend to feel guilty when you stop working, even when there''s nothing wrong with stopping?', 'God, who never gets tired, still rests and calls it holy. What does it mean that rest isn''t just recovery from being worn out — it''s something good in itself, built into the pattern from the very beginning?',
   'Father, you rested and called it holy. Help us believe that about our own rest too, instead of feeling guilty for it. Amen.', true),
  ('069f972e-c13e-5b85-8bdd-f0800efa68a1', '3 John 1:9-10', 'Ambition & Humility', 'I wrote to the assembly, but Diotrephes, who loves to be first among them, doesn''t accept what we say. Therefore, if I come, I will call attention to his deeds which he does, unjustly accusing us with wicked words. Not content with this, neither does he himself receive the brothers, and those who would, he forbids and throws out of the assembly.', 'This is a real, specific person named in a real letter — his desire to be first has led him to reject a leader he should have listened to and to push other people out of the community.',
   'Have you ever seen wanting to be "the one in charge" actually make a group worse, not better?', 'When has wanting to be first made you treat someone else worse than they deserved?', 'John doesn''t just say Diotrephes is ambitious — he traces exactly what that ambition led to: rejecting correction, spreading accusations, and pushing people out. What does it look like, specifically, when wanting to be first starts costing other people something?',
   'God, show us if wanting to be first has ever cost someone else something we didn''t notice. Amen.', true),
  ('ead7d3fe-4107-55c1-8a4a-d01c14b46e82', 'Luke 16:19-31', 'Money', '"Now there was a certain rich man, and he was clothed in purple and fine linen, living in luxury every day. A certain beggar, named Lazarus, was laid at his gate, full of sores, and desiring to be fed with the crumbs that fell from the rich man''s table. Yes, even the dogs came and licked his sores. It happened that the beggar died, and that he was carried away by the angels to Abraham''s bosom. The rich man also died, and was buried. In Hades, he lifted up his eyes, being in torment, and saw Abraham far off, and Lazarus at his bosom. He cried and said, ''Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water, and cool my tongue! For I am in anguish in this flame.'' "But Abraham said, ''Son, remember that you, in your lifetime, received your good things, and Lazarus, in like manner, bad things. But now here he is comforted and you are in anguish. Besides all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed, that those who want to pass from here to you are not able, and that none may cross over from there to us.'' "He said, ''I ask you therefore, father, that you would send him to my father''s house; for I have five brothers, that he may testify to them, so they won''t also come into this place of torment.'' "But Abraham said to him, ''They have Moses and the prophets. Let them listen to them.'' "He said, ''No, father Abraham, but if one goes to them from the dead, they will repent.'' "He said to him, ''If they don''t listen to Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded if one rises from the dead.''"', 'Jesus doesn''t say the rich man did anything actively cruel to Lazarus — the story''s discomfort is that he simply never seemed to notice him, a starving man laid at his own gate every day.',
   'Is there someone or something right in front of you regularly that''s easy not to really notice?', 'Does never actively harming anyone feel like enough to you, or does this story complicate that?', 'The rich man''s failure in this story isn''t one dramatic act of cruelty — it''s an entire lifetime of not noticing a specific person at his own gate. What does that suggest about the kind of failure that''s easiest to overlook in yourself?',
   'Jesus, open our eyes to who''s at our own gate — the person right in front of us we''ve stopped really seeing. Amen.', true),
  ('e91e6c92-8ed2-5dc2-b220-bc0fcd0c7edd', '1 Kings 17:10-16', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'So he arose and went to Zarephath; and when he came to the gate of the city, behold, a widow was there gathering sticks: and he called to her, and said, Please get me a little water in a vessel, that I may drink. As she was going to get it, he called to her, and said, Please bring me a morsel of bread in your hand. She said, As Yahweh your God lives, I don''t have a cake, but a handful of meal in the jar, and a little oil in the jar: and, behold, I am gathering two sticks, that I may go in and bake it for me and my son, that we may eat it, and die. Elijah said to her, Don''t be afraid; go and do as you have said; but make me of it a little cake first, and bring it forth to me, and afterward make for you and for your son. For thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, The jar of meal shall not empty, neither shall the jar of oil fail, until the day that Yahweh sends rain on the earth. She went and did according to the saying of Elijah: and she, and he, and her house, ate many days. The jar of meal didn''t empty, neither did the jar of oil fail, according to the word of Yahweh, which he spoke by Elijah.', 'This widow isn''t giving out of surplus — she''s telling Elijah, plainly, that she and her son are about to eat their literal last meal before Elijah asks her to feed him first.',
   'Has anyone ever given you something when you knew they genuinely couldn''t spare it?', 'Would you be able to give generously if giving meant real, immediate sacrifice, not just spare change?', 'Elijah asks this widow to give away food when she believes she''s about to starve, and she does it before seeing any proof it will work out. What''s the difference between generosity that costs you nothing and generosity that requires trusting God first?',
   'God, we don''t always give from our extra. Give us the widow''s trust when giving actually costs us something. Amen.', true),
  ('7f5e4bb2-0fb6-52d2-837d-885304c74020', 'Proverbs 16:32', 'Anger & Conflict', 'One who is slow to anger is better than the mighty; One who rules his spirit, than he who takes a city.', 'The proverb compares two kinds of strength directly — the kind that conquers cities, and the kind that governs your own reactions — and says the second is greater.',
   'What''s something impressive you''ve seen someone accomplish that seemed easier, honestly, than staying calm under pressure?', 'Which is harder for you — controlling a situation, or controlling your own reaction to it?', 'This proverb ranks ruling your own spirit above conquering a city — a much bigger, more publicly impressive achievement. Why might self-control be the harder, and quieter, victory?',
   'Lord, help us want this kind of strength — the quiet kind, not just the impressive kind. Amen.', true),
  ('2e117369-1ad2-5686-8f78-b4b0b7ff3479', 'Mark 2:27', 'Work', 'He said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.', 'Jesus says this after being criticized for letting his disciples pick grain to eat on the Sabbath — he''s responding to people who had turned a gift meant to serve people into a rulebook people had to serve instead.',
   'Has a rule that was supposed to help you ever ended up feeling like a burden instead?', 'Do you treat rest as something you''re allowed to have, or something you have to justify?', 'Jesus flips the usual assumption — the Sabbath exists for people''s benefit, not the other way around. What would change about how you rest if you actually believed rest was made for you, not the other way around?',
   'Jesus, help us receive rest as the gift you meant it to be, not one more thing to manage correctly. Amen.', true),
  ('f1a2c783-ad75-57ac-a95e-d47cbc4eb762', 'Ecclesiastes 2:22-24', 'Work', 'For what has a man of all his labor, and of the striving of his heart, in which he labors under the sun? For all his days are sorrows, and his travail is grief; yes, even in the night his heart takes no rest. This also is vanity. There is nothing better for a man than that he should eat and drink, and make his soul enjoy good in his labor. This also I saw, that it is from the hand of God.', 'The Teacher doesn''t pretend work is only good — he names its real toll, sleepless nights included, before landing on a modest, honest conclusion: enjoying the good in your work, day to day, is itself a gift from God.',
   'What''s a small, ordinary satisfaction from work — your job, a chore, a project — that you don''t usually stop to notice?', 'Has work ever cost you sleep or peace of mind more than it was actually worth?', 'The Teacher doesn''t resolve work''s frustration with a bigger success or a different job — he resolves it by noticing the good that''s already there, day to day. What would change if that was actually enough for you?',
   'Father, help us notice the good that''s already in our work today, instead of only chasing the next thing. Amen.', true),
  ('67136992-b65c-5880-b24e-d6caefe880e2', 'Proverbs 22:7', 'Money', 'The rich rule over the poor. The borrower is servant to the lender.', 'This proverb states a plain, observable fact about debt — not a judgment on anyone who has it, but an honest description of the leverage it creates.',
   'What''s a purchase you made that ended up costing you more freedom than money?', 'Has owing something to someone — money, a favor, an obligation — ever changed how free you actually felt around them?', 'The proverb calls the borrower a "servant" to the lender — strong language for a financial arrangement. What does it mean to think of debt as something that actually limits your freedom, not just your bank account?',
   'Lord, give us wisdom about what we owe and to whom, and help us value the freedom that comes from owing less. Amen.', true),
  ('3004a106-a872-5b69-8e8e-5002f6342b4f', 'Genesis 11:1-4', 'Ambition & Humility', 'The whole earth was of one language and of one speech. It happened, as they traveled east, that they found a plain in the land of Shinar; and they lived there. They said one to another, "Come, let''s make brick, and burn them thoroughly." They had brick for stone, and they used tar for mortar. They said, "Come, let''s build us a city, and a tower, whose top reaches to the sky, and let''s make us a name; lest we be scattered abroad on the surface of the whole earth."', 'The people building this tower say their actual goal plainly: to make a name for themselves. Their skill and cooperation aren''t the problem here — what they''re building it all toward is.',
   'What''s something impressive people build or achieve mostly to be remembered for it?', 'What "tower" are you building mainly so people will notice you built it?', 'The builders'' skill, cooperation, and ambition are all genuinely impressive — the story doesn''t criticize their ability, only what they aimed it at. Two people can build the exact same excellent thing for entirely different reasons — how would you know which one you''re actually doing?',
   'Father, we want to build good things. Keep us from building them only to be noticed for it. Amen.', true),
  ('62f4415d-b3b9-5484-bc39-944311345e36', 'Proverbs 11:24-25', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'There is one who scatters, and increases yet more. There is one who withholds more than is appropriate, but gains poverty. The liberal soul shall be made fat. He who waters shall be watered also himself.', 'The proverb describes two opposite instincts — scattering generously versus withholding tightly — and says the results run counter to what you''d expect from either one.',
   'Have you ever noticed that holding onto something too tightly made you feel poorer, not richer?', 'What do you tend to withhold more than you actually need to — time, attention, money?', 'The proverb says the generous soul is "made fat" and the one who waters others is watered themselves — generosity described as something that comes back around, not just something given away. Do you think that''s a promise, or just an honest pattern the writer noticed?',
   'God, loosen our grip on what we hold too tightly, and help us trust what you say about generosity coming back around. Amen.', true),
  ('f48902da-6dc8-5924-8fc2-bb489093837e', '2 Corinthians 3:17', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'Now the Lord is the Spirit and where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.', 'This is a short, direct claim — liberty isn''t described as a reward for good behavior, but as something that simply exists wherever God''s own Spirit is present.',
   'What''s a place, or moment, where you''ve genuinely felt free — not just relieved, but actually free?', 'Does this verse describe freedom as something you earn or something you''re given? Which does it feel like in your own life?', 'This verse ties liberty directly to the Spirit''s presence, not to circumstances changing. What would it look like to look for freedom there, rather than waiting for your situation to be different first?',
   'Spirit, where you are, there''s freedom. Be present with us tonight, in whatever still feels bound. Amen.', true),
  ('c21d68ae-986a-51cf-99fc-39c4f7c3934b', '2 Thessalonians 3:10-12', 'Work', 'For even when we were with you, we commanded you this: "If anyone will not work, neither let him eat." For we hear of some who walk among you in rebellion, who don''t work at all, but are busybodies. Now those who are that way, we command and exhort in the Lord Jesus Christ, that with quietness they work, and eat their own bread.', 'Paul is addressing a specific problem in this church — people who had stopped working and were instead meddling in everyone else''s business — not making a general statement about anyone unable to work.',
   'What''s a responsibility you sometimes avoid by staying busy with something easier instead?', 'Where would you rather comment on what other people are doing than actually do your own part?', 'Paul pairs "won''t work" with "busybody" — as if avoiding your own responsibility and meddling in someone else''s are connected, not separate problems. Why might those two habits actually feed each other?',
   'Lord, show us where we''ve been more interested in someone else''s work than our own, and give us the focus to do ours quietly and well. Amen.', true),
  ('c27b8265-12ec-5a5d-a641-66fe002a8bac', 'Numbers 11:4-6', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'The mixed multitude that was among them lusted exceedingly: and the children of Israel also wept again, and said, Who shall give us flesh to eat? We remember the fish, which we ate in Egypt for nothing; the cucumbers, and the melons, and the leeks, and the onions, and the garlic: but now our soul is dried away; there is nothing at all save this manna to look on.', 'The people complaining here are being fed daily, supernaturally, by God — manna they didn''t have to plant or harvest — and are still homesick for the food they ate as slaves in Egypt.',
   'Have you ever found yourself missing something from your past that, honestly, wasn''t even that good?', 'What good, steady provision in your life has started to feel boring instead of like the gift it actually is?', 'The Israelites are being fed by God himself and still call it "nothing at all" compared to food they ate as slaves. What does it take to actually see daily provision as good, instead of taking it for granted the moment it becomes routine?',
   'Father, forgive us for calling your daily provision "nothing" just because it''s become familiar. Help us actually taste the good in it again. Amen.', true),
  ('edcef88f-7672-5cfc-b12a-c9f4c8bbce79', '1 Peter 2:16', 'Recovery & Freedom', 'as free, and not using your freedom for a cloak of wickedness, but as bondservants of God.', 'Peter holds two things together in one short sentence — real freedom, and a warning not to use that freedom as a cover for doing whatever you want.',
   'Have you ever used the excuse "I''m free to do this" to justify something you already knew wasn''t a good idea?', 'What''s the difference, in your own life, between freedom and just doing whatever you feel like?', 'Peter calls believers both "free" and "servants of God" in the very same verse, without treating those as contradictions. What does it mean that real freedom and real responsibility aren''t actually opposites?',
   'Jesus, keep us from using freedom as an excuse. Help us use it the way you actually intended. Amen.', true),
  ('e0cb72a8-8e69-5e39-81b8-45455c8f7b57', 'Proverbs 6:6-8', 'Work', 'Go to the ant, you sluggard. Consider her ways, and be wise; Which having no chief, overseer, or ruler, Provides her bread in the summer, And gathers her food in the harvest.', 'The proverb picks the ant specifically because no one is managing it — it works without being told to, which is exactly the point being made to the "sluggard" being addressed.',
   'What''s a task you''re good at doing without anyone reminding you, and one you only do when someone''s checking?', 'Where in your life do you rely on someone else to motivate you that you probably could motivate yourself to do?', 'The ant has no boss and still gets the work done before it''s needed. What would change in your own life if you worked like that — not because someone''s watching, but because the work itself matters to you?',
   'God, we don''t always want to admit we need external motivation. Grow in us the kind of diligence that doesn''t need to be watched. Amen.', true),
  ('2ebd3631-24e0-5743-b91d-092d9c491182', '1 Timothy 6:17-19', 'Money', 'Charge those who are rich in this present world that they not be haughty, nor have their hope set on the uncertainty of riches, but on the living God, who richly provides us with everything to enjoy; that they do good, that they be rich in good works, that they be ready to distribute, willing to communicate; laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold of eternal life.', 'Paul doesn''t tell wealthy believers to give everything away or feel guilty for having money — he tells them where to place their hope, and how to actually use what they have.',
   'What''s something you own that you enjoy, without any guilt attached to enjoying it?', 'Where is your hope actually resting right now — more in what you have, or in God?', 'Paul says God "richly provides us with everything to enjoy" in the same breath as warning against trusting riches. What''s the difference between enjoying what you have and hoping in it?',
   'Father, thank you for what you''ve given us to enjoy. Keep our hope in you, not in what we have. Amen.', true),
  ('6f6784cc-3a5c-538c-b48c-d6bc9f186cb1', 'Luke 14:12-14', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'He also said to the one who had invited him, "When you make a dinner or a supper, don''t call your friends, nor your brothers, nor your kinsmen, nor rich neighbors, or perhaps they might also return the favor, and pay you back. But when you make a feast, ask the poor, the maimed, the lame, or the blind; and you will be blessed, because they don''t have the resources to repay you. For you will be repaid in the resurrection of the righteous."', 'Jesus is speaking at a dinner, to the host himself, about who typically gets invited to gatherings like this one — the people who can invite you back.',
   'Who''s someone you''ve included in something even though you knew they could never return the favor?', 'Does your own generosity tend to flow toward people who can eventually repay you, even in small, social ways?', 'Jesus points out that normal hospitality usually comes with an unspoken expectation of return. What would change about how you give if you specifically looked for people who couldn''t pay you back at all?',
   'Jesus, show us someone to include who has no way to repay us, and help that be the whole point. Amen.', true),
  ('0b7e175c-1c60-5bf4-bf7e-7b4f5d42b861', 'Proverbs 27:1', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Don''t boast about tomorrow; For you don''t know what a day may bring forth.', 'A short, plain proverb — not a warning against planning, but against assuming tomorrow is guaranteed to go the way you expect.',
   'What''s a plan you were completely confident about that fell apart anyway?', 'Do you tend to talk about the future with more certainty than you actually have?', 'The proverb doesn''t say don''t plan — it says don''t boast, as if tomorrow is already yours. Planning wisely and assuming you''re owed a day you haven''t lived yet can look the same on a calendar — how would you tell them apart in your own attitude?',
   'God, we don''t know what today will bring, let alone tomorrow. Help us hold our plans a little more loosely. Amen.', true),
  ('d4774388-0f53-57ca-bbfd-8d4cf69f9ecf', 'Ephesians 6:5-8', 'Work', 'Servants, be obedient to those who according to the flesh are your masters, with fear and trembling, in singleness of your heart, as to Christ; not in the way of service only when eyes are on you, as men-pleasers; but as servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart; with good will doing service, as to the Lord, and not to men; knowing that whatever good thing each one does, he will receive the same again from the Lord, whether he is bound or free.', 'Paul is writing to servants in a household economy very different from ours, but the specific instruction — not just performing well when watched — translates directly to any kind of work, for anyone, in any era.',
   'What''s a task you do differently depending on whether someone''s watching?', 'What would change about your work — a job, chores, homework — if you did the unwatched parts exactly as carefully as the watched parts?', 'Paul says to work "as to the Lord, and not to men" — as if the real audience for your effort was never actually your boss or your teacher. What difference would that actually make in the parts of your work nobody ever sees?',
   'Lord, help us do good work because it''s for you, not just because someone''s watching. Amen.', true),
  ('3f3314c1-e70e-5fe0-acd6-462e5764e6c7', 'James 5:1-6', 'Money', 'Come now, you rich, weep and howl for your miseries that are coming on you. Your riches are corrupted and your garments are moth-eaten. Your gold and your silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be for a testimony against you, and will eat your flesh like fire. You have laid up your treasure in the last days. Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you have kept back by fraud, cry out, and the cries of those who reaped have entered into the ears of the Lord of Hosts. You have lived delicately on the earth, and taken your pleasure. You have nourished your hearts as in a day of slaughter. You have condemned, you have murdered the righteous one. He doesn''t resist you.', 'James isn''t condemning wealth itself here — he''s naming a specific, ugly pattern: wealth built by withholding fair wages from people who did the actual work.',
   'Has anyone ever done work for you, paid or unpaid, that you didn''t fully appreciate or repay at the time?', 'Where might your own comfort, even in small ways, depend on someone else being underpaid or unnoticed?', 'James says the withheld wages themselves "cry out" — as if the unfairness has its own voice God hears, even when no human complaint is ever filed. What does that suggest about what God actually notices, even when it goes unaddressed by everyone else?',
   'God, we don''t want comfort that''s quietly built on someone else''s unfairness. Show us where to look. Amen.', true),
  ('809534bd-9c20-5b90-8b3d-ec6d4bad29fe', 'Proverbs 10:9', 'Integrity', 'He who walks blamelessly walks surely, But he who perverts his ways will be found out.', 'The proverb contrasts two ways of moving through life — one straightforward, one twisted — and says only one of them actually holds up over time.',
   'Is it easier to keep track of the truth, or to keep track of a story you''ve bent a little?', 'What shortcut have you taken that you''ve had to keep managing ever since, just to keep it from unraveling?', 'The proverb says the crooked path will eventually "be found out" — not might be, will be. Why might integrity actually be the easier long-term path, even though it''s often the harder short-term one?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to manage a bent story. Give us the courage to walk the straight one, even when it costs more up front. Amen.', true),
  ('912c4d1a-d1d2-5e3c-97df-770fb696bbc0', 'Luke 16:10', 'Integrity', 'He who is faithful in a very little is faithful also in much. He who is dishonest in a very little is also dishonest in much.', 'Jesus makes this claim as a simple statement of pattern — the size of the situation isn''t what determines someone''s honesty, their actual character does, regardless of scale.',
   'What''s a "very little" thing — returning extra change, admitting a small mistake — where you can tell a lot about someone''s character?', 'Is there a small area of your own life where you cut corners you''d never cut in something bigger and more visible?', 'Jesus says faithfulness in small things and faithfulness in large things aren''t actually different qualities — they''re the same one, just at different scales. What would it look like to treat your smallest, most unnoticed responsibilities as a real test of your character, not just practice for something that matters more later?',
   'Lord, we want to be faithful in the small, unnoticed things, not just the big visible ones. Amen.', true),
  ('de1dc02b-d5eb-5d50-8118-2a346d09f01b', 'Ruth 2:2-3, 11-12', 'Work', 'Ruth the Moabitess said to Naomi, Let me now go to the field, and glean among the ears of grain after him in whose sight I shall find favor. She said to her, Go, my daughter. She went, and came and gleaned in the field after the reapers: and she happened to come to the portion of the field belonging to Boaz, who was of the family of Elimelech. Boaz answered her, It has fully been shown me, all that you have done to your mother-in-law since the death of your husband; and how you have left your father and your mother, and the land of your birth, and have come to a people that you didn''t know before. Yahweh recompense your work, and a full reward be given you of Yahweh, the God of Israel, under whose wings you are come to take refuge.', 'Gleaning meant walking behind the paid harvesters, picking up whatever grain they left behind — the lowest, most exhausting way to gather food, done here by a widowed foreigner with no other options.',
   'Has anyone ever specifically noticed and named a hard thing you did that most people overlooked?', 'What''s a form of work in your own life — caregiving, a thankless task, a quiet sacrifice — that mostly goes unnoticed?', 'Boaz doesn''t just compliment Ruth''s work ethic — he names the specific sacrifice behind it, leaving her whole family and country. What does it mean that being truly seen requires someone noticing the story behind the work, not just the effort itself?',
   'Father, you see the work that goes unnoticed by everyone else. Thank you for that, and help us notice it in others too. Amen.', true),
  ('1d9ca557-a3a7-5047-ab18-b10c164e1fc5', 'Psalm 127:2', 'Work', 'It is vain for you to rise up early, To stay up late, Eating the bread of toil; For he gives sleep to his loved ones.', 'The psalm directly confronts the instinct to earn security through sheer exhausting effort — rising early, staying up late — and contrasts it with something God simply gives.',
   'What''s the earliest you''ve gotten up or the latest you''ve stayed up just trying to get ahead of something?', 'Do you associate rest with something you have to earn, or something you''re given?', 'The psalm calls anxious overwork "vain" — not admirable, not necessary, actually pointless — right next to calling sleep something God gives freely to those he loves. What would it take to actually believe rest is a gift, not a reward for enough exhausting effort first?',
   'Father, we''ve tried to out-work our own exhaustion before. Help us receive the rest you actually want to give us. Amen.', true),
  ('73027370-17e6-5e9b-ad73-cd6593b1f6d5', '1 Corinthians 14:40', 'Self-Control & Habits', 'Let all things be done decently and in order.', 'Paul writes this at the end of instructions about how a chaotic church gathering should actually run — a short, practical close to a much longer conversation about order.',
   'What''s a small routine in your life that makes everything else run more smoothly when you actually keep it?', 'What happens, honestly, on the days you skip whatever "order" usually holds your day together?', 'Paul isn''t describing order as a rigid rule for its own sake — it''s in service of something bigger working well. What''s a routine you keep, or should keep, not because it''s exciting but because of what it actually makes possible?',
   'God, help us build the small, unglamorous order that actually makes room for what matters. Amen.', true),
  ('1c68bd87-da65-5207-b9c1-8c7ab25bd5e2', '1 John 2:15-16', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'Don''t love the world, neither the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father''s love isn''t in him. For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn''t the Father''s, but is the world''s.', 'John doesn''t leave "the world" as a vague idea — he names three specific pulls: physical desire, what looks good to the eyes, and pride in status or accomplishment.',
   'Of those three — physical desire, what looks appealing, pride in your own status — which one tends to pull at you the hardest?', 'Think of a recent purchase or decision — which of these three pulls, if any, was actually behind it?', 'John gives three distinct categories instead of one vague warning about "worldliness." Why might naming the specific pull make it easier to actually notice and resist, compared to a general warning?',
   'Father, help us name the specific thing pulling at us tonight, instead of a vague feeling we can''t quite pin down. Amen.', true),
  ('b8d01d82-0137-5e21-9e31-144c5c113317', 'Matthew 25:14-30', 'Work', '"For it is like a man, going into another country, who called his own servants, and entrusted his goods to them. To one he gave five talents, to another two, to another one; to each according to his own ability. Then he went on his journey. Immediately he who received the five talents went and traded with them, and made another five talents. In like manner he also who got the two gained another two. But he who received the one went away and dug in the earth, and hid his lord''s money. "Now after a long time the lord of those servants came, and reconciled accounts with them. He who received the five talents came and brought another five talents, saying, ''Lord, you delivered to me five talents. Behold, I have gained another five talents besides them.'' "His lord said to him, ''Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a few things, I will set you over many things. Enter into the joy of your lord.'' "He also who got the two talents came and said, ''Lord, you delivered to me two talents. Behold, I have gained another two talents besides them.'' "His lord said to him, ''Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a few things, I will set you over many things. Enter into the joy of your lord.'' "He also who had received the one talent came and said, ''Lord, I knew you that you are a hard man, reaping where you did not sow, and gathering where you did not scatter. I was afraid, and went away and hid your talent in the earth. Behold, you have what is yours.'' "But his lord answered him, ''You wicked and slothful servant. You knew that I reap where I didn''t sow, and gather where I didn''t scatter. You ought therefore to have deposited my money with the bankers, and at my coming I should have received back my own with interest. Take away therefore the talent from him, and give it to him who has the ten talents. For to everyone who has will be given, and he will have abundance, but from him who has not, even that which he has will be taken away. Throw out the unprofitable servant into the outer darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.''', 'Each servant is given an amount matched to his own ability, not an equal share — the story''s judgment falls not on how much each one had, but on what each one did with it. The parable''s ending is genuinely severe, and Jesus doesn''t soften it; this dinner''s questions focus on the choice at the story''s center, not on resolving its hardest verse.',
   'What''s something you''ve been given — a skill, an opportunity, resources — that you could either grow or just sit on?', 'Is there something you''re currently "burying" out of caution or fear, rather than actually using?', 'The third servant''s excuse is fear, not laziness — he''s afraid of the master and plays it safe. What''s the difference between being careful with what you''ve been given and being afraid to actually use it?',
   'Lord, show us what we''ve buried out of fear instead of using well. Give us courage to actually risk something for you. Amen.', true),
  ('aead8288-ce68-56cf-8061-1c0f96ed5058', 'Colossians 3:1-2', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'If then you were raised together with Christ, seek the things that are above, where Christ is, seated on the right hand of God. Set your mind on the things that are on the earth.', 'Paul isn''t telling believers to ignore ordinary life — he''s naming where their deepest attention and ambition should actually be aimed, as a direct result of who they already are in Christ.',
   'Where does your mind drift to automatically when it''s not focused on anything in particular?', 'What''s something "above" — eternal, lasting — that you''d like to think about more than you currently do?', 'Paul roots this instruction in something already true ("if you were raised with Christ"), not in effort alone. What would change about your daily focus if you actually believed that starting point was already settled?',
   'Father, draw our minds toward what actually lasts, not just what''s in front of us today. Amen.', true),
  ('f762b671-8e05-51ac-a0e5-62826c9b1b9a', 'Hebrews 13:16', 'Generosity & Contentment', 'But don''t forget to be doing good and sharing, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased.', 'The writer frames generosity as something easy to simply forget, not refuse — a short reminder tucked among other practical closing instructions.',
   'What''s something generous you meant to do recently and just never got around to?', 'Is forgetting to be generous different, in your own life, from deciding not to be?', 'The writer calls doing good and sharing a "sacrifice" that pleases God — ordinary, easy-to-forget acts, described with the same weight as something offered at an altar. What changes if you thought of small, forgettable generosity as genuine worship?',
   'God, don''t let us forget the good we meant to do. Remind us today, before the moment passes. Amen.', true),
  ('45956541-9f83-51c8-a7e7-91ca7d36e691', 'Malachi 2:14-16', 'Marriage', 'Yet you say, ''Why?'' Because Yahweh has been witness between you and the wife of your youth, against whom you have dealt treacherously, though she is your companion, and the wife of your covenant. Did he not make one, although he had the residue of the Spirit? Why one? He sought a godly seed. Therefore take heed to your spirit, and let none deal treacherously against the wife of his youth. For I hate divorce," says Yahweh, the God of Israel, "and him who covers his garment with violence!'' says Yahweh of Hosts. "Therefore take heed to your spirit, that you don''t deal treacherously.', 'Malachi is confronting men who assumed God wasn''t paying attention to how they treated their wives — the prophet''s answer is that God has been a witness to the marriage covenant the whole time, not an outside party to it.',
   'What''s a promise you''ve kept even when it stopped feeling easy?', 'Where have you quietly let faithfulness slide because you assumed no one was really watching?', 'Malachi calls God a "witness" to marriage, not just its author. What changes about how you treat a commitment if you believe someone is actually watching it be kept or broken, not just approving of it in theory?',
   'Father, you''re a witness to every promise we make. Help us take heed to our spirit in the commitments we''ve already made. Amen.', true),
  ('0636a616-d132-53e0-b200-ebd5184deab4', 'Isaiah 53:3-4', 'Grief & Loss', 'He was despised, and rejected by men; a man of suffering, and acquainted with disease: and as one from whom men hide their face he was despised; and we didn''t respect him. Surely he has borne our sickness, and carried our suffering; yet we considered him plagued, struck by God, and afflicted.', 'Christians have long read this Isaiah passage as describing Jesus, written centuries before he lived — whether or not that connection persuades you, the passage itself describes a person specifically marked by suffering and rejection, not triumph.',
   'Have you ever misjudged someone''s hard circumstances as somehow their own fault?', 'What kind of suffering do you tend to look away from instead of toward?', 'The passage says people considered this man "struck by God" because of how he suffered — assuming his pain meant something bad about him. What does it mean that Jesus, of all people, was the one this assumption got aimed at?',
   'Jesus, you carried real suffering and were misjudged for it. Help us not make the same mistake about the suffering people around us. Amen.', true),
  ('71c4906c-a014-5817-8014-d76258d050af', 'Daniel 4:30-32', 'Ambition & Humility', 'The king spoke and said, Is not this great Babylon, which I have built for the royal dwelling-place, by the might of my power and for the glory of my majesty? While the word was in the king''s mouth, there fell a voice from the sky, saying, O king Nebuchadnezzar, to you it is spoken: The kingdom is departed from you: and you shall be driven from men; and they dwelling shall be with the animals of the field; you shall be made to eat grass as oxen; and seven times shall pass over you; until you know that the Most High rules in the kingdom of men, and gives it to whoever he will.', 'This king is genuinely, historically powerful, not exaggerating his own success — the story''s point isn''t that his achievements were fake, but that he credited them entirely to himself.',
   'What''s something you worked hard for that you''re tempted to take all the credit for?', 'What accomplishment in your life have you quietly forgotten had a lot of help, timing, or circumstance behind it?', 'The king''s downfall comes at the exact moment he''s admiring his own achievement out loud. What''s the difference between being proud of good work and believing that work makes you the source of your own success?',
   'Father, we''re grateful for what we''ve been able to build. Keep us from forgetting who actually gave us the ability to build it. Amen.', true),
  ('52d79e16-b78a-512f-a784-5b3e52d67b56', 'Genesis 29:20', 'Perseverance', 'Jacob served seven years for Rachel. They seemed to him but a few days, for the love he had for her.', 'Seven years of labor is a genuinely long wait by any measure — the verse doesn''t say the time passed easily because it was short, but because of what Jacob was waiting for.',
   'What''s something you waited a long time for that ended up feeling worth every bit of the wait?', 'Is there a long wait in your life right now that feels unbearable because you''re not sure yet if it''s worth it?', 'The seven years aren''t shortened here — they''re the same seven years, just experienced differently because of love. What does that suggest about the actual source of patience — is it about the waiting getting easier, or about what you''re waiting for mattering enough?',
   'Lord, some of our waiting still feels long. Help us find, like Jacob did, something worth the wait. Amen.', true),
  ('105ff36e-53e8-5190-8713-375395454606', 'Exodus 3:11-12', 'Purpose', 'Moses said to God, "Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh, and that I should bring forth the children of Israel out of Egypt?" He said, "Certainly I will be with you. This will be the token to you, that I have sent you: when you have brought forth the people out of Egypt, you shall serve God on this mountain."', 'Moses''s question is a real objection, not false modesty — he genuinely doesn''t feel qualified for what he''s being asked to do, and God''s answer doesn''t argue with the self-doubt; it just promises presence.',
   'What''s something you were asked to do that you felt genuinely unqualified for?', 'What responsibility in front of you right now makes "who am I to do this" your honest, first reaction?', 'God doesn''t answer Moses''s "who am I" with a list of reasons Moses is actually qualified — he answers with "I will be with you." What does it mean that the answer to feeling unqualified isn''t a better resume, but a promise of presence?',
   'Father, we don''t always feel ready for what''s in front of us. Be with us the way you were with Moses, not because we''re qualified but because you promised. Amen.', true),
  ('80288e14-fdfd-5d0a-b78c-72b5a63a673d', 'Luke 11:5-8', 'Prayer', 'He said to them, "Which of you, if you go to a friend at midnight, and tell him, ''Friend, lend me three loaves of bread, for a friend of mine has come to me from a journey, and I have nothing to set before him,'' and he from within will answer and say, ''Don''t bother me. The door is now shut, and my children are with me in bed. I can''t get up and give it to you''? I tell you, although he will not rise and give it to him because he is his friend, yet because of his persistence, he will get up and give him as many as he needs.', 'Jesus tells this as a relatable, almost funny scenario — a late-night, awkward request between neighbors — before using it to make a point about prayer''s persistence.',
   'Have you ever gotten something just by asking again, and again, after being told no the first time?', 'What have you stopped asking God for because you asked once and moved on?', 'The friend in the story gets up not out of friendship but because of sheer persistence. What does it say about prayer that Jesus uses annoying, repeated asking as something to actually imitate, not something to be embarrassed about?',
   'Father, we give up on asking too quickly sometimes. Teach us to keep knocking. Amen.', true),
  ('4a41aad5-d37b-56b9-ae76-f0740f30b508', 'Genesis 22:1-2, 7-8', 'Faith', 'It happened after these things, that God tested Abraham, and said to him, "Abraham!" He said, "Here I am." He said, "Now take your son, your only son, whom you love, even Isaac, and go into the land of Moriah. Offer him there for a burnt offering on one of the mountains which I will tell you of." Isaac spoke to Abraham his father, and said, "My father?" He said, "Here I am, my son." He said, "Here is the fire and the wood, but where is the lamb for a burnt offering?" Abraham said, "God will provide himself the lamb for a burnt offering, my son." So they both went together.', 'Scripture calls this a test from the very first sentence — it doesn''t ask you to guess whether God actually intended harm. What happens next in the story (not shown here) is that God stops Abraham before anything happens to Isaac, and provides a ram instead.',
   'Has anyone ever asked you to trust them with something before you understood why?', 'What situation in your life makes trusting God feel like it''s asking more of you than you know how to give?', 'Abraham answers Isaac''s exact question — "where is the lamb?" — without knowing yet how it will actually be answered. What does it mean to say something true about God''s provision before you''ve seen it happen?',
   'Father, some trust asks more of us than we understand. Help us say "you will provide" honestly, even before we see how. Amen.', true),
  ('6e03fa9c-d0f0-5c15-8db2-65b255d07905', '2 Timothy 2:2', 'Discipleship', 'The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also.', 'Paul describes a specific chain here — what Paul taught Timothy, Timothy is to teach others, who will then teach still others — discipleship designed from the start to keep going past any one person.',
   'Who taught you something important that you''ve since passed on to someone else?', 'What''s something you''ve learned that you''re not yet passing on to anyone?', 'Paul''s instruction assumes at least four links in the chain — himself, Timothy, "faithful people," and the "others also" they''ll teach. What changes if you think of what you know not as something to keep, but as something you''re holding for someone further down the chain?',
   'Lord, show us who we''re meant to pass this on to, instead of just holding onto it ourselves. Amen.', true),
  ('27cc0e34-b4c0-5439-854f-2d51c35220e9', 'Mark 4:35-41', 'Faith', 'On that day, when evening had come, he said to them, "Let''s go over to the other side." Leaving the multitude, they took him with them, even as he was, in the boat. Other small boats were also with him. There arose a great wind storm, and the waves beat into the boat, so much that the boat was already filled. He himself was in the stern, asleep on the cushion, and they woke him up, and told him, "Teacher, don''t you care that we are dying?" He awoke, and rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, "Peace! Be still!" The wind ceased, and there was a great calm. He said to them, "Why are you so afraid? How is it that you have no faith?" They were greatly afraid, and said to one another, "Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?"', 'These are experienced fishermen, not people unfamiliar with storms — their terror is a real, professional judgment that this storm was genuinely life-threatening, which makes Jesus sleeping through it, and then calming it with a sentence, land harder.',
   'What''s the most afraid you''ve ever been in a situation that turned out okay?', 'Is there a storm in your own life right now where it feels like Jesus is asleep in the boat?', 'Jesus''s question isn''t "why did you wake me" — it''s "why are you so afraid." What does that suggest about the relationship between fear and what, or who, you actually believe is with you?',
   'Jesus, calm what''s actually storming in us right now, and help our fear make room for faith. Amen.', true),
  ('7b45d898-c0ae-560a-a64d-1eb84e9968a4', 'Ephesians 6:10-12', 'Courage', 'Finally, be strong in the Lord, and in the strength of his might. Put on the whole armor of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil. For our wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against the principalities, against the powers, against the world''s rulers of the darkness of this age, and against the spiritual hosts of wickedness in the heavenly places.', 'Paul names a struggle underneath the visible one — the actual people you might be frustrated with aren''t described here as the real opponent.',
   'Has a conflict with a person ever turned out to actually be about something bigger than that person?', 'Who have you treated as the enemy in a situation where the real issue was something else entirely?', 'Paul says the real struggle isn''t against "flesh and blood." What would change about how you handle conflict if you genuinely believed the person in front of you usually isn''t the actual battle?',
   'Father, help us see past the person in front of us to whatever''s actually at war underneath. Amen.', true),
  ('dbad2db7-c415-5500-8223-feb2fb0f1342', 'Proverbs 29:15, 17', 'Parenting', 'The rod of correction gives wisdom, But a child left to himself causes shame to his mother. Correct your son, and he will give you peace; Yes, he will bring delight to your soul.', 'These two verses sit a couple lines apart in the same chapter of practical wisdom — read together, the point isn''t a specific method of correction, but that consistent correction is aimed at a relationship that eventually brings peace, not just controlled behavior.',
   'What''s a correction someone gave you as a kid that you''re actually grateful for now?', 'In your own experience, does correction out of love actually feel different from correction out of frustration?', 'The proverb''s goal isn''t obedience for its own sake — it''s peace and delight in the relationship afterward. What would change about how correction happens in your home if that was the stated goal every time?',
   'Father, help correction in our home always aim at peace between us, not just controlled behavior. Amen.', true),
  ('ba6376e8-16a3-5101-8389-ed6634c21199', 'Psalm 90:12', 'Wisdom', 'So teach us to number our days, That we may gain a heart of wisdom.', 'This is a short prayer, not a statement — the psalmist isn''t just observing that life is short, he''s asking God to help him actually live differently because of it.',
   'If you knew you only had this exact day left, what''s one thing you''d actually do differently?', 'What do you keep putting off because you''re assuming you''ll have plenty more time for it?', 'The psalmist connects "numbering our days" directly to gaining wisdom — as if genuinely reckoning with mortality is what makes someone wise, not just sobering. Does knowing life is short actually change your choices, or just sit there as something you know?',
   'Lord, teach us to number our days, not to make us afraid, but to make us wise about how we spend them. Amen.', true),
  ('c78525d8-cff3-5860-bef3-e99203552d8f', 'James 4:6, 10', 'Ambition & Humility', 'But he gives more grace. Therefore it says, "God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble." Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you.', 'James doesn''t just command humility — he pairs it with a specific promise about what God does in response to it, on both sides: resistance to pride, grace toward humility.',
   'Is it harder for you to admit when you''re wrong, or to accept help when you need it?', 'Where in your life are you currently resisting humbling yourself, even a little?', 'James says God actively "resists" the proud — not just declines to help them, but pushes back. What does it mean that pride isn''t just unhelpful, but something God is described as working against?',
   'Father, we don''t want to be resisted by you. Humble us before we have to be humbled. Amen.', true),
  ('9c71aba1-5617-540a-8935-9f091e91ee32', 'Romans 8:24-25', 'Perseverance', 'For we were saved in hope, but hope that is seen is not hope. For who hopes for that which he sees? But if we hope for that which we don''t see, we wait for it with patience.', 'Paul makes a simple logical point — hope only makes sense for something you don''t have yet, which is exactly why hope requires patience, not just optimism.',
   'What''s something you''re hopeful about that you genuinely can''t see any evidence of yet?', 'Does waiting for something you can''t see feel different to you than waiting for something you''re confident is coming?', 'Paul says hope for something already visible isn''t really hope at all. What does it mean that real hope and real patience are actually the same posture, just named two different ways?',
   'Father, help us wait patiently for what we can''t yet see, and trust that real hope doesn''t need to see it first. Amen.', true),
  ('0fa413ea-0752-5d64-bc3e-235b2336c2eb', 'Ephesians 2:10', 'Purpose', 'For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared before that we would walk in them.', 'Paul doesn''t describe purpose as something you have to construct on your own — he describes specific good works already prepared in advance, waiting to be walked into.',
   'What''s something good you''ve done that felt like it was exactly the right thing at exactly the right time?', 'What good work in front of you right now have you been treating as optional rather than prepared specifically for you?', 'Paul says these works were "prepared before" — already planned, not invented by you in the moment. What changes if you stopped asking "what should I create for my life" and started asking "what''s already been prepared for me to walk into"?',
   'Father, thank you for preparing good work for us before we ever thought to look for it. Help us walk into it today. Amen.', true),
  ('51feb5aa-19ed-5c26-a69f-91959ce6b17d', 'Luke 18:1-8', 'Prayer', 'He also spoke a parable to them that they must always pray, and not give up, saying, "There was a judge in a certain city who didn''t fear God, and didn''t respect man. A widow was in that city, and she often came to him, saying, ''Defend me from my adversary!'' He wouldn''t for a while, but afterward he said to himself, ''Though I neither fear God, nor respect man, yet because this widow bothers me, I will defend her, or else she will wear me out by her continual coming.''" The Lord said, "Listen to what the unrighteous judge says. Won''t God avenge his chosen ones, who are crying out to him day and night, and yet he exercises patience with them? I tell you that he will avenge them quickly. Nevertheless, when the Son of Man comes, will he find faith on the earth?"', 'Luke tells us Jesus''s exact point before the story even starts — this parable is specifically about persisting in prayer without giving up, not a lesson about judges or legal systems.',
   'What''s a request you''ve had to make more than once before it was actually heard?', 'Is there something you''ve stopped praying about because it felt like nothing was happening?', 'Jesus contrasts an unjust judge who eventually gives in with a good God who already cares. If even an uncaring judge responds to persistence, what does that suggest about praying persistently to a God who isn''t unjust or unwilling in the first place?',
   'Lord, we don''t want to give up on the things we''ve been praying about. Give us the widow''s persistence. Amen.', true),
  ('020e40b5-518e-5c55-9286-fae433df63f4', '1 Peter 2:9-10', 'Identity', 'But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God''s own possession, that you may show forth the excellencies of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light: who in time past were no people, but now are God''s people, who had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy.', 'Peter is writing to scattered, often marginalized believers, and describes their identity in language usually reserved for kings and nations — not as flattery, but as a genuine claim about what being called by God actually makes of a person.',
   'What''s a label — good or bad — that other people gave you that stuck longer than it should have?', 'Which feels more true to you day-to-day: "chosen and called," or whatever labels the world has handed you?', 'Peter says these believers were once "no people" and are now "God''s people" — a total change of status, not just an improvement. What would it look like to actually let that identity outweigh the other labels competing for the same space?',
   'Father, thank you for calling us out of darkness into light. Help that identity be louder than every other label we carry. Amen.', true),
  ('51d9be4d-6344-513d-a0ff-15a391f86138', 'Luke 6:40', 'Discipleship', 'A disciple is not above his teacher, but everyone when he is fully trained will be like his teacher.', 'Jesus names the actual destination of discipleship here — not information transfer, but a person becoming like the one they''re following.',
   'Who''s someone you''ve picked up habits or mannerisms from just by spending a lot of time around them?', 'If someone became just like you spiritually after years of watching your life, would that be a good outcome?', 'Jesus says the goal of being a disciple isn''t just to know more, but to become like the teacher. What''s the difference between following Jesus''s teaching and actually becoming like him?',
   'Jesus, we want to become like you, not just know about you. Shape us through the time we spend with you. Amen.', true),
  ('d5da1878-0c7c-5fa8-aae1-8fa08e867315', 'John 6:66-69', 'Discipleship', 'At this, many of his disciples went back, and walked no more with him. Jesus said therefore to the twelve, "You don''t also want to go away, do you?" Simon Peter answered him, "Lord, to whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life. We have come to believe and know that you are the Christ, the Son of the living God."', 'This comes right after a hard teaching that caused many of Jesus''s followers to actually leave — Jesus doesn''t chase after them or soften what he said; he simply asks the twelve if they''re leaving too.',
   'Has there ever been a moment you almost walked away from something you''d committed to?', 'What''s kept you following Jesus through the parts that are genuinely hard to accept or understand?', 'Peter doesn''t say he fully understands everything Jesus just taught — he says he has nowhere else to go that has what Jesus has. Can you stay faithful without having resolved every hard question first — the way Peter does here?',
   'Lord, to whom would we go? Keep us close, especially through the parts we don''t fully understand yet. Amen.', true),
  ('25924c0f-9ac0-5757-819b-13805c8b7fcf', 'Acts 4:19-20', 'Courage', 'But Peter and John answered them, "Whether it is right in the sight of God to listen to you rather than to God, judge for yourselves, for we can''t help telling the things which we saw and heard."', 'Peter and John are ordinary, uneducated fishermen being questioned by religious authorities — their defense isn''t a theological argument, just a plain statement that they can''t stop talking about what actually happened to them.',
   'What''s something good that happened to you that you couldn''t help telling people about?', 'What''s something true about your own faith you''ve stayed quiet about because you didn''t feel qualified to explain it well?', 'Peter and John don''t claim to have all the answers — they claim only what they personally saw and heard. Why might simply saying what you''ve experienced carry its own kind of weight, even without a full defense of everything you believe?',
   'Father, we don''t need all the answers to talk about what we''ve actually experienced with you. Give us that same plain courage. Amen.', true),
  ('a404fe32-3a5b-5d89-9f82-0e8a9f80eb83', '1 Samuel 18:1-4', 'Friendship', 'It happened, when he had made an end of speaking to Saul, that the soul of Jonathan was knit with the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as his own soul. Saul took him that day, and would let him go no more home to his father''s house. Then Jonathan and David made a covenant, because he loved him as his own soul. Jonathan stripped himself of the robe that was on him, and gave it to David, and his clothing, even to his sword, and to his bow, and to his sash.', 'Jonathan is the king''s son and heir — giving David his robe and weapons is a real, symbolic transfer of status, not just a friendly gesture, from a man who by every worldly logic should have seen David as a rival.',
   'Who''s a friend that''s given you something — time, help, an actual object — that cost them more than they let on?', 'What friendship in your life have you let stay shallow that could actually hold more weight than you''ve given it?', 'Jonathan stands to lose the most from David''s rise, and gives to him anyway. What does real friendship look like when it might actually cost you something you''d otherwise want to protect?',
   'Father, thank you for friends who''ve given us more than they had to. Help us be that kind of friend to someone else. Amen.', true),
  ('80f40954-dac1-54ee-a427-42f142628d92', '1 Peter 5:5-6', 'Ambition & Humility', 'Likewise, you younger ones, be subject to the elder. Yes, all of you gird yourselves with humility, to subject yourselves to one another; for "God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble." Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time;', 'Peter uses clothing as the image here — humility isn''t described as a feeling you wait to have, but something you put on deliberately, the way you''d get dressed.',
   'What''s an outfit or uniform that changes how you act just by wearing it?', 'Where could you practically "put on" humility today, even if you don''t feel like it yet?', 'Peter tells people to "clothe" themselves with humility toward each other, not just toward God. Is it easier to be humble in the abstract than to actually be humble toward the specific people sitting at your own table?',
   'Father, help us put on humility today, even in the moments we don''t feel it yet. Amen.', true),
  ('d4ae07b0-77d4-5250-bf6d-76626628cc2b', '2 Peter 3:8-9', 'Perseverance', 'But don''t forget this one thing, beloved, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day. The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.', 'Peter is answering people who thought God was being slow to keep a promise — his answer isn''t that they''re wrong about the delay, but that what looks like slowness is actually patience, aimed at giving more people a chance.',
   'Has waiting on something ever turned out, later, to have been exactly the right amount of time?', 'Is there something you''ve accused God of being "slow" about that might actually be patience aimed at someone besides you?', 'Peter reframes God''s timing as patience rather than delay. What changes if the thing you''ve been calling "God is taking too long" is actually "God is giving someone more time"?',
   'Lord, help us see your timing as patience, not slowness — for us, and for the people we''re waiting alongside. Amen.', true),
  ('008c5ba4-8ef1-555c-802e-ad269dbc0cb0', 'Galatians 5:13', 'Leadership & Service', 'For you, brothers, were called for freedom. Only don''t use your freedom for gain to the flesh, but through love be servants to one another.', 'Paul directly connects two ideas people often treat as opposites — being genuinely free, and choosing to serve someone else — as if real freedom actually enables service rather than replacing it.',
   'What''s something you''re free to do that you choose not to do because of how it affects someone else?', 'Do you experience your own freedom more as "I get to do what I want" or "I get to actually help people"?', 'Paul says freedom is meant to be used for serving others "through love," not for self-gain. Which feels more free to you: doing whatever you want, or having the freedom to actually help someone else?',
   'Father, thank you for real freedom. Help us use it to serve the people around us, not just ourselves. Amen.', true),
  ('5237c8fa-8488-59f7-bdc4-76060eb5a5b9', 'Luke 17:12-19', 'Gratitude', 'As he entered into a certain village, ten men who were lepers met him, who stood at a distance. They lifted up their voices, saying, "Jesus, Master, have mercy on us!" When he saw them, he said to them, "Go and show yourselves to the priests." It happened that as they went, they were cleansed. One of them, when he saw that he was healed, turned back, glorifying God with a loud voice. He fell on his face at Jesus'' feet, giving him thanks; and he was a Samaritan. Jesus answered, "Weren''t the ten cleansed? But where are the nine? Were there none found who returned to give glory to God, except this stranger?" Then he said to him, "Get up, and go your way. Your faith has healed you."', 'All ten men are healed — the story doesn''t punish the other nine or take back their healing. It simply, pointedly, notices that only one came back.',
   'What''s something good that happened to you recently that you never actually thanked anyone for?', 'What gift in your life has become so constant it''s started to feel like it doesn''t need thanking anymore?', 'Jesus doesn''t undo the healing of the nine who didn''t return — the miracle stands either way. What does it say about gratitude that it doesn''t change whether you received something, only what you do after?',
   'Father, we don''t want to be counted among the nine. Turn us back to actually say thank you. Amen.', true),
  ('29892f34-a9a7-53ad-b796-e27505cea153', 'Galatians 3:26, 28', 'Identity', 'For you are all children of God, through faith in Christ Jesus. There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free man, there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus.', 'Paul is naming the deepest social and ethnic divisions of his world — free versus enslaved, Jew versus Greek — and saying plainly that none of them determine a person''s standing in Christ.',
   'What''s a category — status, background, achievement — that people often use to rank each other?', 'Have you used a category like that, even quietly, to think of yourself as more or less valuable than someone else?', 'Paul doesn''t say these categories disappear from the world — he says they don''t determine identity "in Christ." What would change about how you treat people if you actually believed no category ranked anyone above or below you?',
   'Father, thank you for making us one in Christ, past every category the world uses to rank us. Help us actually live like that''s true. Amen.', true),
  ('92c61665-a9c5-54dc-9236-90dc47eecd19', '1 John 4:20', 'Love', 'If a man says, "I love God," and hates his brother, he is a liar; for he who doesn''t love his brother whom he has seen, how can he love God whom he has not seen?', 'John doesn''t soften this — he calls the gap between claimed love for God and actual hatred of a real person a lie, not a lesser sin or an inconsistency.',
   'Is it easier to say you love someone in general, or to actually love a specific, difficult person in front of you?', 'Who can you see that you''re treating worse than your stated love for God would suggest you should?', 'John''s logic is blunt: loving an unseen God should be harder than loving a visible person, not easier — so failing at the easier one exposes something real about the harder claim. What does that suggest about where to actually check whether your love for God is real?',
   'Lord, show us where our love for you and our treatment of people don''t match up. Close that gap in us. Amen.', true),
  ('962f535e-0dce-5f57-8c56-a3b232204528', 'Genesis 3:8-10', 'Redemption', 'They heard the voice of Yahweh God walking in the garden in the cool of the day, and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of Yahweh God among the trees of the garden. Yahweh God called to the man, and said to him, "Where are you?" The man said, "I heard your voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself."', 'This is the very first recorded instance of a human hiding from God — before any punishment is announced, the instinct to hide has already kicked in.',
   'What''s something you''ve tried to avoid talking about because you were embarrassed, even before anyone confronted you about it?', 'What are you currently "hiding" from God, or from someone else, more out of shame than actual guilt?', 'God asks "where are you" — not because he doesn''t know, but as an invitation for Adam to come out of hiding himself. What does it mean that God''s first response to human shame is a question, not a punishment?',
   'Lord, we hide sometimes, the same way Adam did. Thank you for asking us out instead of leaving us there. Amen.', true),
  ('d3485a3a-ec1b-54cb-9105-8c07aa3ad989', 'Psalm 27:10', 'Grief & Loss', 'When my father and my mother forsake me, Then Yahweh will take me up.', 'The psalmist names the worst-case version of rejection — abandonment by his own parents — and states, directly, what he believes God''s response to that would be.',
   'Who''s someone who was there for you when you expected them not to be?', 'Have you ever felt rejected by someone who was supposed to love you unconditionally?', 'The psalmist doesn''t say this rejection wouldn''t hurt — he names it as a real, painful possibility and answers it with a specific promise, not a denial that it could happen. What does it mean that Scripture makes room for the worst version of rejection, rather than pretending it away?',
   'Lord, if anyone here has felt forsaken by the people who should have loved them most, take them up the way this psalm says you will. Amen.', true),
  ('120e37e4-8209-57a8-812f-a3fe15ab22b6', 'Proverbs 17:17', 'Friendship', 'A friend loves at all times; And a brother is born for adversity.', 'A short, plain proverb — the kind of line easy to agree with in general and worth actually testing against a specific relationship.',
   'Who''s a friend that''s shown up for you specifically during a hard stretch, not just the good times?', 'Are you the kind of friend who''s easy to find during good times and harder to reach during hard ones?', 'The proverb doesn''t just describe friendship as loyal — it says a friend loves "at all times," including the times that cost something. How would you know whether a friendship could survive real adversity, not just good times?',
   'Lord, thank you for friends who''ve stayed through hard times. Help us be that kind of friend to someone else. Amen.', true),
  ('102595b3-b6ce-5b29-a5ff-004af42936c8', 'Luke 23:42-43', 'Hope', 'He said to Jesus, "Lord, remember me when you come into your Kingdom." Jesus said to him, "Assuredly I tell you, today you will be with me in Paradise."', 'This exchange happens between two men being executed, at nearly the last possible moment either of them has — Jesus doesn''t ask for anything more from this man before answering him.',
   'Has anyone ever given you a second chance later than you thought was possible?', 'What part of your life have you assumed is "too late" for things to be made right?', 'This man has no time left to prove anything or change his life — his request is answered immediately, based on nothing but asking. What does it say about grace that it reaches all the way to the very last moment, with nothing required but a genuine ask?',
   'Jesus, thank you that it''s never too late to ask you to remember us. Amen.', true),
  ('46e9754e-983a-5de3-89f1-bf882f91c5d1', '2 Chronicles 7:14', 'Ambition & Humility', 'if my people, who are called by my name, shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways; then will I hear from heaven, and will forgive their sin, and will heal their land.', 'This is God''s specific answer to Solomon, about Israel and the temple he''d just built — worth reading as what it actually is, a promise to a particular covenant people at a particular moment, before drawing any wider lesson from its pattern of humility, prayer, and turning leading to God''s response.',
   'Has your family ever had to own a mistake together, not just individually?', 'Is there something your whole household — not just one person — needs to be more honest about right now?', 'This promise is addressed to a whole people, not one individual praying alone. What would it look like for your own household or community to humble itself together, not just each person separately?',
   'Lord, we don''t just need to humble ourselves individually. Help our whole household seek you together. Amen.', true),
  ('73633ea2-3403-5eed-80da-69486d257c29', 'Mark 10:42-44', 'Leadership & Service', 'Jesus summoned them, and said to them, "You know that they who are recognized as rulers over the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them. But it shall not be so among you, but whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant. Whoever of you wants to become first among you, shall be bondservant of all.', 'Jesus is responding to two disciples who''d just asked for the best seats in his coming kingdom — instead of answering that request directly, he redefines what greatness itself is supposed to look like among his followers.',
   'Who''s a leader you''ve respected specifically because of how they treated the people under them?', 'In your own life — at work, at school, at home — do you lead more like you''re serving people, or like you''re being served?', 'Jesus doesn''t say leadership is bad — he says its whole shape should be inverted from what the world calls greatness. What would change in your own ambitions if "greatest" specifically meant "serves the most," not "is served the most"?',
   'Jesus, reshape what we think greatness looks like. Make us servants first. Amen.', true),
  ('3b560ec3-d41a-5cad-ae6e-b89a073549e4', '1 Peter 4:10', 'Leadership & Service', 'According as each has received a gift, be ministering it among yourselves, as good stewards of the grace of God in its various forms.', 'Peter assumes every single reader already has something to offer — the instruction isn''t to go find a gift, but to actually use the one already given.',
   'What''s something you''re good at that you don''t often think of as a "gift" you could actually use to help someone?', 'Where have you held back from serving because you assumed you didn''t have anything valuable enough to offer?', 'Peter calls believers "stewards" of grace they''ve received, not owners of talent they''ve earned. What''s the difference between offering something because you''re proud of having it and offering it because you''re stewarding something given to you?',
   'Lord, show us the gift you''ve already given us, and give us the confidence to actually use it. Amen.', true),
  ('0c430633-a7f3-5ed6-b112-d11a3e5400ae', 'Colossians 3:15-17', 'Gratitude', 'And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to which also you were called in one body; and be thankful. Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly; in all wisdom teaching and admonishing one another with psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your heart to the Lord. Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father, through him.', 'Paul connects gratitude directly to peace and to how people treat each other within a shared community — not just personal thankfulness in isolation, but a household or church actually functioning well together.',
   'What''s something ordinary about your household — a song, a habit, a joke — that actually holds you together as a family?', 'Does gratitude in your home tend to stay private, or does it get said out loud to each other?', 'Paul ties "let peace rule" and "be thankful" together in the same sentence, as if they''re connected, not separate instructions. What''s the relationship between how thankful a household is and how peaceful it actually feels to live in?',
   'Lord, let your peace rule in this house, and make us the kind of family that says thank you to each other, not just to you. Amen.', true),
  ('5b951951-f609-5000-9e6d-8b29ebb5554e', 'Ephesians 1:4-5', 'Identity', 'even as he chose us in him before the foundation of the world, that we would be holy and without blemish before him in love; having predestined us for adoption as children through Jesus Christ to himself, according to the good pleasure of his desire,', 'Paul pushes the timeline of being chosen back further than birth, further than any decision or achievement — before the world itself existed.',
   'What''s something about yourself you feel like you have to earn approval for, over and over?', 'Does it feel true to you that you were wanted before you''d done anything at all to deserve it?', 'Paul says this choosing happened "before the foundation of the world" — before anything you could have done to earn or lose it. What changes about how you carry your own worth if it was never actually based on performance to begin with?',
   'Lord, thank you for choosing us before we could do anything to earn it. Help that settle something in us tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('3a27df83-fc72-5c7c-9583-44ea74d35049', 'Hosea 3:1', 'Love', 'Yahweh said to me, "Go again, love a woman loved by another, and an adulteress, even as Yahweh loves the children of Israel, though they turn to other gods, and love cakes of raisins."', 'God commands the prophet Hosea to keep loving his unfaithful wife specifically as a living picture of God''s own love for a people who kept turning away from him — an unusual, uncomfortable, and deliberately real illustration, not a hypothetical.',
   'Has anyone ever kept caring about you even after you let them down?', 'When have you had to decide whether to keep loving someone who hurt or disappointed you?', 'God compares his own love for an unfaithful people to Hosea''s love for an unfaithful spouse — not an easy, comfortable comparison. What does it say about God''s love that Scripture is willing to illustrate it with something this uncomfortable?',
   'Lord, thank you for loving us the way this passage describes — even when we''ve turned away. Amen.', true),
  ('91c8436c-2a9c-5b42-ac9d-3d0a310034ed', 'Isaiah 54:4', 'Redemption', 'Don''t be afraid; for you shall not be ashamed: neither be confounded; for you shall not be disappointed: for you shall forget the shame of your youth; and the reproach of your widowhood shall you remember no more.', 'This promise is aimed specifically at shame from the past — not present guilt needing correction, but old wounds and old embarrassments the speaker is told they won''t need to carry forever.',
   'What''s something from years ago that still makes you cringe when you think about it, even though it''s long over?', 'What''s a piece of shame from your past you''re still carrying that this verse would actually be speaking to?', 'This verse promises forgetting shame, not forgetting the event itself — the past isn''t erased, but its power to define you is. Can a memory stay with you honestly, once the shame attached to it is actually gone?',
   'Lord, take the shame of things long past. Let the memory stay if it must, but not the shame attached to it. Amen.', true),
  ('d8d0a26f-bc7c-549d-b0de-81e30c012915', 'Proverbs 27:6', 'Friendship', 'Faithful are the wounds of a friend; Although the kisses of an enemy are profuse.', 'The proverb pairs two surprising images — a friend''s "wound" and an enemy''s "kisses" — to make the point that honest correction from someone who loves you is worth more than easy flattery from someone who doesn''t.',
   'Do you generally prefer people who tell you what you want to hear, or people who tell you the truth even when it''s uncomfortable?', 'Who''s someone who''s told you a hard truth that turned out to actually help you?', 'The proverb calls a friend''s honest correction a "wound" — it''s not painless, but it''s called faithful anyway. How can you tell, in the moment, whether a hard word is meant to help you or just to hurt you?',
   'Lord, give us friends who''ll tell us the truth, and give us the humility to actually listen when they do. Amen.', true),
  ('9605136b-e550-5e37-be47-0a2176d8ee61', '1 Peter 4:12-13', 'Suffering', 'Beloved, don''t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you, to test you, as though a strange thing happened to you. But because you are partakers of Christ''s sufferings, rejoice; that at the revelation of his glory also you may rejoice with exceeding joy.', 'Peter is writing to believers facing real persecution, and his instruction isn''t to explain why it''s happening, but to name that suffering itself isn''t a sign that something has gone wrong or that God has abandoned them.',
   'Have you ever assumed something was uniquely wrong with you because you were going through a hard time?', 'Does hardship in your life tend to make you feel singled out, or does it feel like part of something bigger you''re not alone in?', 'Peter says not to be surprised by suffering, as though it were "a strange thing." What changes if hardship is treated as an expected part of following Jesus, rather than evidence that faith isn''t working?',
   'Jesus, when hard things come, help us not assume you''ve left. Meet us in it instead. Amen.', true),
  ('93f8a139-3337-55c9-a7f7-5521e1a82a81', 'Daniel 6:3-5, 10', 'Integrity', 'Then this Daniel was distinguished above the presidents and the satraps, because an excellent spirit was in him; and the king thought to set him over the whole realm. Then the presidents and the satraps sought to find occasion against Daniel as touching the kingdom; but they could find no occasion nor fault, because he was faithful, neither was there any error or fault found in him. Then said these men, We shall not find any occasion against this Daniel, except we find it against him concerning the law of his God. When Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house (now his windows were open in his chamber toward Jerusalem) and he kneeled on his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did before.', 'Daniel''s enemies have to invent a law specifically targeting his faith because his actual conduct gives them nothing else to use against him — and once the law passes, he keeps praying exactly as he always had, in full view, changing nothing.',
   'Who do you know whose character is so consistent that people just trust them automatically?', 'If someone wanted to find fault with your daily conduct, would they have much to work with?', 'Daniel doesn''t hide his prayer once it becomes dangerous, or pray more cautiously — he does exactly what he always did. What does it mean that his consistency, not a dramatic act of defiance, is what actually threatens his enemies'' plan?',
   'Jesus, make our daily lives consistent enough that there''s nothing to find fault with — not because we''re trying to look good, but because it''s actually true. Amen.', true),
  ('bd70d08f-74cf-5d80-8be1-ae026b9fde10', 'Exodus 18:17-23', 'Leadership & Service', 'Moses'' father-in-law said to him, "The thing that you do is not good. You will surely wear away, both you, and this people that is with you; for the thing is too heavy for you. You are not able to perform it yourself alone. Listen now to my voice. I will give you counsel, and God be with you. You represent the people before God, and bring the causes to God. You shall teach them the statutes and the laws, and shall show them the way in which they must walk, and the work that they must do. Moreover you shall provide out of all the people able men, such as fear God: men of truth, hating unjust gain; and place such over them, to be rulers of thousands, rulers of hundreds, rulers of fifties, and rulers of tens. Let them judge the people at all times. It shall be that every great matter they shall bring to you, but every small matter they shall judge themselves. So shall it be easier for you, and they shall share the load with you. If you will do this thing, and God commands you so, then you will be able to endure, and all of these people also will go to their place in peace."', 'Moses is trying to personally judge every dispute for the entire nation by himself — his father-in-law Jethro is the one who tells him plainly that this will wear him out, and offers a practical structure instead.',
   'What''s something you''ve tried to do entirely by yourself that you probably should have asked for help with?', 'Is there a responsibility you''re currently carrying alone that could actually be shared?', 'Jethro doesn''t tell Moses to work less hard — he tells him to share the load with capable people so the whole system can actually last. Is wanting to be needed the same thing as insisting on being the only one who can do it?',
   'Jesus, show us where we''re trying to carry something alone that was never meant to be carried that way. Amen.', true),
  ('f3acfa61-063b-5270-b62e-36c25b2c8802', 'Acts 6:1-4', 'Leadership & Service', 'Now in those days, when the number of the disciples was multiplying, a complaint arose from the Grecian Jews against the Hebrews because their widows were neglected in the daily service. The twelve summoned the multitude of the disciples and said, "It is not appropriate for us to forsake the word of God and serve tables. Therefore select from among you, brothers, seven men of good report, full of the Holy Spirit and of wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business. But we will continue steadfastly in prayer and in the ministry of the word."', 'This is one of the earliest recorded organizational problems in the church — a genuine complaint about widows not getting fed fairly — solved not with a speech, but by appointing specific, qualified people to a specific, unglamorous job.',
   'What''s a behind-the-scenes job — at church, school, or home — that keeps things running but rarely gets noticed?', 'Do you tend to value visible, public service more than quiet, practical service like this?', 'The apostles don''t treat "serving tables" as beneath the church''s mission — they treat it as important enough to require careful selection of good people. What does it say about service that this ordinary logistical need gets this much serious attention in Scripture?',
   'Jesus, help us take the ordinary, practical needs around us as seriously as this church did. Amen.', true),
  ('7ff26f7c-820e-5beb-a0c3-ab72c30b49df', 'Proverbs 11:13', 'Integrity', 'One who brings gossip betrays a confidence, But one who is of a trustworthy spirit is one who keeps a secret.', 'The proverb frames gossip not just as unkind talk, but specifically as breaking someone''s trust — a sharper, more precise problem than just "being mean."',
   'Are you someone people trust with things they don''t want repeated?', 'Have you ever had something you shared in confidence end up somewhere you didn''t intend?', 'The proverb frames this as a trust issue, not just a kindness issue. What''s the difference between "that was an unkind thing to say" and "that was a trust I broke"?',
   'Lord, make us the kind of trustworthy people others can actually confide in. Amen.', true),
  ('1a14e0c4-c4a2-596b-8b3b-0a903da99ede', 'Matthew 4:18-20', 'Discipleship', 'Walking by the sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers: Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew, his brother, casting a net into the sea; for they were fishermen. He said to them, "Come after me, and I will make you fishers for men." They immediately left their nets and followed him.', 'These men are in the middle of their actual livelihood, working, when Jesus calls them — the text emphasizes how immediate their response was, not how long they thought it over.',
   'What''s the fastest you''ve ever said yes to something that ended up changing your life?', 'What is Jesus asking of you that you''ve been slow-walking instead of leaving immediately?', 'These fishermen don''t finish the day''s work or make a plan first — they leave the nets right there. What would it cost you to respond that immediately to something you already know you should do?',
   'Jesus, we don''t always respond as fast as these fishermen did. Give us that same readiness today. Amen.', true),
  ('7d8cadfc-85d4-511e-9d9a-e9e390f86a27', 'Exodus 16:29', 'Work', 'Behold, because Yahweh has given you the Sabbath, therefore he gives you on the sixth day the bread of two days. Everyone stay in his place. Let no one go out of his place on the seventh day."', 'This comes during the story of manna in the wilderness — God arranges the provision itself around the day of rest, doubling the sixth day''s portion so no one has to work to gather food on the seventh.',
   'Has anyone ever planned ahead specifically so you wouldn''t have to work later?', 'Do you tend to treat rest as something you have to plan for and protect, or does it just get whatever time is left over?', 'God doesn''t just command rest here — he engineers the provision itself so rest is actually possible without anxiety about missing out. What would it look like for you to plan ahead the way this passage describes, so rest doesn''t require worry?',
   'Jesus, thank you for providing in a way that makes rest possible, not just commanded. Help us actually plan for it. Amen.', true),
  ('69dff5b6-a8c8-552c-9f42-0782cf6f5815', 'John 21:20-22', 'Ambition & Humility', 'Then Peter, turning around, saw a disciple following. This was the disciple whom Jesus sincerely loved, the one who had also leaned on Jesus'' breast at the supper and asked, "Lord, who is going to betray You?" Peter seeing him, said to Jesus, "Lord, what about this man?" Jesus said to him, "If I desire that he stay until I come, what is that to you? You follow me."', 'Peter has just been told something serious about his own future, and immediately turns his attention to comparing it with John''s — Jesus''s answer is short and pointed.',
   'Have you ever been given good news or a plan for your own life and immediately started comparing it to someone else''s?', 'Whose path are you most tempted to measure your own against?', 'Jesus doesn''t answer Peter''s question about John at all — he just redirects Peter back to his own calling. What does it cost you to actually let someone else''s path be "not your business," the way Jesus says here?',
   'Jesus, when we start comparing our path to someone else''s, bring us back the way you brought Peter back — to just following you. Amen.', true),
  ('ffaefbc0-aa3d-5fcb-a0c2-ad2d6cad148a', 'Psalm 68:6', 'Grief & Loss', 'God sets the lonely in families. He brings out the prisoners with singing, But the rebellious dwell in a sun-scorched land.', 'The psalm states this as something God actually does, not just a nice sentiment — placing lonely people into real belonging, the way he brought prisoners out into real freedom.',
   'Has a group of people ever become like family to you, even without being related?', 'Who in your life right now might genuinely be lonely, that you could actually include?', 'The psalm doesn''t say God comforts the lonely from a distance — it says he sets them "in families," actual belonging. What would it look like for your own family or table to be the specific place God is doing that for someone?',
   'God, thank you for setting the lonely in families. Show us who around us needs to be brought into ours. Amen.', true),
  ('6944644e-2f9e-51e5-be24-b74a8fcc0907', 'Proverbs 16:18', 'Ambition & Humility', 'Pride goes before destruction, And a haughty spirit before a fall.', 'This is one of the most familiar proverbs in the whole Bible — familiar enough that it''s worth actually stopping to ask what it means, rather than nodding along.',
   'What''s a time being overconfident led you to underestimate something you shouldn''t have?', 'Where might your confidence be outrunning your actual awareness of the risk right now?', 'The proverb doesn''t say pride is simply wrong — it says pride specifically comes right before a fall, as if it blinds you to what''s coming. What is it about pride, specifically, that makes it hard to see danger clearly?',
   'God, keep us from the kind of confidence that blinds us to what''s actually in front of us. Amen.', true),
  ('ae721228-2048-5dca-8e90-c657d16148b5', 'Psalm 15:1-2', 'Integrity', 'Yahweh, who shall dwell in your sanctuary? Who shall live on your holy hill? He who walks blamelessly does what is right, And speaks truth in his heart;', 'The psalm opens with a genuine question — who actually gets to be close to God — and answers with a description of ordinary, daily honesty rather than religious performance.',
   'Would your private honesty hold up to the same standard as your public honesty?', 'Where do what you say publicly and what you actually think privately not quite match?', 'The psalm''s answer to "who may dwell with God" isn''t a list of religious achievements — it''s someone who "speaks truth in his heart," meaning even privately, to themselves. What would change if you held yourself to that standard, not just your public words?',
   'God, we want to speak truth in our hearts, not just out loud. Search us there tonight. Amen.', true),
  ('afbe1a7c-6dbb-579f-beaf-514a4ce524f0', '1 Timothy 4:12', 'Leadership & Service', 'Let no man despise your youth; but be an example to those who believe, in word, in your way of life, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity.', 'Paul is writing to Timothy, a genuinely young leader some people were dismissing because of his age — his answer isn''t to demand respect, but to tell Timothy to earn it through how he actually lives.',
   'Has anyone ever underestimated you because of your age?', 'If you''re one of the younger people at this table, what''s one area from Paul''s list — word, life, love, faith — you could actually lead in this week?', 'Paul doesn''t tell Timothy to argue for respect — he tells him to become the kind of example that makes the objection irrelevant. What''s the difference between demanding to be taken seriously and actually living in a way that earns it?',
   'God, whoever at this table feels overlooked because of their age, give them the confidence to lead the way Timothy did — through example, not argument. Amen.', true),
  ('7a40b580-75fc-52e9-b07b-91ad6c093ccd', 'Luke 10:30-37', 'Love', 'Jesus answered, "A certain man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and he fell among robbers, who both stripped him and beat him, and departed, leaving him half dead. By chance a certain priest was going down that way. When he saw him, he passed by on the other side. In the same way a Levite also, when he came to the place, and saw him, passed by on the other side. But a certain Samaritan, as he traveled, came where he was. When he saw him, he was moved with compassion, came to him, and bound up his wounds, pouring on oil and wine. He set him on his own animal, and brought him to an inn, and took care of him. On the next day, when he departed, he took out two denarii, and gave them to the host, and said to him, ''Take care of him. Whatever you spend beyond that, I will repay you when I return.'' Now which of these three do you think seemed to be a neighbor to him who fell among the robbers?" He said, "He who showed mercy on him." Then Jesus said to him, "Go and do likewise."', 'Samaritans and Jews were bitterly divided in Jesus''s time — Jesus tells this story specifically to a Jewish audience, making the "hero" the exact kind of person they''d have been least likely to expect it from.',
   'Have you ever received real help from someone you least expected it from?', 'What group of people would genuinely surprise you if they gave you costly help — or if you were asked to give it to them?', 'The religious men in the story pass by, and the person least expected to help is the one who actually stops, at real cost to himself. How would your own definition of "neighbor" change if it was based on who actually shows mercy, not on category?',
   'Jesus, help us be the kind of neighbor who actually stops, not the kind who has good reasons to pass by. Amen.', true),
  ('e32d7896-0162-54c5-9d86-a315a741665e', 'Colossians 4:6', 'Integrity', 'Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one.', 'Paul uses an easy, physical image — salt seasoning food — for how speech should work: not bland, not harsh, but flavored with grace and fitted to the actual person you''re talking to.',
   'What''s a compliment or comment someone gave you that was worded just right — kind, but not empty?', 'Do your words tend to be more "salty" (sharp, blunt) or more "bland" (avoiding anything real) when a conversation gets difficult?', 'Paul says speech should be seasoned "that you may know how to answer each one" — as if the same words wouldn''t fit every person the same way. What does it look like to season your words differently depending on who''s actually in front of you, without changing what''s true?',
   'God, season our words with grace today — not bland, not harsh, but fitted to the person in front of us. Amen.', true),
  ('08f8ea30-c41d-5a62-8acd-04493b079ff5', 'Luke 14:28-30', 'Discipleship', 'For which of you, desiring to build a tower, doesn''t first sit down and count the cost, to see if he has enough to complete it? Or perhaps, when he has laid a foundation, and is not able to finish, everyone who sees begins to mock him, saying, ''This man began to build, and wasn''t able to finish.''', 'Jesus is warning against starting something — following him — without first being honest about what it will actually require, using an ordinary building project as the example.',
   'Have you ever started a big project without realizing what it would actually take to finish?', 'Think of a commitment you made without really counting the cost first — are you still finishing it, or did you walk away?', 'Jesus doesn''t discourage people from following him — he wants them to actually understand the cost before starting, not be surprised by it later. What would it look like to genuinely count the cost of following Jesus, rather than assuming it will be easy?',
   'Jesus, we don''t want to start something with you and walk away when it gets hard. Help us count the cost honestly, and follow anyway. Amen.', true),
  ('d5477094-8007-5c8a-8aad-db490f9aad6f', 'Luke 6:46', 'Surrender', '"Why do you call me, ''Lord, Lord,'' and don''t do the things which I say?', 'Jesus asks this as a direct, pointed question — not about people who reject him outright, but about people who use his name while not actually doing what he says.',
   'Do your actual daily choices show what you''d say you believe, or something different?', 'What''s something Jesus has clearly said that you''d call yourself a believer in, while still not actually doing it?', 'Jesus''s question isn''t about people who don''t know what he taught — it''s about people who call him "Lord" while ignoring what he actually said. Does calling Jesus "Lord" cost you anything if it doesn''t actually change what you do?',
   'Jesus, we call you Lord. Help our actual choices match that, not just our words. Amen.', true),
  ('575bc9e4-458e-51e8-b79d-347d830b2a57', 'Galatians 6:4-5', 'Ambition & Humility', 'But let each man test his own work, and then he will take pride in himself and not in his neighbor. For each man will bear his own burden.', 'Paul''s advice here is aimed squarely at the comparison instinct — measuring your own work not against someone else''s, but against what it actually is on its own.',
   'What''s something you do well that you only feel good about when you compare it to someone doing it worse?', 'What would it feel like to evaluate your own work or effort without checking it against anyone else''s first?', 'Paul says to test "your own work," not your work compared to your neighbor''s. Would you still push yourself to improve if there were no one around to compare yourself to?',
   'God, help us test our own work honestly, without needing to measure it against anyone else''s to feel okay about it. Amen.', true),
  ('02c78d8f-a5ee-5a25-aa6b-f605decee0e6', 'Psalm 42:1-3, 5', 'Grief & Loss', 'As the deer pants for the water brooks, So my soul pants after you, God. My soul thirsts for God, for the living God. When shall I come and appear before God? My tears have been my food day and night, While they continually ask me, "Where is your God?" Why are you in despair, my soul? Why are you disturbed within me? Hope in God! For I shall still praise him for the saving help of his presence.', 'The psalmist does something unusual here — he talks directly to his own soul, questioning his own despair out loud, rather than just describing how he feels or pretending it away.',
   'Have you ever had to talk yourself through a hard feeling, almost like arguing with yourself?', 'What does it look like for you, specifically, to be honest with yourself about despair instead of just performing okay-ness?', 'The psalmist doesn''t resolve his despair by ignoring it — he names it directly, asks his own soul why, and then chooses hope in the middle of the asking. What''s the difference between pretending you''re not downcast and actually talking yourself toward hope while admitting you are?',
   'God, when we''re downcast, help us do what this psalm does — name it honestly, and still choose to hope in you. Amen.', true),
  ('09d6729c-3719-5253-8714-e9a26162b604', 'Luke 18:10-14', 'Ambition & Humility', '"Two men went up into the temple to pray; one was a Pharisee, and the other was a tax collector. The Pharisee stood and prayed to himself like this: ''God, I thank you, that I am not like the rest of men, extortioners, unrighteous, adulterers, or even like this tax collector. I fast twice a week. I give tithes of all that I get.'' But the tax collector, standing far away, wouldn''t even lift up his eyes to heaven, but beat his breast, saying, ''God, be merciful to me, a sinner!'' I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other; for everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but he who humbles himself will be exalted."', 'The Pharisee''s list of good deeds is likely all true — he isn''t lying about his fasting or giving. Jesus''s point isn''t that his actions were fake, but that his prayer was really about comparing himself favorably to someone else.',
   'Have you ever caught yourself feeling good about yourself mainly because you compared yourself to someone doing worse?', 'Which prayer is closer to how you actually talk to God most often — the Pharisee''s list, or the tax collector''s plain request for mercy?', 'The Pharisee''s prayer is technically true and still condemned; the tax collector''s prayer is short and desperate and is the one Jesus praises. What does that suggest about what God actually listens for in a prayer?',
   'God, be merciful to us, sinners — not because we''ve earned it, but because that''s the only prayer that actually reaches you. Amen.', true),
  ('d836abc7-4854-5e5e-8946-24eb78044d36', 'Job 27:5', 'Integrity', 'Far be it from me that I should justify you. Until I die I will not put away my integrity from me.', 'Job says this after losing nearly everything, in the middle of his friends pressuring him to admit wrongdoing he doesn''t believe he''s guilty of — his integrity, at this point, gets him nothing back.',
   'Have you ever stuck to something true about yourself even when it would have been easier to just agree with everyone pressuring you?', 'Is there pressure on you right now to compromise something you actually believe is true about your own integrity?', 'Job holds onto his integrity with no reward in sight — he''s not being rewarded for it, he''s simply refusing to let it go. What does it mean to value integrity even when nothing you can see is riding on it anymore?',
   'God, give us Job''s stubbornness about integrity — not for a reward, but because it''s simply true. Amen.', true),
  ('7792ccd1-fcfa-561c-8adf-f44a51ec1270', 'Proverbs 14:23', 'Work', 'In all hard work there is profit, But the talk of the lips leads only to poverty.', 'A short, practical proverb contrasting two things people can substitute for each other — actually doing the work, and just talking about doing it.',
   'What''s a project or goal where you spent more time talking about it than actually doing it?', 'Where has "talk" quietly replaced real effort in your life right now?', 'The proverb doesn''t say talking is wrong — it says talk alone, without the work, leads nowhere. How do you know when your own talk has stopped being planning and started being a substitute for the work?',
   'God, help us do the hard work in front of us today, not just talk about doing it. Amen.', true),
  ('2c6caf0c-792b-5e9d-b821-f9e377c0e2ca', 'Matthew 11:2-6', 'Faith', 'Now when John heard in the prison the works of Christ, he sent two of his disciples and said to him, "Are you he who comes, or should we look for another?" Jesus answered them, "Go and tell John the things which you hear and see: the blind receive their sight, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have good news preached to them. Blessed is he who finds no occasion for stumbling in me."', 'John the Baptist is the very man who baptized Jesus and publicly identified him — and here, near the end of his life, sitting in prison, he sends people to ask Jesus directly whether he''s actually the one.',
   'Have you ever doubted something you were previously completely sure about?', 'What doubt have you been carrying quietly because you think you''re "supposed" to already have it figured out?', 'Jesus doesn''t rebuke John for asking, or treat the question as a failure of faith — he simply points John back to the actual evidence. What does it mean that even the person who introduced Jesus to the world was allowed to ask, honestly, whether he was right?',
   'Jesus, if we''re doubting something we thought we were already sure of, meet us the way you met John — with evidence, not shame. Amen.', true),
  ('e62c7a9e-91e4-5c52-966b-4c880ce1365c', 'Daniel 3:16-18', 'Faith', 'Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego answered the king, Nebuchadnezzar, we have no need to answer you in this matter. If it be so, our God whom we serve is able to deliver us from the burning fiery furnace; and he will deliver us out of your hand, O king. But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods, nor worship the golden image which you have set up.', 'These three men state their trust in God''s ability to save them, and then, in the very next breath, state that they''ll remain faithful even if he doesn''t — before knowing which outcome they''re actually going to get.',
   'Have you ever committed to doing the right thing before knowing how it was going to turn out?', 'Is there a "but if not" in your own faith — something you''d stay faithful to even if God didn''t rescue you from it?', 'These men don''t know yet whether they''ll be saved from the furnace when they say this — their trust isn''t based on already knowing the outcome. What''s the difference between trusting God because you''re confident of the outcome and trusting God even if the outcome is the one you feared?',
   'God, we want the kind of trust that holds even before we know how things turn out. Amen.', true),
  ('7fbe80a8-52d6-58a1-8ab5-6288366aa67e', 'John 15:8', 'Discipleship', '"In this is my Father glorified, that you bear much fruit; and so you will be my disciples.', 'Jesus names what actually glorifies the Father — fruit borne by his followers — and ties that fruit directly to what it means to actually be his disciple.',
   'What''s some "fruit" — a changed habit, a repaired relationship, a genuine kindness — you''ve seen grow slowly in your own life?', 'Is the fruit in your life right now something that actually points back to God, or does it mostly point back to you?', 'Jesus connects the Father''s glory directly to fruit borne by ordinary disciples, not to any dramatic achievement of his own. What would change if you measured your own spiritual life by fruit that glorifies God, rather than by personal spiritual accomplishment?',
   'Jesus, grow real fruit in us — the kind that actually points back to you, not to ourselves. Amen.', true),
  ('bfa99859-e011-5a0d-9e53-69eee4a83ab3', 'James 1:22', 'Surrender', 'But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves.', 'James calls hearing without doing a specific kind of self-deception — not a small gap, but something that actually fools the person doing it.',
   'What''s something you''ve heard taught many times but only recently actually started doing?', 'What piece of Scripture would you say you believe strongly that isn''t actually showing up in your daily choices?', 'James says hearing without doing "deludes" you — as if simply agreeing with truth can trick you into thinking you''re further along than you are. What''s the danger of mistaking agreement with a teaching for actually living it?',
   'God, we don''t want to just agree with your word. Make us doers of it, starting today. Amen.', true),
  ('76a5936d-75e2-5235-aa03-45ce410477d3', 'Luke 10:38-42', 'Peace', 'It happened as they went on their way, he entered into a certain village, and a certain woman named Martha received him into her house. She had a sister called Mary, who also sat at Jesus'' feet, and heard his word. But Martha was distracted with much serving, and she came up to him, and said, "Lord, don''t you care that my sister left me to serve alone? Ask her therefore to help me." Jesus answered her, "Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things, but one thing is needed. Mary has chosen the good part, which will not be taken away from her."', 'Martha''s frustration is genuinely understandable — she''s the one doing the work of hosting while her sister sits and listens — and Jesus doesn''t scold her for serving, only gently redirects her anxiety about it.',
   'Have you ever been so busy preparing for people that you barely got to actually be with them?', 'Do you tend to show love more through doing things for people, or through simply being present with them?', 'Jesus doesn''t tell Martha that serving is wrong — he tells her she''s "anxious and troubled," and that presence was the one thing actually needed in that moment. Could you tell, in your own hosting, whether you''re serving out of anxious duty or actually present with your guests?',
   'Jesus, help us choose the good part, like Mary did — presence with you, not just anxious activity for you. Amen.', true),
  ('17b27135-97da-59a5-b5e4-ddf86a6e522b', 'Psalm 6:6-9', 'Grief & Loss', 'I am weary with my groaning; Every night I flood my bed; I drench my couch with my tears. My eye wastes away because of grief; It grows old because of all my adversaries. Depart from me, all you workers of iniquity, For Yahweh has heard the voice of my weeping. Yahweh has heard my supplication. Yahweh accepts my prayer.', 'The psalmist describes his grief in strikingly physical terms — a bed literally soaked with tears — the kind of raw honesty rarely spoken out loud, even at church.',
   'Has grief or stress ever affected you physically — sleep, appetite, energy — more than you expected?', 'What exhaustion are you carrying right now that you''ve been describing as "fine" when it''s actually more like this psalm?', 'The psalm moves from describing genuinely physical, exhausted grief straight into confidence that God has heard him — without the circumstances changing in between. What does it mean that this confidence comes in the middle of the exhaustion, not after it''s resolved?',
   'God, you hear the weeping we don''t say out loud. Hear ours tonight, exactly as exhausted as it is. Amen.', true),
  ('fd601c46-c07a-51f6-8859-07e2867d2c48', 'Obadiah 1:3-4', 'Ambition & Humility', 'The pride of your heart has deceived you, you who dwell in the clefts of the rock, whose habitation is high, who says in his heart, ''Who will bring me down to the ground?'' Though you mount on high as the eagle, and though your nest is set among the stars, I will bring you down from there, says Yahweh.', 'This is directed at a nation that had built its identity around being literally unreachable, high in mountain fortresses — the prophet names the specific lie pride tells: that height or success makes you untouchable.',
   'What''s a time you felt completely secure about something that turned out to be more fragile than you thought?', 'Is there an area of your life where success has quietly convinced you that you''re beyond correction?', 'The passage names pride specifically as deception — not just arrogance, but something that actually distorts how safe you believe you are. What''s the difference between confidence based on something real and confidence pride has talked you into?',
   'God, keep us from believing our own height makes us untouchable. Amen.', true),
  ('137296b7-bf83-51c1-b3f4-8ea98642b058', 'James 5:7-8', 'Perseverance', 'Be patient therefore, brothers, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, being patient over it, until it receives the early and late rain. You also be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand.', 'James roots patience in an image everyone in his original audience would have understood firsthand — a farmer who can''t rush a harvest, no matter how much he wants it sooner.',
   'What''s something you''ve grown, built, or worked on that simply couldn''t be rushed, no matter how much you wanted it done sooner?', 'Is there something in your life right now you''re trying to force to happen faster than it''s actually able to?', 'A farmer''s patience isn''t passive — he''s still working, still watching, just not rushing the process itself. What''s the difference between patience that does nothing and patience that keeps working while trusting the timing?',
   'Lord, give us the farmer''s patience — still working, still trusting the timing isn''t ours to control. Amen.', true),
  ('fc1a8b2e-3fb5-55ef-9fcf-1bde1faacef2', 'Jeremiah 1:5', 'Purpose', 'Before I formed you in the belly I knew you, and before you came forth out of the womb I sanctified you; I have appointed you a prophet to the nations.', 'God says this to Jeremiah before Jeremiah has done anything at all — purpose here is described as something already assigned, not something Jeremiah earned or discovered on his own.',
   'Do you tend to think your worth comes from what you accomplish, or from something more settled than that?', 'What purpose have you been putting off pursuing because you don''t feel "formed" enough for it yet?', 'God says he knew Jeremiah before he was even formed — purpose here comes before any qualification exists. What changes about how you approach your own calling if it was never actually about being ready first?',
   'God, thank you for knowing us before we had done anything to prove ourselves. Help us live out of that instead of out of proving ourselves. Amen.', true),
  ('002acf54-be26-54c6-ba85-e9eb59f09b26', 'Psalm 73:2-3, 16-17', 'Faith', 'But as for me, my feet were almost gone. My steps had nearly slipped. For I was envious of the arrogant, When I saw the prosperity of the wicked. When I tried to understand this, It was too painful for me; Until I entered God''s sanctuary, And considered their latter end.', 'The psalmist admits his faith nearly collapsed over something specific — envy of people who seemed to be doing fine while ignoring God — and names exactly what changed his perspective, not with a new argument, but with a different vantage point.',
   'Have you ever felt resentful watching someone who seems to be doing great despite cutting corners you wouldn''t cut?', 'Is there a comparison like that eating at you right now — someone whose success bothers you more than it should?', 'The psalmist doesn''t say he figured out an answer to why the wicked prosper — he says his perspective changed once he "entered the sanctuary." What''s the difference between resolving a hard question with a better argument and resolving it by getting closer to God?',
   'God, when we''re envious of people who seem to have it all figured out without you, bring us back into your presence until our perspective changes too. Amen.', true),
  ('256fb782-9ca3-5719-8ca8-d50ce4f70ed6', '2 Kings 6:15-17', 'Faith', 'When the servant of the man of God was risen early, and gone forth, behold, a host with horses and chariots was round about the city. His servant said to him, Alas, my master! how shall we do? He answered, Don''t be afraid; for those who are with us are more than those who are with them. Elisha prayed, and said, Yahweh, Please open his eyes, that he may see. Yahweh opened the eyes of the young man; and he saw: and, behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha.', 'Elisha''s servant is terrified because he can only see the enemy army — Elisha''s confidence isn''t based on ignoring the danger, but on a reality that''s actually there and simply not yet visible to his servant.',
   'Has a situation ever looked hopeless to you until you learned something you hadn''t known was true the whole time?', 'Is there a fear right now where you feel like Elisha''s servant — only able to see the threat, not whatever else might actually be true?', 'Elisha doesn''t deny the danger is real — the enemy army is genuinely there. His confidence comes from a bigger reality that was true before his servant could see it. What does it mean that faith here isn''t about denying what''s visible, but trusting what''s real even before it''s seen?',
   'God, open our eyes the way you opened this servant''s. Help us trust what''s true even before we can see it. Amen.', true),
  ('300b3bd7-44b2-5c56-b079-bb62466b5f19', 'Matthew 28:18-20', 'Discipleship', 'Jesus came to them and spoke to them, saying, "All authority has been given to me in heaven and on earth. Therefore go, and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all things that I commanded you. Behold, I am with you always, even to the end of the age." Amen.', 'These are among the very last words Jesus speaks to his disciples before ascending — a clear assignment, paired with a promise of his presence for the entire length of it.',
   'What''s a responsibility someone gave you, along with a promise to help you through it?', 'Does the idea of "making disciples" feel like something reserved for pastors and missionaries, or something that actually includes you?', 'Jesus pairs the assignment ("go, make disciples") with a promise ("I am with you always") in the very same breath. What changes about how you approach this calling if the promise of his presence is guaranteed no matter how big the assignment feels?',
   'Jesus, you promised to be with us to the end. Help us actually live out this assignment, trusting that promise instead of our own capability. Amen.', true),
  ('8a943d63-7f00-50d5-8461-3217c681772c', 'Genesis 6:22', 'Surrender', 'Thus Noah did. According to all that God commanded him, so he did.', 'This one verse covers what was, in the actual story, a massive, strange, decades-long project — building an ark for a flood no one else believed was coming — summarized simply as obedience, done.',
   'What''s the longest you''ve ever worked on something before seeing any actual result?', 'What have you been asked to do that makes no visible sense yet, that you''ve been putting off because of that?', 'This verse doesn''t record Noah''s doubts, his neighbors'' mockery, or how strange the assignment must have felt — it just states that he did all of it. What does it mean that obedience can be this simple to summarize, even when the actual experience of it was almost certainly much harder?',
   'God, give us Noah''s kind of obedience — the steady kind that keeps building even before there''s any visible reason to. Amen.', true),
  ('fdd9d5fc-3ab7-58b1-935d-7c6582f9e2d1', '1 Corinthians 12:14, 18, 26', 'Community', 'For the body is not one member, but many. But now God has set the members, each one of them, in the body, just as he desired. When one member suffers, all the members suffer with it. Or when one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it.', 'Paul is describing the church using the image of a human body, where every part is genuinely connected to every other part — not a loose metaphor, but a claim that what happens to one person actually affects the whole group.',
   'Has someone else''s hard time or good news at this table ever genuinely affected how your own day went?', 'Who in your community''s suffering have you kept at arm''s length instead of actually feeling connected to?', 'Paul says the body doesn''t get to choose which parts it''s connected to — a hand can''t opt out of feeling what happens to the foot. What would change about how you show up for people if you actually believed their suffering and joy were as connected to you as this passage claims?',
   'God, we''re more connected to each other than we act like. Help us actually feel what this table feels, not just observe it. Amen.', true)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

commit;

